cts/05-message-edit-cascade/streams/004-verify.stdout.log:18:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/02-derivation-pipeline/artifacts/05-message-edit-cascade/003-implementor.json | sed -n '1,500p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"     1\\t{\\\"command\\\":\\\"story-implement\\\",\\\"version\\\":1,\\\"status\\\":\\\"ok\\\",\\\"outcome\\\":\\\"ready-for-verification\\\",\\\"result\\\":{\\\"resultId\\\":\\\"8aa56afe-03ec-4da6-a2e8-2d6f10d67bd3\\\",\\\"provider\\\":\\\"claude-code\\\",\\\"model\\\":\\\"claude-fable-5\\\",\\\"role\\\":\\\"story_implementor\\\",\\\"sessionId\\\":\\\"11cfbce6-8c6f-47e4-8ae9-6d1d829e88b4\\\",\\\"continuation\\\":{\\\"provider\\\":\\\"claude-code\\\",\\\"sessionId\\\":\\\"11cfbce6-8c6f-47e4-8ae9-6d1d829e88b4\\\",\\\"storyId\\\":\\\"05-message-edit-cascade\\\"},\\\"outcome\\\":\\\"ready-for-verification\\\",\\\"story\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"05-message-edit-cascade\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"Story 5: Message Edit and Cascade\\\"},\\\"planSummary\\\":\\\"Implemented Story 5 (message edit and cascade): a new shared cascade module (src/domains/messages/internal/cascade.ts) derives the clear-set structurally from the message → turn → chunk walk over existing derived_form rows, supersede-deletes still-queued old-version items (reported on the result), and enqueues replacements at each group's max source version + 1 inside the mutation's transaction. messages.edit validates through the deleted-filtered read (turn_open / message_not_found refusals), applies content + blocks + token re-stamp via new store.ts helpers (readMutableMessage / applyMessageEdit mirroring projectEvent's counted fields), runs the cascade, and returns the MutationResult — one synchronous commit, with enqueue pokes riding onCommit so background mode rebuilds with no further call. The source-version check (existing single write path in work-queue complete()) is provoked by TC-5.4's delayed-double recipe: a real claimed pre-edit item held across the edit completes after the post-edit replacement is queued and discards as stale_discarded. CLI: new src/cli/messages-mutate.ts and `lhc messages edit --message/--message-id --content` wiring with parity proven through the spawned binary.\\\",\\\"changedFiles\\\":[{\\\"path\\\":\\\"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/internal/cascade.ts\\\",\\\"reason\\\":\\\"NEW: walk-and-clear cascade module (DD-8) — structural chain enumeration, supersede-delete of queued old-version items, replacement enqueues at next source version\\\"},{\\\"path\\\":\\\"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/internal/store.ts\\\",\\\"reason\\\":\\\"edit's validation read (readMutableMessage, deleted-filtered, joined turn status) and record apply (applyMessageEdit: block content update + token re-stamp via the one estimator)\\\"},{\\\"path\\\":\\\"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/index.ts\\\",\\\"reason\\\":\\\"messages.edit operation (one runInTransaction: validate → apply → cascade) and the MutationResult interface\\\"},{\\\"path\\\":\\\"packages/lhc/src/cli/messages-mutate.ts\\\",\\\"reason\\\":\\\"NEW per §Placement: runMessagesEdit mirroring the SDK operation one-for-one\\\"},{\\\"path\\\":\\\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\\\",\\\"reason\\\":\\\"`lhc messages edit` command wiring, --message/--message-id and --content flags, HELP line\\\"},{\\\"path\\\":\\\"packages/lhc/src/sdk.ts\\\",\\\"reason\\\":\\\"export MutationResult type on the public surface\\\"},{\\\"path\\\":\\\"packages/lhc/vitest.config.ts\\\",\\\"reason\\\":\\\"exclude the new cli-process-mutations suite outside LHC_PROCESS_SUITE=1, matching the other process suites\\\"},{\\\"path\\\":\\\"packages/lhc/test/mutations.test.ts\\\",\\\"reason\\\":\\\"NEW: TC-5.1 through TC-5.5 plus the induced-failure atomicity leg and a background-mode edit-and-walk-away production-path test\\\"},{\\\"path\\\":\\\"packages/lhc/test/cli-process-mutations.test.ts\\\",\\\"reason\\\":\\\"NEW: TC-5.6 SDK/CLI twin parity (result JSON, cascade rows, read-back) plus CLI refusal parity\\\"},{\\\"path\\\":\\\"packages/lhc/test/red-manifest.json\\\",\\\"reason\\\":\\\"recorded the two new test files' hashes (green may add files; the record step locks them)\\\"}],\\\"tests\\\":{\\\"added\\\":[\\\"test/mutations.test.ts — TC-5.1: synchronous content/blocks/estimate update, cleared + queued named on the result, rebuild derives from edited content\\\",\\\"test/mutations.test.ts — TC-5.1 atomicity (architecture risk): induced cascade failure (planted claimed row at the replacement id) rolls back the content change too; full snapshot byte-stable\\\",\\\"test/mutations.test.ts — TC-5.2 (architecture risk): two-chunk thread, cleared set exactly 5 forms (message + turn's 2 + chunk 1's 2); every form outside the chain deep-equal pre-edit including source version\\\",\\\"test/mutations.test.ts — TC-5.3: post-return all dependents pending, replacement ids carry the new source version; second edit supersedes the still-queued first wave, rows deleted, ids reported\\\",\\\"test/mutations.test.ts — TC-5.4 (epic's named architecture-risk test): delayed double holds a claimed pre-edit smoothing across the edit; old and replacement items coexist (version-scoped ids); drain report shows stale_discarded then done; exactly one ready row from post-edit content\\\",\\\"test/mutations.test.ts — TC-5.5: turn_open and message_not_found refusals plus deleted-target-reads-as-message_not_found; snapshot (messages + forms + queue) identical after each\\\",\\\"test/mutations.test.ts — background-mode edit-and-walk-away: cascade pokes ride the commit; rebuilds run with no further call (production path proof)\\\",\\\"test/cli-process-mutations.test.ts — TC-5.6: same edit via SDK and spawned CLI on twin fixtures: identical result JSON, identical queue rows (minus wall-clock queuedAt), identical message and form read-back\\\",\\\"test/cli-process-mutations.test.ts — TC-5.6 refusal parity: CLI exits 1 with the same stable message_not_found code; record unchanged\\\"],\\\"modified\\\":[],\\\"removed\\\":[],\\\"totalAfterStory\\\":217,\\\"deltaFromPriorBaseline\\\":9},\\\"gatesRun\\\":[{\\\"command\\\":\\\"pnpm run green-verify\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"pass\\\"},{\\\"command\\\":\\\"pnpm run verify-all\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"pass\\\"},{\\\"command\\\":\\\"pnpm vitest run test/mutations.test.ts\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"pass\\\"},{\\\"command\\\":\\\"LHC_PROCESS_SUITE=1 pnpm vitest run test/cli-process-mutations.test.ts\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"pass\\\"}],\\\"selfReview\\\":{\\\"passesRun\\\":0,\\\"findingsFixed\\\":[\\\"Atomicity fault injection initially targeted a queued conflicting row, which the cascade's supersede step would have deleted before the enqueue; switched to a claimed row so the replacement insert genuinely collides mid-cascade and forces the rollback path\\\",\\\"Cascade source-version bump computed per rebuild group as max(existing versions) + 1 rather than per-form +1, so the two turn forms (one shared turn_derivation item) can never enqueue at diverged versions\\\"],\\\"findingsSurfaced\\\":[\\\"TC-5.4 timing: the edit must land inside the delayed double's 400ms provider window after the poll observes the handler start; edit completes in single-digit milliseconds so the margin is wide, but an extremely loaded CI machine could in principle let the v1 completion land first and fail the mid-flight coexistence assertion\\\",\\\"messages.listMessages and turns reads do not yet filter deleted_at (Story 6 scope); TC-5.5's deleted-target leg stamps deleted_at below the SDK and only asserts the edit refusal reads through the filtered validation view\\\"]},\\\"openQuestions\\\":[],\\\"specDeviations\\\":[\\\"TC-5.6's CLI parity legs live in a new test/cli-process-mutations.test.ts instead of test/cli-process-work.test.ts named by the story's test mapping: that file is hash-locked by the Story 1 red manifest (green may add files, not edit) — same precedent Story 4 set with cli-process-report-repair.test.ts\\\",\\\"TC-5.4 holds the claimed pre-edit item via an unawaited manual-mode work.drain promise rather than background mode + drainSettled, so the drain report's stale_discarded disposition is directly assertable (the test plan's storage-contract note requires that assertion); the claimed-item-held-across-the-edit overlap is identical, and background mode is separately proven by the edit-and-walk-away test\\\",\\\"CLI accepts both --message (the story's and tech design's spelling) and --message-id (the house convention used by report/requeue); both parse into the same input\\\",\\\"For tool_call blocks the edit's content string lands verbatim as the new arguments value (estimate re-stamped from its serialization, mirroring projection); the design's edit signature is content: string for all kinds and only text-kind edits are TC-covered — noted for the Story 6/verifier record\\\"],\\\"recommendedNextStep\\\":\\\"Proceed to story verification; then Story 6 (delete) can reuse cascadeFromMessage's walk with the dropped-forms close path\\\"},\\\"errors\\\":[],\\\"warnings\\\":[],\\\"artifacts\\\":[{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"result-envelope\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/02-derivation-pipeline/artifacts/05-message-edit-cascade/003-implementor.json\\\"}],\\\"startedAt\\\":\\\"2026-06-11T05:57:21.029Z\\\",\\\"finishedAt\\\":\\\"2026-06-11T06:14:31.156Z\\\"}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/artifacts/05-message-edit-cascade/streams/004-verify.stdout.log:41:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_19\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba packages/lhc/src/tech-utils/work-queue/index.ts | sed -n '1,360p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"     1\\t// Durable work-item mechanics: recording, ordered listing, and the enqueue\\n     2\\t// wrapper that makes \\\"queueing is what schedules processing\\\" structural\\n     3\\t// (DD-5). The util is domain-blind by the tech-arch capability rule — it\\n     4\\t// knows the item vocabulary (owner, kind, sourceRef) but nothing about what\\n     5\\t// a turn or a summary is; which kinds queue for which sources, and which\\n     6\\t// derived forms a kind produces, is the owning domains' business (callers\\n     7\\t// pass the form targets in). Writes run on the caller's handle inside the\\n     8\\t// ambient transaction, so items commit (or roll back) with the batch. No\\n     9\\t// public SDK surface: read-back reaches here only through the owning\\n    10\\t// domains' listQueuedWork.\\n    11\\timport type { DatabaseSync } from \\\"node:sqlite\\\";\\n    12\\timport {\\n    13\\t  createCommitHooks,\\n    14\\t  fireSchedulerPoke,\\n    15\\t  type OperationContext,\\n    16\\t} from \\\"../../shared/context.js\\\";\\n    17\\timport type {\\n    18\\t  CompletionTx,\\n    19\\t  FormKind,\\n    20\\t  HandlerFormWrite,\\n    21\\t  SubjectKind,\\n    22\\t} from \\\"../../shared/derivation.js\\\";\\n    23\\t\\n    24\\texport type WorkOwner = \\\"messages\\\" | \\\"turns\\\";\\n    25\\texport type WorkKind =\\n    26\\t  | \\\"prompt_smoothing\\\"\\n    27\\t  | \\\"tool_call_summary\\\"\\n    28\\t  | \\\"tool_result_summary\\\"\\n    29\\t  | \\\"turn_derivation\\\"\\n    30\\t  | \\\"chunk_summary_detailed\\\"\\n    31\\t  | \\\"chunk_summary_brief\\\";\\n    32\\texport type WorkSourceRef = { messageId: string } | { turnId: string } | { chunkId: string };\\n    33\\t\\n    34\\t// The work-kind registry: owner and sourceRef semantics per the epic's Work\\n    35\\t// Item contract. Mechanical metadata only — what a kind *means* stays with\\n    36\\t// the owning domain's handler (registered at SDK construction, DD-6).\\n    37\\texport const WORK_KIND_REGISTRY: Readonly<\\n    38\\t  Record<WorkKind, { owner: WorkOwner; sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" | \\\"turnId\\\" | \\\"chunkId\\\" }>\\n    39\\t> = {\\n    40\\t  prompt_smoothing: { owner: \\\"messages\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" },\\n    41\\t  tool_call_summary: { owner: \\\"messages\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" },\\n    42\\t  tool_result_summary: { owner: \\\"messages\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" },\\n    43\\t  turn_derivation: { owner: \\\"turns\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"turnId\\\" },\\n    44\\t  chunk_summary_detailed: { owner: \\\"turns\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"chunkId\\\" },\\n    45\\t  chunk_summary_brief: { owner: \\\"turns\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"chunkId\\\" },\\n    46\\t};\\n    47\\t\\n    48\\texport interface WorkItemRecord {\\n    49\\t  workItemId: string;\\n    50\\t  owner: WorkOwner;\\n    51\\t  kind: WorkKind;\\n    52\\t  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n    53\\t  status: \\\"queued\\\"; // the only status written before a drain claims (Epic 02 Story 1)\\n    54\\t  queuedAt: string;\\n    55\\t}\\n    56\\t\\n    57\\t// The derived forms an enqueue is scheduling work toward; the owning domain\\n    58\\t// names them (the util stays meaning-blind). Stored in the row's payload so\\n    59\\t// the drain's terminal paths (retry exhaustion, blocked source) can land the\\n    60\\t// form's failed/blocked state without asking any domain what a kind means.\\n    61\\texport interface EnqueueFormTarget {\\n    62\\t  subjectKind: SubjectKind;\\n    63\\t  subjectId: string;\\n    64\\t  form: FormKind;\\n    65\\t}\\n    66\\t\\n    67\\texport interface WorkItemInput {\\n    68\\t  owner: WorkOwner;\\n    69\\t  kind: WorkKind;\\n    70\\t  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n    71\\t  sourceVersion?: number; // defaults to 1 — first version of a fresh source\\n    72\\t  forms?: readonly EnqueueFormTarget[]; // carried in payload for the drain's terminal paths\\n    73\\t}\\n    74\\t\\n    75\\tfunction sourceIdOf(sourceRef: WorkSourceRef): string {\\n    76\\t  if (\\\"messageId\\\" in sourceRef) return sourceRef.messageId;\\n    77\\t  if (\\\"turnId\\\" in sourceRef) return sourceRef.turnId;\\n    78\\t  return sourceRef.chunkId;\\n    79\\t}\\n    80\\t\\n    81\\t// Deterministic id scoped to the source version (DD-1/DD-3): re-queueing the\\n    82\\t// same kind for the same source *at the same version* is the same id —\\n    83\\t// natural idempotency for the repair path — while a post-mutation\\n    84\\t// replacement (next version) never collides with an in-flight pre-mutation\\n    85\\t// item.\\n    86\\texport function recordItem(\\n    87\\t  db: DatabaseSync,\\n    88\\t  input: WorkItemInput,\\n    89\\t  queuedAt: string,\\n    90\\t): WorkItemRecord {\\n    91\\t  const sourceVersion = input.sourceVersion ?? 1;\\n    92\\t  const workItemId = `w-${sourceIdOf(input.sourceRef)}-${input.kind}-v${sourceVersion}`;\\n    93\\t  db.prepare(\\n    94\\t    `INSERT INTO work_item (work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, status, queued_at, payload)\\n    95\\t     VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, 'queued', ?, ?)`,\\n    96\\t  ).run(\\n    97\\t    workItemId,\\n    98\\t    input.owner,\\n    99\\t    input.kind,\\n   100\\t    JSON.stringify(input.sourceRef),\\n   101\\t    queuedAt,\\n   102\\t    JSON.stringify({ sourceVersion, forms: input.forms ?? [] }),\\n   103\\t  );\\n   104\\t  return {\\n   105\\t    workItemId,\\n   106\\t    owner: input.owner,\\n   107\\t    kind: input.kind,\\n   108\\t    sourceRef: input.sourceRef,\\n   109\\t    status: \\\"queued\\\",\\n   110\\t    queuedAt,\\n   111\\t  };\\n   112\\t}\\n   113\\t\\n   114\\texport interface EnqueueInput extends WorkItemInput {\\n   115\\t  forms: readonly EnqueueFormTarget[];\\n   116\\t}\\n   117\\t\\n   118\\t// The one way work is scheduled (DD-5): the work row, the form's `pending`\\n   119\\t// state row, and the scheduler poke all ride the ambient transaction — they\\n   120\\t// commit together or vanish together. Enqueue is the *only* place a\\n   121\\t// derived_form row is created (completion is UPDATE-only); re-enqueueing\\n   122\\t// resets an existing row to pending at the enqueued source version.\\n   123\\texport function enqueue(ctx: OperationContext, input: EnqueueInput): WorkItemRecord {\\n   124\\t  const item = recordItem(ctx.db, input, ctx.clock().toISOString());\\n   125\\t  const sourceVersion = input.sourceVersion ?? 1;\\n   126\\t  const upsert = ctx.db.prepare(\\n   127\\t    `INSERT INTO derived_form (subject_kind, subject_id, form, state, source_version)\\n   128\\t     VALUES (?, ?, ?, 'pending', ?)\\n   129\\t     ON CONFLICT (subject_kind, subject_id, form) DO UPDATE SET\\n   130\\t       state = 'pending', content = NULL, reason = NULL, metadata = NULL,\\n   131\\t       gaps = NULL, derived_at = NULL, source_version = excluded.source_version`,\\n   132\\t  );\\n   133\\t  for (const target of input.forms) {\\n   134\\t    upsert.run(target.subjectKind, target.subjectId, target.form, sourceVersion);\\n   135\\t  }\\n   136\\t  ctx.onCommit(() => fireSchedulerPoke(ctx.threadId));\\n   137\\t  return item;\\n   138\\t}\\n   139\\t\\n   140\\tinterface RawWorkItemRow {\\n   141\\t  work_item_id: string;\\n   142\\t  owner: string;\\n   143\\t  kind: string;\\n   144\\t  source_ref: string;\\n   145\\t  queued_at: string;\\n   146\\t}\\n   147\\t\\n   148\\t// ── claim mechanics (Epic 02 Story 1, tech design §Mechanics) ─────\\n   149\\t// All pure SQL against the caller's open handle, domain-blind. Claim,\\n   150\\t// complete, and the terminal half of failAttempt each own one short\\n   151\\t// BEGIN IMMEDIATE; the handler in between runs with no open transaction.\\n   152\\t\\n   153\\t// The claimed row as the drain holds it: mechanical fields plus the payload's\\n   154\\t// source version and form targets. `kind` is a plain string here — a raw row\\n   155\\t// with an unregistered kind must still be claimable so the drain can land it\\n   156\\t// failed_terminal instead of skipping it (AC-1.8).\\n   157\\texport interface ClaimedWorkItem {\\n   158\\t  workItemId: string;\\n   159\\t  owner: WorkOwner;\\n   160\\t  kind: string;\\n   161\\t  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n   162\\t  attempts: number;\\n   163\\t  queuedAt: string;\\n   164\\t  sourceVersion: number;\\n   165\\t  forms: EnqueueFormTarget[];\\n   166\\t}\\n   167\\t\\n   168\\texport type ClaimOutcome =\\n   169\\t  | { outcome: \\\"claimed\\\"; item: ClaimedWorkItem }\\n   170\\t  | { outcome: \\\"empty\\\" }\\n   171\\t  | { outcome: \\\"in_flight\\\"; claimExpiresAt: string }\\n   172\\t  | { outcome: \\\"waiting\\\"; waitingUntil: string };\\n   173\\t\\n   174\\tinterface RawClaimRow {\\n   175\\t  work_item_id: string;\\n   176\\t  owner: string;\\n   177\\t  kind: string;\\n   178\\t  source_ref: string;\\n   179\\t  attempts: number | bigint;\\n   180\\t  queued_at: string;\\n   181\\t  payload: string | null;\\n   182\\t}\\n   183\\t\\n   184\\t// Form targets for pre-v5 rows, whose payload is NULL (Epic 01 wrote no\\n   185\\t// payload column). Exactly the F-02 backfill's kind→form mapping (migration\\n   186\\t// v5, shared/storage.ts): the backfill created pending derived_form rows at\\n   187\\t// source version 1 for these kinds, and a terminal failure must find them —\\n   188\\t// otherwise exhaustion would delete the work row and strand the backfilled\\n   189\\t// form as pending forever, breaking AC-1.9/DD-1 for upgraded threads.\\n   190\\tconst LEGACY_KIND_FORMS: Partial<\\n   191\\t  Record<string, ReadonlyArray<{ subjectKind: SubjectKind; form: FormKind }>>\\n   192\\t> = {\\n   193\\t  prompt_smoothing: [{ subjectKind: \\\"message\\\", form: \\\"smoothed_prompt\\\" }],\\n   194\\t  tool_result_summary: [{ subjectKind: \\\"message\\\", form: \\\"tool_result_summary\\\" }],\\n   195\\t  turn_derivation: [\\n   196\\t    { subjectKind: \\\"turn\\\", form: \\\"turn_rendering\\\" },\\n   197\\t    { subjectKind: \\\"turn\\\", form: \\\"lower_band_projection\\\" },\\n   198\\t  ],\\n   199\\t};\\n   200\\t\\n   201\\tfunction legacyFormTargets(kind: string, sourceRef: WorkSourceRef): EnqueueFormTarget[] {\\n   202\\t  const mapped = LEGACY_KIND_FORMS[kind];\\n   203\\t  if (mapped === undefined) return [];\\n   204\\t  const subjectId = sourceIdOf(sourceRef);\\n   205\\t  return mapped.map((target) => ({ ...target, subjectId }));\\n   206\\t}\\n   207\\t\\n   208\\tfunction toClaimedItem(row: RawClaimRow): ClaimedWorkItem {\\n   209\\t  const sourceRef = JSON.parse(row.source_ref) as WorkSourceRef;\\n   210\\t  const payload =\\n   211\\t    row.payload === null\\n   212\\t      ? // Pre-v5 row: version 1 (matching the backfill) and form targets\\n   213\\t        // reconstructed from the backfill's own mapping.\\n   214\\t        { sourceVersion: 1, forms: legacyFormTargets(row.kind, sourceRef) }\\n   215\\t      : (JSON.parse(row.payload) as { sourceVersion?: number; forms?: EnqueueFormTarget[] });\\n   216\\t  return {\\n   217\\t    workItemId: row.work_item_id,\\n   218\\t    owner: row.owner as WorkOwner,\\n   219\\t    kind: row.kind,\\n   220\\t    sourceRef,\\n   221\\t    attempts: Number(row.attempts),\\n   222\\t    queuedAt: row.queued_at,\\n   223\\t    sourceVersion: payload.sourceVersion ?? 1,\\n   224\\t    forms: payload.forms ?? [],\\n   225\\t  };\\n   226\\t}\\n   227\\t\\n   228\\t// Head-first, never skip-ahead: the claim decision is made against the oldest\\n   229\\t// live row only — a later eligible row is never considered while an older\\n   230\\t// live row exists. One atomic UPDATE under BEGIN IMMEDIATE (no read-then-\\n   231\\t// write split: the race window is the bug class). The CASE increments\\n   232\\t// `attempts` only when reclaiming an expired claimed row — a reclaim means a\\n   233\\t// run died or hung, and the increment makes the crash visible in the report\\n   234\\t// and counts it against the retry budget; normal claims never touch it.\\n   235\\texport function claimNext(db: DatabaseSync, now: string, leaseDurationMs: number): ClaimOutcome {\\n   236\\t  const expiresAt = new Date(Date.parse(now) + leaseDurationMs).toISOString();\\n   237\\t  db.exec(\\\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\\\");\\n   238\\t  try {\\n   239\\t    const claimed = db\\n   240\\t      .prepare(\\n   241\\t        `UPDATE work_item SET status = 'claimed', claimed_at = ?, claim_expires_at = ?,\\n   242\\t           attempts = attempts + CASE WHEN status = 'claimed' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END\\n   243\\t         WHERE work_item_id = (\\n   244\\t           SELECT work_item_id FROM (\\n   245\\t             SELECT work_item_id, status, eligible_at, claim_expires_at\\n   246\\t             FROM work_item WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed')\\n   247\\t             ORDER BY rowid LIMIT 1\\n   248\\t           )\\n   249\\t           WHERE (status = 'queued' AND (eligible_at IS NULL OR eligible_at <= ?))\\n   250\\t              OR (status = 'claimed' AND claim_expires_at <= ?)\\n   251\\t         )\\n   252\\t         RETURNING work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, attempts, queued_at, payload`,\\n   253\\t      )\\n   254\\t      .get(now, expiresAt, now, now) as unknown as RawClaimRow | undefined;\\n   255\\t    if (claimed !== undefined) {\\n   256\\t      db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n   257\\t      return { outcome: \\\"claimed\\\", item: toClaimedItem(claimed) };\\n   258\\t    }\\n   259\\t    // No row claimable: re-read the head inside the same transaction to\\n   260\\t    // classify the stop. A claimed head with a live lease gates the queue\\n   261\\t    // (in_flight, AC-1.4); a queued head still backing off gates it too\\n   262\\t    // (waiting) — strict ordering, never reordered around the head.\\n   263\\t    const head = db\\n   264\\t      .prepare(\\n   265\\t        `SELECT status, eligible_at, claim_expires_at\\n   266\\t         FROM work_item WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed')\\n   267\\t         ORDER BY rowid LIMIT 1`,\\n   268\\t      )\\n   269\\t      .get() as unknown as\\n   270\\t      | { status: string; eligible_at: string | null; claim_expires_at: string | null }\\n   271\\t      | undefined;\\n   272\\t    db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n   273\\t    if (head === undefined) return { outcome: \\\"empty\\\" };\\n   274\\t    if (head.status === \\\"claimed\\\") {\\n   275\\t      return { outcome: \\\"in_flight\\\", claimExpiresAt: head.claim_expires_at ?? \\\"\\\" };\\n   276\\t    }\\n   277\\t    return { outcome: \\\"waiting\\\", waitingUntil: head.eligible_at ?? \\\"\\\" };\\n   278\\t  } catch (cause) {\\n   279\\t    db.exec(\\\"ROLLBACK;\\\");\\n   280\\t    throw cause;\\n   281\\t  }\\n   282\\t}\\n   283\\t\\n   284\\t// Completion: the version-checked form writes and the item row's deletion in\\n   285\\t// one short transaction (DD-1: terminal transition deletes the row; the\\n   286\\t// disposition is reported in-memory, never stored). UPDATE-only, never\\n   287\\t// upsert — the pending row exists from enqueue; a write that finds no row at\\n   288\\t// the item's source version is discarded as stale (DD-3 truth table). The\\n   289\\t// optional onApplied hook (Story 3) runs inside this same transaction, after\\n   290\\t// the form writes and only when they landed — chunk placement and the\\n   291\\t// close→summary enqueues ride the completion commit; its onCommit\\n   292\\t// registrations (the enqueue pokes) flush after COMMIT and drop on rollback.\\n   293\\texport function complete(\\n   294\\t  db: DatabaseSync,\\n   295\\t  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\\n   296\\t  writes: readonly HandlerFormWrite[],\\n   297\\t  derivedAt: string,\\n   298\\t  onApplied?: (tx: CompletionTx) => void,\\n   299\\t): \\\"done\\\" | \\\"stale_discarded\\\" {\\n   300\\t  const hooks = createCommitHooks();\\n   301\\t  db.exec(\\\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\\\");\\n   302\\t  try {\\n   303\\t    let hits = 0;\\n   304\\t    const update = db.prepare(\\n   305\\t      `UPDATE derived_form\\n   306\\t       SET state = 'ready', content = ?, reason = NULL, metadata = ?, gaps = ?, derived_at = ?\\n   307\\t       WHERE subject_kind = ? AND subject_id = ? AND form = ? AND source_version = ?`,\\n   308\\t    );\\n   309\\t    for (const write of writes) {\\n   310\\t      const changed = update.run(\\n   311\\t        write.content,\\n   312\\t        write.metadata === undefined ? null : JSON.stringify(write.metadata),\\n   313\\t        write.gaps === undefined ? null : JSON.stringify(write.gaps),\\n   314\\t        derivedAt,\\n   315\\t        write.subjectKind,\\n   316\\t        write.subjectId,\\n   317\\t        write.form,\\n   318\\t        item.sourceVersion,\\n   319\\t      );\\n   320\\t      hits += Number(changed.changes);\\n   321\\t    }\\n   322\\t    const stale = writes.length > 0 && hits === 0;\\n   323\\t    if (!stale && onApplied !== undefined) {\\n   324\\t      onApplied({ db, onCommit: hooks.register });\\n   325\\t    }\\n   326\\t    db.prepare(`DELETE FROM work_item WHERE work_item_id = ?`).run(item.workItemId);\\n   327\\t    db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n   328\\t    hooks.flush();\\n   329\\t    return stale ? \\\"stale_discarded\\\" : \\\"done\\\";\\n   330\\t  } catch (cause) {\\n   331\\t    db.exec(\\\"ROLLBACK;\\\");\\n   332\\t    throw cause;\\n   333\\t  }\\n   334\\t}\\n   335\\t\\n   336\\texport interface RetryPolicy {\\n   337\\t  budget: number;\\n   338\\t  backoffBaseMs: number;\\n   339\\t  backoffCapMs: number;\\n   340\\t}\\n   341\\t\\n   342\\texport type FailAttemptResult =\\n   343\\t  | { terminal: false; attempts: number; eligibleAt: string }\\n   344\\t  | { terminal: true; attempts: number };\\n   345\\t\\n   346\\t// Terminal failure: the form rows land `failed` (or `blocked` for source\\n   347\\t// damage) carrying the final reason plus attempts/last-error copied onto the\\n   348\\t// form's metadata, and the item row is deleted — one transaction (DD-1).\\n   349\\t// Form writes stay version-checked: if a mutation moved the source since this\\n   350\\t// item was queued, the stale failure must not stamp the rebuilt form.\\n   351\\texport function failTerminal(\\n   352\\t  db: DatabaseSync,\\n   353\\t  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\\n   354\\t  opts: { reason: string; formState: \\\"failed\\\" | \\\"blocked\\\"; attempts: number; now: string },\\n   355\\t): void {\\n   356\\t  db.exec(\\\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\\\");\\n   357\\t  try {\\n   358\\t    const update = db.prepare(\\n   359\\t      `UPDATE derived_form\\n   360\\t       SET state = ?, content = NULL, reason = ?, metadata = ?, gaps = NULL, derived_at = ?\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/artifacts/fix/streams/001-fix-result.stdout.log:144:{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_018DAUGe39pMXLnrwXNZ9WhK\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"1\\t// Durable work-item mechanics: recording, ordered listing, and the enqueue\\n2\\t// wrapper that makes \\\"queueing is what schedules processing\\\" structural\\n3\\t// (DD-5). The util is domain-blind by the tech-arch capability rule — it\\n4\\t// knows the item vocabulary (owner, kind, sourceRef) but nothing about what\\n5\\t// a turn or a summary is; which kinds queue for which sources, and which\\n6\\t// derived forms a kind produces, is the owning domains' business (callers\\n7\\t// pass the form targets in). Writes run on the caller's handle inside the\\n8\\t// ambient transaction, so items commit (or roll back) with the batch. No\\n9\\t// public SDK surface: read-back reaches here only through the owning\\n10\\t// domains' listQueuedWork.\\n11\\timport type { DatabaseSync } from \\\"node:sqlite\\\";\\n12\\timport {\\n13\\t  createCommitHooks,\\n14\\t  fireSchedulerPoke,\\n15\\t  type OperationContext,\\n16\\t} from \\\"../../shared/context.js\\\";\\n17\\timport type {\\n18\\t  CompletionTx,\\n19\\t  FormKind,\\n20\\t  HandlerFormWrite,\\n21\\t  SubjectKind,\\n22\\t} from \\\"../../shared/derivation.js\\\";\\n23\\t\\n24\\texport type WorkOwner = \\\"messages\\\" | \\\"turns\\\";\\n25\\texport type WorkKind =\\n26\\t  | \\\"prompt_smoothing\\\"\\n27\\t  | \\\"tool_call_summary\\\"\\n28\\t  | \\\"tool_result_summary\\\"\\n29\\t  | \\\"turn_derivation\\\"\\n30\\t  | \\\"chunk_summary_detailed\\\"\\n31\\t  | \\\"chunk_summary_brief\\\";\\n32\\texport type WorkSourceRef = { messageId: string } | { turnId: string } | { chunkId: string };\\n33\\t\\n34\\t// The work-kind registry: owner and sourceRef semantics per the epic's Work\\n35\\t// Item contract. Mechanical metadata only — what a kind *means* stays with\\n36\\t// the owning domain's handler (registered at SDK construction, DD-6).\\n37\\texport const WORK_KIND_REGISTRY: Readonly<\\n38\\t  Record<WorkKind, { owner: WorkOwner; sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" | \\\"turnId\\\" | \\\"chunkId\\\" }>\\n39\\t> = {\\n40\\t  prompt_smoothing: { owner: \\\"messages\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" },\\n41\\t  tool_call_summary: { owner: \\\"messages\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" },\\n42\\t  tool_result_summary: { owner: \\\"messages\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" },\\n43\\t  turn_derivation: { owner: \\\"turns\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"turnId\\\" },\\n44\\t  chunk_summary_detailed: { owner: \\\"turns\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"chunkId\\\" },\\n45\\t  chunk_summary_brief: { owner: \\\"turns\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"chunkId\\\" },\\n46\\t};\\n47\\t\\n48\\texport interface WorkItemRecord {\\n49\\t  workItemId: string;\\n50\\t  owner: WorkOwner;\\n51\\t  kind: WorkKind;\\n52\\t  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n53\\t  status: \\\"queued\\\"; // the only status written before a drain claims (Epic 02 Story 1)\\n54\\t  queuedAt: string;\\n55\\t}\\n56\\t\\n57\\t// The derived forms an enqueue is scheduling work toward; the owning domain\\n58\\t// names them (the util stays meaning-blind). Stored in the row's payload so\\n59\\t// the drain's terminal paths (retry exhaustion, blocked source) can land the\\n60\\t// form's failed/blocked state without asking any domain what a kind means.\\n61\\texport interface EnqueueFormTarget {\\n62\\t  subjectKind: SubjectKind;\\n63\\t  subjectId: string;\\n64\\t  form: FormKind;\\n65\\t}\\n66\\t\\n67\\texport interface WorkItemInput {\\n68\\t  owner: WorkOwner;\\n69\\t  kind: WorkKind;\\n70\\t  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n71\\t  sourceVersion?: number; // defaults to 1 — first version of a fresh source\\n72\\t  forms?: readonly EnqueueFormTarget[]; // carried in payload for the drain's terminal paths\\n73\\t}\\n74\\t\\n75\\tfunction sourceIdOf(sourceRef: WorkSourceRef): string {\\n76\\t  if (\\\"messageId\\\" in sourceRef) return sourceRef.messageId;\\n77\\t  if (\\\"turnId\\\" in sourceRef) return sourceRef.turnId;\\n78\\t  return sourceRef.chunkId;\\n79\\t}\\n80\\t\\n81\\t// Deterministic id scoped to the source version (DD-1/DD-3): re-queueing the\\n82\\t// same kind for the same source *at the same version* is the same id —\\n83\\t// natural idempotency for the repair path — while a post-mutation\\n84\\t// replacement (next version) never collides with an in-flight pre-mutation\\n85\\t// item.\\n86\\texport function recordItem(\\n87\\t  db: DatabaseSync,\\n88\\t  input: WorkItemInput,\\n89\\t  queuedAt: string,\\n90\\t): WorkItemRecord {\\n91\\t  const sourceVersion = input.sourceVersion ?? 1;\\n92\\t  const workItemId = `w-${sourceIdOf(input.sourceRef)}-${input.kind}-v${sourceVersion}`;\\n93\\t  db.prepare(\\n94\\t    `INSERT INTO work_item (work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, status, queued_at, payload)\\n95\\t     VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, 'queued', ?, ?)`,\\n96\\t  ).run(\\n97\\t    workItemId,\\n98\\t    input.owner,\\n99\\t    input.kind,\\n100\\t    JSON.stringify(input.sourceRef),\\n101\\t    queuedAt,\\n102\\t    JSON.stringify({ sourceVersion, forms: input.forms ?? [] }),\\n103\\t  );\\n104\\t  return {\\n105\\t    workItemId,\\n106\\t    owner: input.owner,\\n107\\t    kind: input.kind,\\n108\\t    sourceRef: input.sourceRef,\\n109\\t    status: \\\"queued\\\",\\n110\\t    queuedAt,\\n111\\t  };\\n112\\t}\\n113\\t\\n114\\texport interface EnqueueInput extends WorkItemInput {\\n115\\t  forms: readonly EnqueueFormTarget[];\\n116\\t}\\n117\\t\\n118\\t// The one way work is scheduled (DD-5): the work row, the form's `pending`\\n119\\t// state row, and the scheduler poke all ride the ambient transaction — they\\n120\\t// commit together or vanish together. Enqueue is the *only* place a\\n121\\t// derived_form row is created (completion is UPDATE-only); re-enqueueing\\n122\\t// resets an existing row to pending at the enqueued source version.\\n123\\texport function enqueue(ctx: OperationContext, input: EnqueueInput): WorkItemRecord {\\n124\\t  const item = recordItem(ctx.db, input, ctx.clock().toISOString());\\n125\\t  const sourceVersion = input.sourceVersion ?? 1;\\n126\\t  const upsert = ctx.db.prepare(\\n127\\t    `INSERT INTO derived_form (subject_kind, subject_id, form, state, source_version)\\n128\\t     VALUES (?, ?, ?, 'pending', ?)\\n129\\t     ON CONFLICT (subject_kind, subject_id, form) DO UPDATE SET\\n130\\t       state = 'pending', content = NULL, reason = NULL, metadata = NULL,\\n131\\t       gaps = NULL, derived_at = NULL, source_version = excluded.source_version`,\\n132\\t  );\\n133\\t  for (const target of input.forms) {\\n134\\t    upsert.run(target.subjectKind, target.subjectId, target.form, sourceVersion);\\n135\\t  }\\n136\\t  ctx.onCommit(() => fireSchedulerPoke(ctx.threadId));\\n137\\t  return item;\\n138\\t}\\n139\\t\\n140\\tinterface RawWorkItemRow {\\n141\\t  work_item_id: string;\\n142\\t  owner: string;\\n143\\t  kind: string;\\n144\\t  source_ref: string;\\n145\\t  queued_at: string;\\n146\\t}\\n147\\t\\n148\\t// ── claim mechanics (Epic 02 Story 1, tech design §Mechanics) ─────\\n149\\t// All pure SQL against the caller's open handle, domain-blind. Claim,\\n150\\t// complete, and the terminal half of failAttempt each own one short\\n151\\t// BEGIN IMMEDIATE; the handler in between runs with no open transaction.\\n152\\t\\n153\\t// The claimed row as the drain holds it: mechanical fields plus the payload's\\n154\\t// source version and form targets. `kind` is a plain string here — a raw row\\n155\\t// with an unregistered kind must still be claimable so the drain can land it\\n156\\t// failed_terminal instead of skipping it (AC-1.8).\\n157\\texport interface ClaimedWorkItem {\\n158\\t  workItemId: string;\\n159\\t  owner: WorkOwner;\\n160\\t  kind: string;\\n161\\t  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n162\\t  attempts: number;\\n163\\t  queuedAt: string;\\n164\\t  sourceVersion: number;\\n165\\t  forms: EnqueueFormTarget[];\\n166\\t}\\n167\\t\\n168\\texport type ClaimOutcome =\\n169\\t  | { outcome: \\\"claimed\\\"; item: ClaimedWorkItem }\\n170\\t  | { outcome: \\\"empty\\\" }\\n171\\t  | { outcome: \\\"in_flight\\\"; claimExpiresAt: string }\\n172\\t  | { outcome: \\\"waiting\\\"; waitingUntil: string };\\n173\\t\\n174\\tinterface RawClaimRow {\\n175\\t  work_item_id: string;\\n176\\t  owner: string;\\n177\\t  kind: string;\\n178\\t  source_ref: string;\\n179\\t  attempts: number | bigint;\\n180\\t  queued_at: string;\\n181\\t  payload: string | null;\\n182\\t}\\n183\\t\\n184\\t// Form targets for pre-v5 rows, whose payload is NULL (Epic 01 wrote no\\n185\\t// payload column). Exactly the F-02 backfill's kind→form mapping (migration\\n186\\t// v5, shared/storage.ts): the backfill created pending derived_form rows at\\n187\\t// source version 1 for these kinds, and a terminal failure must find them —\\n188\\t// otherwise exhaustion would delete the work row and strand the backfilled\\n189\\t// form as pending forever, breaking AC-1.9/DD-1 for upgraded threads.\\n190\\tconst LEGACY_KIND_FORMS: Partial<\\n191\\t  Record<string, ReadonlyArray<{ subjectKind: SubjectKind; form: FormKind }>>\\n192\\t> = {\\n193\\t  prompt_smoothing: [{ subjectKind: \\\"message\\\", form: \\\"smoothed_prompt\\\" }],\\n194\\t  tool_result_summary: [{ subjectKind: \\\"message\\\", form: \\\"tool_result_summary\\\" }],\\n195\\t  turn_derivation: [\\n196\\t    { subjectKind: \\\"turn\\\", form: \\\"turn_rendering\\\" },\\n197\\t    { subjectKind: \\\"turn\\\", form: \\\"lower_band_projection\\\" },\\n198\\t  ],\\n199\\t};\\n200\\t\\n201\\tfunction legacyFormTargets(kind: string, sourceRef: WorkSourceRef): EnqueueFormTarget[] {\\n202\\t  const mapped = LEGACY_KIND_FORMS[kind];\\n203\\t  if (mapped === undefined) return [];\\n204\\t  const subjectId = sourceIdOf(sourceRef);\\n205\\t  return mapped.map((target) => ({ ...target, subjectId }));\\n206\\t}\\n207\\t\\n208\\tfunction toClaimedItem(row: RawClaimRow): ClaimedWorkItem {\\n209\\t  const sourceRef = JSON.parse(row.source_ref) as WorkSourceRef;\\n210\\t  const payload =\\n211\\t    row.payload === null\\n212\\t      ? // Pre-v5 row: version 1 (matching the backfill) and form targets\\n213\\t        // reconstructed from the backfill's own mapping.\\n214\\t        { sourceVersion: 1, forms: legacyFormTargets(row.kind, sourceRef) }\\n215\\t      : (JSON.parse(row.payload) as { sourceVersion?: number; forms?: EnqueueFormTarget[] });\\n216\\t  return {\\n217\\t    workItemId: row.work_item_id,\\n218\\t    owner: row.owner as WorkOwner,\\n219\\t    kind: row.kind,\\n220\\t    sourceRef,\\n221\\t    attempts: Number(row.attempts),\\n222\\t    queuedAt: row.queued_at,\\n223\\t    sourceVersion: payload.sourceVersion ?? 1,\\n224\\t    forms: payload.forms ?? [],\\n225\\t  };\\n226\\t}\\n227\\t\\n228\\t// Head-first, never skip-ahead: the claim decision is made against the oldest\\n229\\t// live row only — a later eligible row is never considered while an older\\n230\\t// live row exists. One atomic UPDATE under BEGIN IMMEDIATE (no read-then-\\n231\\t// write split: the race window is the bug class). The CASE increments\\n232\\t// `attempts` only when reclaiming an expired claimed row — a reclaim means a\\n233\\t// run died or hung, and the increment makes the crash visible in the report\\n234\\t// and counts it against the retry budget; normal claims never touch it.\\n235\\texport function claimNext(db: DatabaseSync, now: string, leaseDurationMs: number): ClaimOutcome {\\n236\\t  const expiresAt = new Date(Date.parse(now) + leaseDurationMs).toISOString();\\n237\\t  db.exec(\\\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\\\");\\n238\\t  try {\\n239\\t    const claimed = db\\n240\\t      .prepare(\\n241\\t        `UPDATE work_item SET status = 'claimed', claimed_at = ?, claim_expires_at = ?,\\n242\\t           attempts = attempts + CASE WHEN status = 'claimed' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END\\n243\\t         WHERE work_item_id = (\\n244\\t           SELECT work_item_id FROM (\\n245\\t             SELECT work_item_id, status, eligible_at, claim_expires_at\\n246\\t             FROM work_item WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed')\\n247\\t             ORDER BY rowid LIMIT 1\\n248\\t           )\\n249\\t           WHERE (status = 'queued' AND (eligible_at IS NULL OR eligible_at <= ?))\\n250\\t              OR (status = 'claimed' AND claim_expires_at <= ?)\\n251\\t         )\\n252\\t         RETURNING work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, attempts, queued_at, payload`,\\n253\\t      )\\n254\\t      .get(now, expiresAt, now, now) as unknown as RawClaimRow | undefined;\\n255\\t    if (claimed !== undefined) {\\n256\\t      db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n257\\t      return { outcome: \\\"claimed\\\", item: toClaimedItem(claimed) };\\n258\\t    }\\n259\\t    // No row claimable: re-read the head inside the same transaction to\\n260\\t    // classify the stop. A claimed head with a live lease gates the queue\\n261\\t    // (in_flight, AC-1.4); a queued head still backing off gates it too\\n262\\t    // (waiting) — strict ordering, never reordered around the head.\\n263\\t    const head = db\\n264\\t      .prepare(\\n265\\t        `SELECT status, eligible_at, claim_expires_at\\n266\\t         FROM work_item WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed')\\n267\\t         ORDER BY rowid LIMIT 1`,\\n268\\t      )\\n269\\t      .get() as unknown as\\n270\\t      | { status: string; eligible_at: string | null; claim_expires_at: string | null }\\n271\\t      | undefined;\\n272\\t    db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n273\\t    if (head === undefined) return { outcome: \\\"empty\\\" };\\n274\\t    if (head.status === \\\"claimed\\\") {\\n275\\t      return { outcome: \\\"in_flight\\\", claimExpiresAt: head.claim_expires_at ?? \\\"\\\" };\\n276\\t    }\\n277\\t    return { outcome: \\\"waiting\\\", waitingUntil: head.eligible_at ?? \\\"\\\" };\\n278\\t  } catch (cause) {\\n279\\t    db.exec(\\\"ROLLBACK;\\\");\\n280\\t    throw cause;\\n281\\t  }\\n282\\t}\\n283\\t\\n284\\t// Completion: the version-checked form writes and the item row's deletion in\\n285\\t// one short transaction (DD-1: terminal transition deletes the row; the\\n286\\t// disposition is reported in-memory, never stored). UPDATE-only, never\\n287\\t// upsert — the pending row exists from enqueue; a write that finds no row at\\n288\\t// the item's source version is discarded as stale (DD-3 truth table). The\\n289\\t// optional onApplied hook (Story 3) runs inside this same transaction, after\\n290\\t// the form writes and only when they landed — chunk placement and the\\n291\\t// close→summary enqueues ride the completion commit; its onCommit\\n292\\t// registrations (the enqueue pokes) flush after COMMIT and drop on rollback.\\n293\\texport function complete(\\n294\\t  db: DatabaseSync,\\n295\\t  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\\n296\\t  writes: readonly HandlerFormWrite[],\\n297\\t  derivedAt: string,\\n298\\t  onApplied?: (tx: CompletionTx) => void,\\n299\\t): \\\"done\\\" | \\\"stale_discarded\\\" {\\n300\\t  const hooks = createCommitHooks();\\n301\\t  db.exec(\\\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\\\");\\n302\\t  try {\\n303\\t    let hits = 0;\\n304\\t    const update = db.prepare(\\n305\\t      `UPDATE derived_form\\n306\\t       SET state = 'ready', content = ?, reason = NULL, metadata = ?, gaps = ?, derived_at = ?\\n307\\t       WHERE subject_kind = ? AND subject_id = ? AND form = ? AND source_version = ?`,\\n308\\t    );\\n309\\t    for (const write of writes) {\\n310\\t      const changed = update.run(\\n311\\t        write.content,\\n312\\t        write.metadata === undefined ? null : JSON.stringify(write.metadata),\\n313\\t        write.gaps === undefined ? null : JSON.stringify(write.gaps),\\n314\\t        derivedAt,\\n315\\t        write.subjectKind,\\n316\\t        write.subjectId,\\n317\\t        write.form,\\n318\\t        item.sourceVersion,\\n319\\t      );\\n320\\t      hits += Number(changed.changes);\\n321\\t    }\\n322\\t    const stale = writes.length > 0 && hits === 0;\\n323\\t    if (!stale && onApplied !== undefined) {\\n324\\t      onApplied({ db, onCommit: hooks.register });\\n325\\t    }\\n326\\t    db.prepare(`DELETE FROM work_item WHERE work_item_id = ?`).run(item.workItemId);\\n327\\t    db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n328\\t    hooks.flush();\\n329\\t    return stale ? \\\"stale_discarded\\\" : \\\"done\\\";\\n330\\t  } catch (cause) {\\n331\\t    db.exec(\\\"ROLLBACK;\\\");\\n332\\t    throw cause;\\n333\\t  }\\n334\\t}\\n335\\t\\n336\\texport interface RetryPolicy {\\n337\\t  budget: number;\\n338\\t  backoffBaseMs: number;\\n339\\t  backoffCapMs: number;\\n340\\t}\\n341\\t\\n342\\texport type FailAttemptResult =\\n343\\t  | { terminal: false; attempts: number; eligibleAt: string }\\n344\\t  | { terminal: true; attempts: number };\\n345\\t\\n346\\t// Terminal failure: the form rows land `failed` (or `blocked` for source\\n347\\t// damage) carrying the final reason plus attempts/last-error copied onto the\\n348\\t// form's metadata, and the item row is deleted — one transaction (DD-1).\\n349\\t// Form writes stay version-checked: if a mutation moved the source since this\\n350\\t// item was queued, the stale failure must not stamp the rebuilt form.\\n351\\texport function failTerminal(\\n352\\t  db: DatabaseSync,\\n353\\t  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\\n354\\t  opts: { reason: string; formState: \\\"failed\\\" | \\\"blocked\\\"; attempts: number; now: string },\\n355\\t): void {\\n356\\t  db.exec(\\\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\\\");\\n357\\t  try {\\n358\\t    const update = db.prepare(\\n359\\t      `UPDATE derived_form\\n360\\t       SET state = ?, content = NULL, reason = ?, metadata = ?, gaps = NULL, derived_at = ?\\n361\\t       WHERE subject_kind = ? AND subject_id = ? AND form = ? AND source_version = ?`,\\n362\\t    );\\n363\\t    const metadata = JSON.stringify({ attempts: opts.attempts, lastError: opts.reason });\\n364\\t    for (const target of item.forms) {\\n365\\t      update.run(\\n366\\t        opts.formState,\\n367\\t        opts.reason,\\n368\\t        metadata,\\n369\\t        opts.now,\\n370\\t        target.subjectKind,\\n371\\t        target.subjectId,\\n372\\t        target.form,\\n373\\t        item.sourceVersion,\\n374\\t      );\\n375\\t    }\\n376\\t    db.prepare(`DELETE FROM work_item WHERE work_item_id = ?`).run(item.workItemId);\\n377\\t    db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n378\\t  } catch (cause) {\\n379\\t    db.exec(\\\"ROLLBACK;\\\");\\n380\\t    throw cause;\\n381\\t  }\\n382\\t}\\n383\\t\\n384\\t// A failed run counts itself. Under budget and retryable: the row goes back\\n385\\t// to `queued` with attempts incremented and eligibility pushed out by backoff\\n386\\t// (min(base × 2^attempts, cap)) — retry is not a status (DD-1). At budget, or\\n387\\t// on a non-retryable failure, the item is terminal: form `failed` with the\\n388\\t// final reason, row deleted (AC-1.9).\\n389\\texport function failAttempt(\\n390\\t  db: DatabaseSync,\\n391\\t  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\\n392\\t  opts: { reason: string; retryable: boolean; now: string; retry: RetryPolicy },\\n393\\t): FailAttemptResult {\\n394\\t  const attempts = item.attempts + 1;\\n395\\t  if (opts.retryable && attempts < opts.retry.budget) {\\n396\\t    const backoffMs = Math.min(\\n397\\t      opts.retry.backoffBaseMs * 2 ** attempts,\\n398\\t      opts.retry.backoffCapMs,\\n399\\t    );\\n400\\t    const eligibleAt = new Date(Date.parse(opts.now) + backoffMs).toISOString();\\n401\\t    db.exec(\\\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\\\");\\n402\\t    try {\\n403\\t      db.prepare(\\n404\\t        `UPDATE work_item\\n405\\t         SET status = 'queued', attempts = ?, last_error = ?,\\n406\\t             claimed_at = NULL, claim_expires_at = NULL, eligible_at = ?\\n407\\t         WHERE work_item_id = ?`,\\n408\\t      ).run(attempts, opts.reason, eligibleAt, item.workItemId);\\n409\\t      db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n410\\t    } catch (cause) {\\n411\\t      db.exec(\\\"ROLLBACK;\\\");\\n412\\t      throw cause;\\n413\\t    }\\n414\\t    return { terminal: false, attempts, eligibleAt };\\n415\\t  }\\n416\\t  failTerminal(db, item, { reason: opts.reason, formState: \\\"failed\\\", attempts, now: opts.now });\\n417\\t  return { terminal: true, attempts };\\n418\\t}\\n419\\t\\n420\\t// Cascade tidy (tech design issue 1): delete still-queued items for the given\\n421\\t// (kind, sourceRef) targets, returning the deleted ids for the mutation\\n422\\t// result's `superseded` list. Runs inside the caller's ambient transaction —\\n423\\t// the cascade owns the BEGIN/COMMIT — so no transaction is opened here.\\n424\\t// Claimed items are deliberately left alone: the source-version check (DD-3)\\n425\\t// discards their stale completions. Ships here as mechanics; its only caller\\n426\\t// is Story 5's cascade.\\n427\\texport function supersedeQueued(\\n428\\t  db: DatabaseSync,\\n429\\t  targets: ReadonlyArray<{ kind: WorkKind; sourceRef: WorkSourceRef }>,\\n430\\t): string[] {\\n431\\t  const ids: string[] = [];\\n432\\t  const remove = db.prepare(\\n433\\t    `DELETE FROM work_item WHERE status = 'queued' AND kind = ? AND source_ref = ?\\n434\\t     RETURNING work_item_id`,\\n435\\t  );\\n436\\t  for (const target of targets) {\\n437\\t    const rows = remove.all(target.kind, JSON.stringify(target.sourceRef)) as unknown as Array<{\\n438\\t      work_item_id: string;\\n439\\t    }>;\\n440\\t    for (const row of rows) ids.push(row.work_item_id);\\n441\\t  }\\n442\\t  return ids;\\n443\\t}\\n444\\t\\n445\\t// The requeue no-op rule's EXISTS check (AC-4.5): is there a live item —\\n446\\t// queued or in flight — for this kind and source at this source version?\\n447\\t// Runs inside the caller's requeue transaction so the check and the enqueue\\n448\\t// commit together (anti-shim: a check-then-enqueue split across transactions\\n449\\t// reintroduces the duplicate-work race). Version-scoped on purpose: a\\n450\\t// claimed pre-mutation item at an older version is dying work whose\\n451\\t// completion DD-3 will discard — it must not block the repair.\\n452\\texport function hasLiveItem(\\n453\\t  db: DatabaseSync,\\n454\\t  kind: WorkKind,\\n455\\t  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef,\\n456\\t  sourceVersion: number,\\n457\\t): boolean {\\n458\\t  const row = db\\n459\\t    .prepare(\\n460\\t      `SELECT 1 FROM work_item\\n461\\t       WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed') AND kind = ? AND source_ref = ?\\n462\\t         AND COALESCE(json_extract(payload, '$.sourceVersion'), 1) = ?\\n463\\t       LIMIT 1`,\\n464\\t    )\\n465\\t    .get(kind, JSON.stringify(sourceRef), sourceVersion);\\n466\\t  return row !== undefined;\\n467\\t}\\n468\\t\\n469\\t// Live items left in the queue — the drain report's `remaining` and the\\n470\\t// repair report's join source (Story 4).\\n471\\texport function countLiveItems(db: DatabaseSync): number {\\n472\\t  const row = db\\n473\\t    .prepare(`SELECT COUNT(*) AS n FROM work_item WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed')`)\\n474\\t    .get() as unknown as { n: number | bigint };\\n475\\t  return Number(row.n);\\n476\\t}\\n477\\t\\n478\\texport interface QueueDetailRow {\\n479\\t  workItemId: string;\\n480\\t  owner: WorkOwner;\\n481\\t  kind: string;\\n482\\t  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n483\\t  status: \\\"queued\\\" | \\\"claimed\\\";\\n484\\t  attempts: number;\\n485\\t  lastError?: string;\\n486\\t  eligibleAt?: string;\\n487\\t  claimedAt?: string;\\n488\\t  claimExpiresAt?: string;\\n489\\t  queuedAt: string;\\n490\\t  sourceVersion: number;\\n491\\t}\\n492\\t\\n493\\t// Mechanical detail of every live row in queue order — what the repair\\n494\\t// report joins against derived_form (Flow 4) and what tests read to assert\\n495\\t// lease/backoff state without raw SQL at every call site.\\n496\\texport function queueDetail(db: DatabaseSync): QueueDetailRow[] {\\n497\\t  const rows = db\\n498\\t    .prepare(\\n499\\t      `SELECT work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, status, attempts, last_error,\\n500\\t              eligible_at, claimed_at, claim_expires_at, queued_at, payload\\n501\\t       FROM work_item ORDER BY rowid`,\\n502\\t    )\\n503\\t    .all() as unknown as Array<\\n504\\t    RawClaimRow & {\\n505\\t      status: string;\\n506\\t      last_error: string | null;\\n507\\t      eligible_at: string | null;\\n508\\t      claimed_at: string | null;\\n509\\t      claim_expires_at: string | null;\\n510\\t    }\\n511\\t  >;\\n512\\t  return rows.map((row) => {\\n513\\t    const item = toClaimedItem(row);\\n514\\t    const detail: QueueDetailRow = {\\n515\\t      workItemId: item.workItemId,\\n516\\t      owner: item.owner,\\n517\\t      kind: item.kind,\\n518\\t      sourceRef: item.sourceRef,\\n519\\t      status: row.status as \\\"queued\\\" | \\\"claimed\\\",\\n520\\t      attempts: item.attempts,\\n521\\t      queuedAt: item.queuedAt,\\n522\\t      sourceVersion: item.sourceVersion,\\n523\\t    };\\n524\\t    if (row.last_error !== null) detail.lastError = row.last_error;\\n525\\t    if (row.eligible_at !== null) detail.eligibleAt = row.eligible_at;\\n526\\t    if (row.claimed_at !== null) detail.claimedAt = row.claimed_at;\\n527\\t    if (row.claim_expires_at !== null) detail.claimExpiresAt = row.claim_expires_at;\\n528\\t    return detail;\\n529\\t  });\\n530\\t}\\n531\\t\\n532\\t// Listing in queue order (insertion order within the walk); each owning\\n533\\t// domain lists only its own items.\\n534\\texport function listItems(db: DatabaseSync, owner: WorkOwner): WorkItemRecord[] {\\n535\\t  const rows = db\\n536\\t    .prepare(\\n537\\t      `SELECT work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, queued_at\\n538\\t       FROM work_item WHERE owner = ? ORDER BY rowid`,\\n539\\t    )\\n540\\t    .all(owner) as unknown as RawWorkItemRow[];\\n541\\t  return rows.map((row) => ({\\n542\\t    workItemId: row.work_item_id,\\n543\\t    owner: row.owner as WorkOwner,\\n544\\t    kind: row.kind as WorkKind,\\n545\\t    sourceRef: JSON.parse(row.source_ref) as WorkSourceRef,\\n546\\t    status: \\\"queued\\\",\\n547\\t    queuedAt: row.queued_at,\\n548\\t  }));\\n549\\t}\\n550\\t\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"b8d4e4d4-0320-4750-a113-f05b3f0942c7\",\"uuid\":\"669ecf4b-4725-4306-a7d5-4122ca6fcf48\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-06-11T08:14:09.495Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/src/tech-utils/work-queue/index.ts\",\"content\":\"// Durable work-item mechanics: recording, ordered listing, and the enqueue\\n// wrapper that makes \\\"queueing is what schedules processing\\\" structural\\n// (DD-5). The util is domain-blind by the tech-arch capability rule — it\\n// knows the item vocabulary (owner, kind, sourceRef) but nothing about what\\n// a turn or a summary is; which kinds queue for which sources, and which\\n// derived forms a kind produces, is the owning domains' business (callers\\n// pass the form targets in). Writes run on the caller's handle inside the\\n// ambient transaction, so items commit (or roll back) with the batch. No\\n// public SDK surface: read-back reaches here only through the owning\\n// domains' listQueuedWork.\\nimport type { DatabaseSync } from \\\"node:sqlite\\\";\\nimport {\\n  createCommitHooks,\\n  fireSchedulerPoke,\\n  type OperationContext,\\n} from \\\"../../shared/context.js\\\";\\nimport type {\\n  CompletionTx,\\n  FormKind,\\n  HandlerFormWrite,\\n  SubjectKind,\\n} from \\\"../../shared/derivation.js\\\";\\n\\nexport type WorkOwner = \\\"messages\\\" | \\\"turns\\\";\\nexport type WorkKind =\\n  | \\\"prompt_smoothing\\\"\\n  | \\\"tool_call_summary\\\"\\n  | \\\"tool_result_summary\\\"\\n  | \\\"turn_derivation\\\"\\n  | \\\"chunk_summary_detailed\\\"\\n  | \\\"chunk_summary_brief\\\";\\nexport type WorkSourceRef = { messageId: string } | { turnId: string } | { chunkId: string };\\n\\n// The work-kind registry: owner and sourceRef semantics per the epic's Work\\n// Item contract. Mechanical metadata only — what a kind *means* stays with\\n// the owning domain's handler (registered at SDK construction, DD-6).\\nexport const WORK_KIND_REGISTRY: Readonly<\\n  Record<WorkKind, { owner: WorkOwner; sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" | \\\"turnId\\\" | \\\"chunkId\\\" }>\\n> = {\\n  prompt_smoothing: { owner: \\\"messages\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" },\\n  tool_call_summary: { owner: \\\"messages\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" },\\n  tool_result_summary: { owner: \\\"messages\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" },\\n  turn_derivation: { owner: \\\"turns\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"turnId\\\" },\\n  chunk_summary_detailed: { owner: \\\"turns\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"chunkId\\\" },\\n  chunk_summary_brief: { owner: \\\"turns\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"chunkId\\\" },\\n};\\n\\nexport interface WorkItemRecord {\\n  workItemId: string;\\n  owner: WorkOwner;\\n  kind: WorkKind;\\n  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n  status: \\\"queued\\\"; // the only status written before a drain claims (Epic 02 Story 1)\\n  queuedAt: string;\\n}\\n\\n// The derived forms an enqueue is scheduling work toward; the owning domain\\n// names them (the util stays meaning-blind). Stored in the row's payload so\\n// the drain's terminal paths (retry exhaustion, blocked source) can land the\\n// form's failed/blocked state without asking any domain what a kind means.\\nexport interface EnqueueFormTarget {\\n  subjectKind: SubjectKind;\\n  subjectId: string;\\n  form: FormKind;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface WorkItemInput {\\n  owner: WorkOwner;\\n  kind: WorkKind;\\n  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n  sourceVersion?: number; // defaults to 1 — first version of a fresh source\\n  forms?: readonly EnqueueFormTarget[]; // carried in payload for the drain's terminal paths\\n}\\n\\nfunction sourceIdOf(sourceRef: WorkSourceRef): string {\\n  if (\\\"messageId\\\" in sourceRef) return sourceRef.messageId;\\n  if (\\\"turnId\\\" in sourceRef) return sourceRef.turnId;\\n  return sourceRef.chunkId;\\n}\\n\\n// Deterministic id scoped to the source version (DD-1/DD-3): re-queueing the\\n// same kind for the same source *at the same version* is the same id —\\n// natural idempotency for the repair path — while a post-mutation\\n// replacement (next version) never collides with an in-flight pre-mutation\\n// item.\\nexport function recordItem(\\n  db: DatabaseSync,\\n  input: WorkItemInput,\\n  queuedAt: string,\\n): WorkItemRecord {\\n  const sourceVersion = input.sourceVersion ?? 1;\\n  const workItemId = `w-${sourceIdOf(input.sourceRef)}-${input.kind}-v${sourceVersion}`;\\n  db.prepare(\\n    `INSERT INTO work_item (work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, status, queued_at, payload)\\n     VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, 'queued', ?, ?)`,\\n  ).run(\\n    workItemId,\\n    input.owner,\\n    input.kind,\\n    JSON.stringify(input.sourceRef),\\n    queuedAt,\\n    JSON.stringify({ sourceVersion, forms: input.forms ?? [] }),\\n  );\\n  return {\\n    workItemId,\\n    owner: input.owner,\\n    kind: input.kind,\\n    sourceRef: input.sourceRef,\\n    status: \\\"queued\\\",\\n    queuedAt,\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nexport interface EnqueueInput extends WorkItemInput {\\n  forms: readonly EnqueueFormTarget[];\\n}\\n\\n// The one way work is scheduled (DD-5): the work row, the form's `pending`\\n// state row, and the scheduler poke all ride the ambient transaction — they\\n// commit together or vanish together. Enqueue is the *only* place a\\n// derived_form row is created (completion is UPDATE-only); re-enqueueing\\n// resets an existing row to pending at the enqueued source version.\\nexport function enqueue(ctx: OperationContext, input: EnqueueInput): WorkItemRecord {\\n  const item = recordItem(ctx.db, input, ctx.clock().toISOString());\\n  const sourceVersion = input.sourceVersion ?? 1;\\n  const upsert = ctx.db.prepare(\\n    `INSERT INTO derived_form (subject_kind, subject_id, form, state, source_version)\\n     VALUES (?, ?, ?, 'pending', ?)\\n     ON CONFLICT (subject_kind, subject_id, form) DO UPDATE SET\\n       state = 'pending', content = NULL, reason = NULL, metadata = NULL,\\n       gaps = NULL, derived_at = NULL, source_version = excluded.source_version`,\\n  );\\n  for (const target of input.forms) {\\n    upsert.run(target.subjectKind, target.subjectId, target.form, sourceVersion);\\n  }\\n  ctx.onCommit(() => fireSchedulerPoke(ctx.threadId));\\n  return item;\\n}\\n\\ninterface RawWorkItemRow {\\n  work_item_id: string;\\n  owner: string;\\n  kind: string;\\n  source_ref: string;\\n  queued_at: string;\\n}\\n\\n// ── claim mechanics (Epic 02 Story 1, tech design §Mechanics) ─────\\n// All pure SQL against the caller's open handle, domain-blind. Claim,\\n// complete, and the terminal half of failAttempt each own one short\\n// BEGIN IMMEDIATE; the handler in between runs with no open transaction.\\n\\n// The claimed row as the drain holds it: mechanical fields plus the payload's\\n// source version and form targets. `kind` is a plain string here — a raw row\\n// with an unregistered kind must still be claimable so the drain can land it\\n// failed_terminal instead of skipping it (AC-1.8).\\nexport interface ClaimedWorkItem {\\n  workItemId: string;\\n  owner: WorkOwner;\\n  kind: string;\\n  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n  attempts: number;\\n  queuedAt: string;\\n  sourceVersion: number;\\n  forms: EnqueueFormTarget[];\\n}\\n\\nexport type ClaimOutcome =\\n  | { outcome: \\\"claimed\\\"; item: ClaimedWorkItem }\\n  | { outcome: \\\"empty\\\" }\\n  | { outcome: \\\"in_flight\\\"; claimExpiresAt: string }\\n  | { outcome: \\\"waiting\\\"; waitingUntil: string };\\n\\ninterface RawClaimRow {\\n  work_item_id: string;\\n  owner: string;\\n  kind: string;\\n  source_ref: string;\\n  attempts: number | bigint;\\n  queued_at: string;\\n  payload: string | null;\\n}\\n\\n// Form targets for pre-v5 rows, whose payload is NULL (Epic 01 wrote no\\n// payload column). Exactly the F-02 backfill's kind→form mapping (migration\\n// v5, shared/storage.ts): the backfill created pending derived_form rows at\\n// source version 1 for these kinds, and a terminal failure must find them —\\n// otherwise exhaustion would delete the work row and strand the backfilled\\n// form as pending forever, breaking AC-1.9/DD-1 for upgraded threads.\\nconst LEGACY_KIND_FORMS: Partial<\\n  Record<string, ReadonlyArray<{ subjectKind: SubjectKind; form: FormKind }>>\\n> = {\\n  prompt_smoothing: [{ subjectKind: \\\"message\\\", form: \\\"smoothed_prompt\\\" }],\\n  tool_result_summary: [{ subjectKind: \\\"message\\\", form: \\\"tool_result_summary\\\" }],\\n  turn_derivation: [\\n    { subjectKind: \\\"turn\\\", form: \\\"turn_rendering\\\" },\\n    { subjectKind: \\\"turn\\\", form: \\\"lower_band_projection\\\" },\\n  ],\\n};\\n\\nfunction legacyFormTargets(kind: string, sourceRef: WorkSourceRef): EnqueueFormTarget[] {\\n  const mapped = LEGACY_KIND_FORMS[kind];\\n  if (mapped === undefined) return [];\\n  const subjectId = sourceIdOf(sourceRef);\\n  return mapped.map((target) => ({ ...target, subjectId }));\\n}\\n\\nfunction toClaimedItem(row: RawClaimRow): ClaimedWorkItem {\\n  const sourceRef = JSON.parse(row.source_ref) as WorkSourceRef;\\n  const payload =\\n    row.payload === null\\n      ? // Pre-v5 row: version 1 (matching the backfill) and form targets\\n        // reconstructed from the backfill's own mapping.\\n        { sourceVersion: 1, forms: legacyFormTargets(row.kind, sourceRef) }\\n      : (JSON.parse(row.payload) as { sourceVersion?: number; forms?: EnqueueFormTarget[] });\\n  return {\\n    workItemId: row.work_item_id,\\n    owner: row.owner as WorkOwner,\\n    kind: row.kind,\\n    sourceRef,\\n    attempts: Number(row.attempts),\\n    queuedAt: row.queued_at,\\n    sourceVersion: payload.sourceVersion ?? 1,\\n    forms: payload.forms ?? [],\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n// Head-first, never skip-ahead: the claim decision is made against the oldest\\n// live row only — a later eligible row is never considered while an older\\n// live row exists. One atomic UPDATE under BEGIN IMMEDIATE (no read-then-\\n// write split: the race window is the bug class). The CASE increments\\n// `attempts` only when reclaiming an expired claimed row — a reclaim means a\\n// run died or hung, and the increment makes the crash visible in the report\\n// and counts it against the retry budget; normal claims never touch it.\\nexport function claimNext(db: DatabaseSync, now: string, leaseDurationMs: number): ClaimOutcome {\\n  const expiresAt = new Date(Date.parse(now) + leaseDurationMs).toISOString();\\n  db.exec(\\\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\\\");\\n  try {\\n    const claimed = db\\n      .prepare(\\n        `UPDATE work_item SET status = 'claimed', claimed_at = ?, claim_expires_at = ?,\\n           attempts = attempts + CASE WHEN status = 'claimed' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END\\n         WHERE work_item_id = (\\n           SELECT work_item_id FROM (\\n             SELECT work_item_id, status, eligible_at, claim_expires_at\\n             FROM work_item WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed')\\n             ORDER BY rowid LIMIT 1\\n           )\\n           WHERE (status = 'queued' AND (eligible_at IS NULL OR eligible_at <= ?))\\n              OR (status = 'claimed' AND claim_expires_at <= ?)\\n         )\\n         RETURNING work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, attempts, queued_at, payload`,\\n      )\\n      .get(now, expiresAt, now, now) as unknown as RawClaimRow | undefined;\\n    if (claimed !== undefined) {\\n      db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n      return { outcome: \\\"claimed\\\", item: toClaimedItem(claimed) };\\n    }\\n    // No row claimable: re-read the head inside the same transaction to\\n    // classify the stop. A claimed head with a live lease gates the queue\\n    // (in_flight, AC-1.4); a queued head still backing off gates it too\\n    // (waiting) — strict ordering, never reordered around the head.\\n    const head = db\\n      .prepare(\\n        `SELECT status, eligible_at, claim_expires_at\\n         FROM work_item WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed')\\n         ORDER BY rowid LIMIT 1`,\\n      )\\n      .get() as unknown as\\n      | { status: string; eligible_at: string | null; claim_expires_at: string | null }\\n      | undefined;\\n    db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n    if (head === undefined) return { outcome: \\\"empty\\\" };\\n    if (head.status === \\\"claimed\\\") {\\n      return { outcome: \\\"in_flight\\\", claimExpiresAt: head.claim_expires_at ?? \\\"\\\" };\\n    }\\n    return { outcome: \\\"waiting\\\", waitingUntil: head.eligible_at ?? \\\"\\\" };\\n  } catch (cause) {\\n    db.exec(\\\"ROLLBACK;\\\");\\n    throw cause;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Completion: the version-checked form writes and the item row's deletion in\\n// one short transaction (DD-1: terminal transition deletes the row; the\\n// disposition is reported in-memory, never stored). UPDATE-only, never\\n// upsert — the pending row exists from enqueue; a write that finds no row at\\n// the item's source version is discarded as stale (DD-3 truth table). The\\n// optional onApplied hook (Story 3) runs inside this same transaction, after\\n// the form writes and only when they landed — chunk placement and the\\n// close→summary enqueues ride the completion commit; its onCommit\\n// registrations (the enqueue pokes) flush after COMMIT and drop on rollback.\\nexport function complete(\\n  db: DatabaseSync,\\n  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\\n  writes: readonly HandlerFormWrite[],\\n  derivedAt: string,\\n  onApplied?: (tx: CompletionTx) => void,\\n): \\\"done\\\" | \\\"stale_discarded\\\" {\\n  const hooks = createCommitHooks();\\n  db.exec(\\\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\\\");\\n  try {\\n    let hits = 0;\\n    const update = db.prepare(\\n      `UPDATE derived_form\\n       SET state = 'ready', content = ?, reason = NULL, metadata = ?, gaps = ?, derived_at = ?\\n       WHERE subject_kind = ? AND subject_id = ? AND form = ? AND source_version = ?`,\\n    );\\n    for (const write of writes) {\\n      const changed = update.run(\\n        write.content,\\n        write.metadata === undefined ? null : JSON.stringify(write.metadata),\\n        write.gaps === undefined ? null : JSON.stringify(write.gaps),\\n        derivedAt,\\n        write.subjectKind,\\n        write.subjectId,\\n        write.form,\\n        item.sourceVersion,\\n      );\\n      hits += Number(changed.changes);\\n    }\\n    const stale = writes.length > 0 && hits === 0;\\n    if (!stale && onApplied !== undefined) {\\n      onApplied({ db, onCommit: hooks.register });\\n    }\\n    db.prepare(`DELETE FROM work_item WHERE work_item_id = ?`).run(item.workItemId);\\n    db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n    hooks.flush();\\n    return stale ? \\\"stale_discarded\\\" : \\\"done\\\";\\n  } catch (cause) {\\n    db.exec(\\\"ROLLBACK;\\\");\\n    throw cause;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nexport interface RetryPolicy {\\n  budget: number;\\n  backoffBaseMs: number;\\n  backoffCapMs: number;\\n}\\n\\nexport type FailAttemptResult =\\n  | { terminal: false; attempts: number; eligibleAt: string }\\n  | { terminal: true; attempts: number };\\n\\n// Terminal failure: the form rows land `failed` (or `blocked` for source\\n// damage) carrying the final reason plus attempts/last-error copied onto the\\n// form's metadata, and the item row is deleted — one transaction (DD-1).\\n// Form writes stay version-checked: if a mutation moved the source since this\\n// item was queued, the stale failure must not stamp the rebuilt form.\\nexport function failTerminal(\\n  db: DatabaseSync,\\n  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\\n  opts: { reason: string; formState: \\\"failed\\\" | \\\"blocked\\\"; attempts: number; now: string },\\n): void {\\n  db.exec(\\\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\\\");\\n  try {\\n    const update = db.prepare(\\n      `UPDATE derived_form\\n       SET state = ?, content = NULL, reason = ?, metadata = ?, gaps = NULL, derived_at = ?\\n       WHERE subject_kind = ? AND subject_id = ? AND form = ? AND source_version = ?`,\\n    );\\n    const metadata = JSON.stringify({ attempts: opts.attempts, lastError: opts.reason });\\n    for (const target of item.forms) {\\n      update.run(\\n        opts.formState,\\n        opts.reason,\\n        metadata,\\n        opts.now,\\n        target.subjectKind,\\n        target.subjectId,\\n        target.form,\\n        item.sourceVersion,\\n      );\\n    }\\n    db.prepare(`DELETE FROM work_item WHERE work_item_id = ?`).run(item.workItemId);\\n    db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n  } catch (cause) {\\n    db.exec(\\\"ROLLBACK;\\\");\\n    throw cause;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// A failed run counts itself. Under budget and retryable: the row goes back\\n// to `queued` with attempts incremented and eligibility pushed out by backoff\\n// (min(base × 2^attempts, cap)) — retry is not a status (DD-1). At budget, or\\n// on a non-retryable failure, the item is terminal: form `failed` with the\\n// final reason, row deleted (AC-1.9).\\nexport function failAttempt(\\n  db: DatabaseSync,\\n  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\\n  opts: { reason: string; retryable: boolean; now: string; retry: RetryPolicy },\\n): FailAttemptResult {\\n  const attempts = item.attempts + 1;\\n  if (opts.retryable && attempts < opts.retry.budget) {\\n    const backoffMs = Math.min(\\n      opts.retry.backoffBaseMs * 2 ** attempts,\\n      opts.retry.backoffCapMs,\\n    );\\n    const eligibleAt = new Date(Date.parse(opts.now) + backoffMs).toISOString();\\n    db.exec(\\\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\\\");\\n    try {\\n      db.prepare(\\n        `UPDATE work_item\\n         SET status = 'queued', attempts = ?, last_error = ?,\\n             claimed_at = NULL, claim_expires_at = NULL, eligible_at = ?\\n         WHERE work_item_id = ?`,\\n      ).run(attempts, opts.reason, eligibleAt, item.workItemId);\\n      db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n    } catch (cause) {\\n      db.exec(\\\"ROLLBACK;\\\");\\n      throw cause;\\n    }\\n    return { terminal: false, attempts, eligibleAt };\\n  }\\n  failTerminal(db, item, { reason: opts.reason, formState: \\\"failed\\\", attempts, now: opts.now });\\n  return { terminal: true, attempts };\\n}\\n\\n// Cascade tidy (tech design issue 1): delete still-queued items for the given\\n// (kind, sourceRef) targets, returning the deleted ids for the mutation\\n// result's `superseded` list. Runs inside the caller's ambient transaction —\\n// the cascade owns the BEGIN/COMMIT — so no transaction is opened here.\\n// Claimed items are deliberately left alone: the source-version check (DD-3)\\n// discards their stale completions. Ships here as mechanics; its only caller\\n// is Story 5's cascade.\\nexport function supersedeQueued(\\n  db: DatabaseSync,\\n  targets: ReadonlyArray<{ kind: WorkKind; sourceRef: WorkSourceRef }>,\\n): string[] {\\n  const ids: string[] = [];\\n  const remove = db.prepare(\\n    `DELETE FROM work_item WHERE status = 'queued' AND kind = ? AND source_ref = ?\\n     RETURNING work_item_id`,\\n  );\\n  for (const target of targets) {\\n    const rows = remove.all(target.kind, JSON.stringify(target.sourceRef)) as unknown as Array<{\\n      work_item_id: string;\\n    }>;\\n    for (const row of rows) ids.push(row.work_item_id);\\n  }\\n  return ids;\\n}\\n\\n// The requeue no-op rule's EXISTS check (AC-4.5): is there a live item —\\n// queued or in flight — for this kind and source at this source version?\\n// Runs inside the caller's requeue transaction so the check and the enqueue\\n// commit together (anti-shim: a check-then-enqueue split across transactions\\n// reintroduces the duplicate-work race). Version-scoped on purpose: a\\n// claimed pre-mutation item at an older version is dying work whose\\n// completion DD-3 will discard — it must not block the repair.\\nexport function hasLiveItem(\\n  db: DatabaseSync,\\n  kind: WorkKind,\\n  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef,\\n  sourceVersion: number,\\n): boolean {\\n  const row = db\\n    .prepare(\\n      `SELECT 1 FROM work_item\\n       WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed') AND kind = ? AND source_ref = ?\\n         AND COALESCE(json_extract(payload, '$.sourceVersion'), 1) = ?\\n       LIMIT 1`,\\n    )\\n    .get(kind, JSON.stringify(sourceRef), sourceVersion);\\n  return row !== undefined;\\n}\\n\\n// Live items left in the queue — the drain report's `remaining` and the\\n// repair report's join source (Story 4).\\nexport function countLiveItems(db: DatabaseSync): number {\\n  const row = db\\n    .prepare(`SELECT COUNT(*) AS n FROM work_item WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed')`)\\n    .get() as unknown as { n: number | bigint };\\n  return Number(row.n);\\n}\\n\\nexport interface QueueDetailRow {\\n  workItemId: string;\\n  owner: WorkOwner;\\n  kind: string;\\n  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n  status: \\\"queued\\\" | \\\"claimed\\\";\\n  attempts: number;\\n  lastError?: string;\\n  eligibleAt?: string;\\n  claimedAt?: string;\\n  claimExpiresAt?: string;\\n  queuedAt: string;\\n  sourceVersion: number;\\n}\\n\\n// Mechanical detail of every live row in queue order — what the repair\\n// report joins against derived_form (Flow 4) and what tests read to assert\\n// lease/backoff state without raw SQL at every call site.\\nexport function queueDetail(db: DatabaseSync): QueueDetailRow[] {\\n  const rows = db\\n    .prepare(\\n      `SELECT work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, status, attempts, last_error,\\n              eligible_at, claimed_at, claim_expires_at, queued_at, payload\\n       FROM work_item ORDER BY rowid`,\\n    )\\n    .all() as unknown as Array<\\n    RawClaimRow & {\\n      status: string;\\n      last_error: string | null;\\n      eligible_at: string | null;\\n      claimed_at: string | null;\\n      claim_expires_at: string | null;\\n    }\\n  >;\\n  return rows.map((row) => {\\n    const item = toClaimedItem(row);\\n    const detail: QueueDetailRow = {\\n      workItemId: item.workItemId,\\n      owner: item.owner,\\n      kind: item.kind,\\n      sourceRef: item.sourceRef,\\n      status: row.status as \\\"queued\\\" | \\\"claimed\\\",\\n      attempts: item.attempts,\\n      queuedAt: item.queuedAt,\\n      sourceVersion: item.sourceVersion,\\n    };\\n    if (row.last_error !== null) detail.lastError = row.last_error;\\n    if (row.eligible_at !== null) detail.eligibleAt = row.eligible_at;\\n    if (row.claimed_at !== null) detail.claimedAt = row.claimed_at;\\n    if (row.claim_expires_at !== null) detail.claimExpiresAt = row.claim_expires_at;\\n    return detail;\\n  });\\n}\\n\\n// Listing in queue order (insertion order within the walk); each owning\\n// domain lists only its own items.\\nexport function listItems(db: DatabaseSync, owner: WorkOwner): WorkItemRecord[] {\\n  const rows = db\\n    .prepare(\\n      `SELECT work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, queued_at\\n       FROM work_item WHERE owner = ? ORDER BY rowid`,\\n    )\\n    .all(owner) as unknown as RawWorkItemRow[];\\n  return rows.map((row) => ({\\n    workItemId: row.work_item_id,\\n    owner: row.owner as WorkOwner,\\n    kind: row.kind as WorkKind,\\n    sourceRef: JSON.parse(row.source_ref) as WorkSourceRef,\\n    status: \\\"queued\\\",\\n    queuedAt: row.queued_at,\\n  }));\\n}\\n\",\"numLines\":550,\"startLine\":1,\"totalLines\":550}}}\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/artifacts/fix/streams/001-fix-result.stdout.log:176:{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01KA1F7NQjreLaAYyXeq8qsA\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"1\\t// SDK-internal scheduler and drain (DD-4): the one component holding\\n2\\t// cross-operation in-memory state. The drain loop lives here per the tech\\n3\\t// design's placement — claim under BEGIN IMMEDIATE, dispatch through the\\n4\\t// handler map with no open transaction (the provider call lives there),\\n5\\t// complete in a second short transaction. Background mode adds per-thread\\n6\\t// single-flight with pending-flag coalescing, post-commit pokes, first-touch\\n7\\t// catch-up (DD-10), and drainSettled (issue 3). The in-memory state is\\n8\\t// advisory only: cross-process safety comes from the durable lease alone, so\\n9\\t// a fresh handle sees identical drain behavior — the queue is the rows.\\n10\\timport { existsSync } from \\\"node:fs\\\";\\n11\\timport { setImmediate as scheduleMacrotask } from \\\"node:timers\\\";\\n12\\timport { DatabaseSync } from \\\"node:sqlite\\\";\\n13\\timport { openThreadDatabase } from \\\"./domains/threads/index.js\\\";\\n14\\timport type {\\n15\\t  HandlerOutcome,\\n16\\t  ResolvedSdkConfig,\\n17\\t  WorkHandler,\\n18\\t} from \\\"./shared/derivation.js\\\";\\n19\\timport { storageFailure, type ErrorResult, type OpResult } from \\\"./shared/errors.js\\\";\\n20\\timport {\\n21\\t  claimNext,\\n22\\t  complete,\\n23\\t  countLiveItems,\\n24\\t  failAttempt,\\n25\\t  failTerminal,\\n26\\t  type ClaimedWorkItem,\\n27\\t  type WorkKind,\\n28\\t  type WorkSourceRef,\\n29\\t} from \\\"./tech-utils/work-queue/index.js\\\";\\n30\\t\\n31\\texport type SchedulerMode = \\\"background\\\" | \\\"manual\\\";\\n32\\t\\n33\\t// Report shape per tech design §Interfaces. `superseded` never appears here:\\n34\\t// the cascade deletes superseded items and reports them on the mutation\\n35\\t// result; a drain never sees them.\\n36\\texport interface DrainReport {\\n37\\t  ran: Array<{\\n38\\t    workItemId: string;\\n39\\t    kind: WorkKind;\\n40\\t    sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n41\\t    disposition: \\\"done\\\" | \\\"failed_terminal\\\" | \\\"stale_discarded\\\";\\n42\\t    attempts: number;\\n43\\t    reason?: string;\\n44\\t  }>;\\n45\\t  stoppedBecause: \\\"empty\\\" | \\\"in_flight\\\" | \\\"waiting\\\" | \\\"max_items\\\";\\n46\\t  waitingUntil?: string; // head's eligible_at when stoppedBecause = \\\"waiting\\\"\\n47\\t  remaining: number; // live items left behind the stop point\\n48\\t}\\n49\\t\\n50\\texport interface DrainDeps {\\n51\\t  lookupHandler: (kind: string) => OpResult<WorkHandler>;\\n52\\t  // Whether any handler is registered at all. Background scheduling is gated\\n53\\t  // on this, fail-closed: with an empty map a background drain could only\\n54\\t  // turn queued rows into failed_terminal — destruction, not processing — so\\n55\\t  // pokes and catch-up stay inert until a handler table is populated\\n56\\t  // (Stories 2–3 in production; explicit registration in tests).\\n57\\t  hasAnyHandler: () => boolean;\\n58\\t  config: ResolvedSdkConfig;\\n59\\t}\\n60\\t\\n61\\tfunction ranEntry(\\n62\\t  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\\n63\\t  disposition: \\\"done\\\" | \\\"failed_terminal\\\" | \\\"stale_discarded\\\",\\n64\\t  attempts: number,\\n65\\t  reason?: string,\\n66\\t): DrainReport[\\\"ran\\\"][number] {\\n67\\t  const entry: DrainReport[\\\"ran\\\"][number] = {\\n68\\t    workItemId: item.workItemId,\\n69\\t    kind: item.kind as WorkKind,\\n70\\t    sourceRef: item.sourceRef,\\n71\\t    disposition,\\n72\\t    attempts,\\n73\\t  };\\n74\\t  if (reason !== undefined) entry.reason = reason;\\n75\\t  return entry;\\n76\\t}\\n77\\t\\n78\\t// The drain loop against an open handle. Claim → dispatch → complete, one\\n79\\t// item at a time, until the head stops it (empty / in_flight / waiting) or\\n80\\t// maxItems is reached. The handler runs with NO open transaction; the only\\n81\\t// exits from a handler failure are failAttempt and the terminal paths — the\\n82\\t// drain never catches an error and records success.\\n83\\texport async function drainOpenDb(\\n84\\t  db: DatabaseSync,\\n85\\t  deps: DrainDeps,\\n86\\t  opts?: { maxItems?: number },\\n87\\t): Promise<DrainReport> {\\n88\\t  const { clock, lease, retry, provider } = deps.config;\\n89\\t  const ran: DrainReport[\\\"ran\\\"] = [];\\n90\\t  let stoppedBecause: DrainReport[\\\"stoppedBecause\\\"];\\n91\\t  let waitingUntil: string | undefined;\\n92\\t\\n93\\t  for (;;) {\\n94\\t    if (opts?.maxItems !== undefined && ran.length >= opts.maxItems) {\\n95\\t      stoppedBecause = \\\"max_items\\\";\\n96\\t      break;\\n97\\t    }\\n98\\t    const claim = claimNext(db, clock().toISOString(), lease.durationMs);\\n99\\t    if (claim.outcome === \\\"empty\\\") {\\n100\\t      stoppedBecause = \\\"empty\\\";\\n101\\t      break;\\n102\\t    }\\n103\\t    if (claim.outcome === \\\"in_flight\\\") {\\n104\\t      stoppedBecause = \\\"in_flight\\\";\\n105\\t      break;\\n106\\t    }\\n107\\t    if (claim.outcome === \\\"waiting\\\") {\\n108\\t      stoppedBecause = \\\"waiting\\\";\\n109\\t      waitingUntil = claim.waitingUntil;\\n110\\t      break;\\n111\\t    }\\n112\\t    const item = claim.item;\\n113\\t\\n114\\t    const lookedUp = deps.lookupHandler(item.kind);\\n115\\t    if (!lookedUp.ok) {\\n116\\t      // Unregistered kind: the normal terminal transaction, not a try/catch\\n117\\t      // skip — failed_terminal with the stable code, form (if resolvable\\n118\\t      // from the payload) lands failed, and the drain continues (AC-1.8).\\n119\\t      failTerminal(db, item, {\\n120\\t        reason: lookedUp.error.code,\\n121\\t        formState: \\\"failed\\\",\\n122\\t        attempts: item.attempts,\\n123\\t        now: clock().toISOString(),\\n124\\t      });\\n125\\t      ran.push(ranEntry(item, \\\"failed_terminal\\\", item.attempts, lookedUp.error.code));\\n126\\t      continue;\\n127\\t    }\\n128\\t\\n129\\t    let outcome: HandlerOutcome;\\n130\\t    try {\\n131\\t      outcome = await lookedUp.value(\\n132\\t        { openDb: () => db, provider, clock, config: deps.config },\\n133\\t        {\\n134\\t          workItemId: item.workItemId,\\n135\\t          kind: item.kind,\\n136\\t          sourceRef: item.sourceRef as unknown as Record<string, string>,\\n137\\t        },\\n138\\t      );\\n139\\t    } catch (cause) {\\n140\\t      // A throwing handler is a bug by the error contract, but the queue\\n141\\t      // must not wedge on it: route it through the normal retry path so it\\n142\\t      // counts attempts and exhausts visibly.\\n143\\t      const detail = cause instanceof Error ? cause.message : String(cause);\\n144\\t      outcome = { ok: false, retryable: true, reason: `handler threw: ${detail}` };\\n145\\t    }\\n146\\t\\n147\\t    if (outcome.ok) {\\n148\\t      const disposition = complete(\\n149\\t        db,\\n150\\t        item,\\n151\\t        outcome.forms ?? [],\\n152\\t        clock().toISOString(),\\n153\\t        outcome.onApplied,\\n154\\t      );\\n155\\t      ran.push(ranEntry(item, disposition, item.attempts));\\n156\\t      continue;\\n157\\t    }\\n158\\t    if (\\\"blocked\\\" in outcome) {\\n159\\t      failTerminal(db, item, {\\n160\\t        reason: outcome.reason,\\n161\\t        formState: \\\"blocked\\\",\\n162\\t        attempts: item.attempts + 1,\\n163\\t        now: clock().toISOString(),\\n164\\t      });\\n165\\t      ran.push(ranEntry(item, \\\"failed_terminal\\\", item.attempts + 1, outcome.reason));\\n166\\t      continue;\\n167\\t    }\\n168\\t    const failed = failAttempt(db, item, {\\n169\\t      reason: outcome.reason,\\n170\\t      retryable: outcome.retryable,\\n171\\t      now: clock().toISOString(),\\n172\\t      retry,\\n173\\t    });\\n174\\t    if (failed.terminal) {\\n175\\t      ran.push(ranEntry(item, \\\"failed_terminal\\\", failed.attempts, outcome.reason));\\n176\\t    }\\n177\\t    // Under budget the item went back to queued (possibly backing off); the\\n178\\t    // next claimNext re-reads the head — a backing-off head ends the drain\\n179\\t    // with \\\"waiting\\\" and gates everything behind it.\\n180\\t  }\\n181\\t\\n182\\t  const report: DrainReport = { ran, stoppedBecause, remaining: countLiveItems(db) };\\n183\\t  if (waitingUntil !== undefined) report.waitingUntil = waitingUntil;\\n184\\t  return report;\\n185\\t}\\n186\\t\\n187\\tfunction threadNotFound(filePath: string): { ok: false; error: ErrorResult } {\\n188\\t  return {\\n189\\t    ok: false,\\n190\\t    error: {\\n191\\t      errorClass: \\\"caller_error\\\",\\n192\\t      code: \\\"thread_not_found\\\",\\n193\\t      reason: `no thread file exists at ${filePath}`,\\n194\\t    },\\n195\\t  };\\n196\\t}\\n197\\t\\n198\\t// The drain operation against a thread file: open (validates + migrates),\\n199\\t// drain, close. Both the SDK's work.drain and the background loop's passes\\n200\\t// run through here — manual and background modes share one drain.\\n201\\texport async function runDrain(\\n202\\t  filePath: string,\\n203\\t  deps: DrainDeps,\\n204\\t  opts?: { maxItems?: number },\\n205\\t): Promise<OpResult<DrainReport>> {\\n206\\t  if (!existsSync(filePath)) return threadNotFound(filePath);\\n207\\t  const opened = openThreadDatabase(filePath);\\n208\\t  if (!opened.ok) return opened;\\n209\\t  const db = opened.value;\\n210\\t  try {\\n211\\t    return { ok: true, value: await drainOpenDb(db, deps, opts) };\\n212\\t  } catch (cause) {\\n213\\t    const detail = cause instanceof Error ? cause.message : String(cause);\\n214\\t    return storageFailure(`drain failed: ${detail}`);\\n215\\t  } finally {\\n216\\t    db.close();\\n217\\t  }\\n218\\t}\\n219\\t\\n220\\tinterface ThreadDrainState {\\n221\\t  filePath: string;\\n222\\t  running: boolean;\\n223\\t  pending: boolean;\\n224\\t  passes: number; // test-only observability (TC-1.2); must not become API\\n225\\t  waiters: Array<() => void>;\\n226\\t}\\n227\\t\\n228\\texport interface Scheduler {\\n229\\t  readonly mode: SchedulerMode;\\n230\\t  // Post-commit nudge that work was queued for a thread (DD-5). Manual mode:\\n231\\t  // no-op by contract. Background mode: starts a drain, or coalesces into\\n232\\t  // the pending flag if one is already running for the thread (AC-1.2).\\n233\\t  poke(threadId: string): void;\\n234\\t  // Thread-file open announcement (DD-10): learns threadId → filePath and,\\n235\\t  // on the first touch of a thread this process lifetime, schedules a\\n236\\t  // catch-up drain if the queue has leftover live work (AC-1.6).\\n237\\t  touch(filePath: string, db: DatabaseSync): void;\\n238\\t  // Resolves when the scheduler has no running or pending drain for the\\n239\\t  // thread (issue 3). Manual mode resolves immediately.\\n240\\t  drainSettled(threadId: string): Promise<void>;\\n241\\t  // Test-only observability for coalescing exactness (TC-1.2): drain passes\\n242\\t  // started for a thread. Named as a test hook on purpose — not API.\\n243\\t  testPassCount(threadId: string): number;\\n244\\t}\\n245\\t\\n246\\tfunction readThreadId(db: DatabaseSync): string | null {\\n247\\t  const row = db.prepare(\\\"SELECT thread_id FROM thread_metadata WHERE id = 1\\\").get() as\\n248\\t    | { thread_id: string }\\n249\\t    | undefined;\\n250\\t  return row?.thread_id ?? null;\\n251\\t}\\n252\\t\\n253\\t// Read a thread file's id without side effects (no migration, no touch) —\\n254\\t// drainSettled must observe scheduler state, never schedule work.\\n255\\texport function peekThreadId(filePath: string): string | null {\\n256\\t  if (!existsSync(filePath)) return null;\\n257\\t  let db: DatabaseSync | undefined;\\n258\\t  try {\\n259\\t    db = new DatabaseSync(filePath, { readOnly: true });\\n260\\t    return readThreadId(db);\\n261\\t  } catch {\\n262\\t    return null;\\n263\\t  } finally {\\n264\\t    db?.close();\\n265\\t  }\\n266\\t}\\n267\\t\\n268\\texport function createScheduler(mode: SchedulerMode, deps: DrainDeps): Scheduler {\\n269\\t  const states = new Map<string, ThreadDrainState>();\\n270\\t  const seen = new Set<string>(); // first-touch catch-up guard, process lifetime\\n271\\t\\n272\\t  function stateFor(threadId: string): ThreadDrainState {\\n273\\t    let st = states.get(threadId);\\n274\\t    if (st === undefined) {\\n275\\t      st = { filePath: \\\"\\\", running: false, pending: false, passes: 0, waiters: [] };\\n276\\t      states.set(threadId, st);\\n277\\t    }\\n278\\t    return st;\\n279\\t  }\\n280\\t\\n281\\t  async function runLoop(st: ThreadDrainState): Promise<void> {\\n282\\t    // Defer past the committing operation's synchronous tail so the drain\\n283\\t    // never contends with the transaction whose commit scheduled it.\\n284\\t    await new Promise((resolve) => scheduleMacrotask(resolve));\\n285\\t    try {\\n286\\t      do {\\n287\\t        st.pending = false;\\n288\\t        st.passes += 1;\\n289\\t        // A failed pass (storage error) has no caller to report to in\\n290\\t        // background mode; the durable rows are untouched and the next poke\\n291\\t        // or touch retries. A pass stopping on \\\"waiting\\\" likewise leaves the\\n292\\t        // backing-off head for the next nudge — the lease and eligibility\\n293\\t        // gates are durable, not timer state.\\n294\\t        await runDrain(st.filePath, deps);\\n295\\t      } while (st.pending);\\n296\\t    } finally {\\n297\\t      st.running = false;\\n298\\t      for (const waiter of st.waiters.splice(0)) waiter();\\n299\\t    }\\n300\\t  }\\n301\\t\\n302\\t  function schedule(threadId: string): void {\\n303\\t    const st = stateFor(threadId);\\n304\\t    if (st.filePath === \\\"\\\") return; // never touched here: no path to drain\\n305\\t    if (st.running) {\\n306\\t      st.pending = true; // burst coalesce: at most one further pass (AC-1.2)\\n307\\t      return;\\n308\\t    }\\n309\\t    st.running = true;\\n310\\t    void runLoop(st);\\n311\\t  }\\n312\\t\\n313\\t  return {\\n314\\t    mode,\\n315\\t    poke(threadId: string): void {\\n316\\t      if (mode !== \\\"background\\\") return;\\n317\\t      if (!deps.hasAnyHandler()) return;\\n318\\t      schedule(threadId);\\n319\\t    },\\n320\\t    touch(filePath: string, db: DatabaseSync): void {\\n321\\t      if (mode !== \\\"background\\\") return;\\n322\\t      const threadId = readThreadId(db);\\n323\\t      if (threadId === null) return;\\n324\\t      const st = stateFor(threadId);\\n325\\t      st.filePath = filePath;\\n326\\t      if (seen.has(threadId)) return;\\n327\\t      seen.add(threadId);\\n328\\t      // Catch-up (DD-10): leftover live work from a previous process runs on\\n329\\t      // first touch. One indexed query on the already-open handle keeps the\\n330\\t      // empty-queue case cheap.\\n331\\t      if (deps.hasAnyHandler() && countLiveItems(db) > 0) schedule(threadId);\\n332\\t    },\\n333\\t    drainSettled(threadId: string): Promise<void> {\\n334\\t      const st = states.get(threadId);\\n335\\t      if (st === undefined || (!st.running && !st.pending)) return Promise.resolve();\\n336\\t      return new Promise((resolve) => st.waiters.push(resolve));\\n337\\t    },\\n338\\t    testPassCount(threadId: string): number {\\n339\\t      return states.get(threadId)?.passes ?? 0;\\n340\\t    },\\n341\\t  };\\n342\\t}\\n343\\t\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"b8d4e4d4-0320-4750-a113-f05b3f0942c7\",\"uuid\":\"ab524e2b-455d-479e-b83b-cac4b3b749a2\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-06-11T08:14:17.692Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/src/scheduler.ts\",\"content\":\"// SDK-internal scheduler and drain (DD-4): the one component holding\\n// cross-operation in-memory state. The drain loop lives here per the tech\\n// design's placement — claim under BEGIN IMMEDIATE, dispatch through the\\n// handler map with no open transaction (the provider call lives there),\\n// complete in a second short transaction. Background mode adds per-thread\\n// single-flight with pending-flag coalescing, post-commit pokes, first-touch\\n// catch-up (DD-10), and drainSettled (issue 3). The in-memory state is\\n// advisory only: cross-process safety comes from the durable lease alone, so\\n// a fresh handle sees identical drain behavior — the queue is the rows.\\nimport { existsSync } from \\\"node:fs\\\";\\nimport { setImmediate as scheduleMacrotask } from \\\"node:timers\\\";\\nimport { DatabaseSync } from \\\"node:sqlite\\\";\\nimport { openThreadDatabase } from \\\"./domains/threads/index.js\\\";\\nimport type {\\n  HandlerOutcome,\\n  ResolvedSdkConfig,\\n  WorkHandler,\\n} from \\\"./shared/derivation.js\\\";\\nimport { storageFailure, type ErrorResult, type OpResult } from \\\"./shared/errors.js\\\";\\nimport {\\n  claimNext,\\n  complete,\\n  countLiveItems,\\n  failAttempt,\\n  failTerminal,\\n  type ClaimedWorkItem,\\n  type WorkKind,\\n  type WorkSourceRef,\\n} from \\\"./tech-utils/work-queue/index.js\\\";\\n\\nexport type SchedulerMode = \\\"background\\\" | \\\"manual\\\";\\n\\n// Report shape per tech design §Interfaces. `superseded` never appears here:\\n// the cascade deletes superseded items and reports them on the mutation\\n// result; a drain never sees them.\\nexport interface DrainReport {\\n  ran: Array<{\\n    workItemId: string;\\n    kind: WorkKind;\\n    sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n    disposition: \\\"done\\\" | \\\"failed_terminal\\\" | \\\"stale_discarded\\\";\\n    attempts: number;\\n    reason?: string;\\n  }>;\\n  stoppedBecause: \\\"empty\\\" | \\\"in_flight\\\" | \\\"waiting\\\" | \\\"max_items\\\";\\n  waitingUntil?: string; // head's eligible_at when stoppedBecause = \\\"waiting\\\"\\n  remaining: number; // live items left behind the stop point\\n}\\n\\nexport interface DrainDeps {\\n  lookupHandler: (kind: string) => OpResult<WorkHandler>;\\n  // Whether any handler is registered at all. Background scheduling is gated\\n  // on this, fail-closed: with an empty map a background drain could only\\n  // turn queued rows into failed_terminal — destruction, not processing — so\\n  // pokes and catch-up stay inert until a handler table is populated\\n  // (Stories 2–3 in production; explicit registration in tests).\\n  hasAnyHandler: () => boolean;\\n  config: ResolvedSdkConfig;\\n}\\n\\nfunction ranEntry(\\n  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\\n  disposition: \\\"done\\\" | \\\"failed_terminal\\\" | \\\"stale_discarded\\\",\\n  attempts: number,\\n  reason?: string,\\n): DrainReport[\\\"ran\\\"][number] {\\n  const entry: DrainReport[\\\"ran\\\"][number] = {\\n    workItemId: item.workItemId,\\n    kind: item.kind as WorkKind,\\n    sourceRef: item.sourceRef,\\n    disposition,\\n    attempts,\\n  };\\n  if (reason !== undefined) entry.reason = reason;\\n  return entry;\\n}\\n\\n// The drain loop against an open handle. Claim → dispatch → complete, one\\n// item at a time, until the head stops it (empty / in_flight / waiting) or\\n// maxItems is reached. The handler runs with NO open transaction; the only\\n// exits from a handler failure are failAttempt and the terminal paths — the\\n// drain never catches an error and records success.\\nexport async function drainOpenDb(\\n  db: DatabaseSync,\\n  deps: DrainDeps,\\n  opts?: { maxItems?: number },\\n): Promise<DrainReport> {\\n  const { clock, lease, retry, provider } = deps.config;\\n  const ran: DrainReport[\\\"ran\\\"] = [];\\n  let stoppedBecause: DrainReport[\\\"stoppedBecause\\\"];\\n  let waitingUntil: string | undefined;\\n\\n  for (;;) {\\n    if (opts?.maxItems !== undefined && ran.length >= opts.maxItems) {\\n      stoppedBecause = \\\"max_items\\\";\\n      break;\\n    }\\n    const claim = claimNext(db, clock().toISOString(), lease.durationMs);\\n    if (claim.outcome === \\\"empty\\\") {\\n      stoppedBecause = \\\"empty\\\";\\n      break;\\n    }\\n    if (claim.outcome === \\\"in_flight\\\") {\\n      stoppedBecause = \\\"in_flight\\\";\\n      break;\\n    }\\n    if (claim.outcome === \\\"waiting\\\") {\\n      stoppedBecause = \\\"waiting\\\";\\n      waitingUntil = claim.waitingUntil;\\n      break;\\n    }\\n    const item = claim.item;\\n\\n    const lookedUp = deps.lookupHandler(item.kind);\\n    if (!lookedUp.ok) {\\n      // Unregistered kind: the normal terminal transaction, not a try/catch\\n      // skip — failed_terminal with the stable code, form (if resolvable\\n      // from the payload) lands failed, and the drain continues (AC-1.8).\\n      failTerminal(db, item, {\\n        reason: lookedUp.error.code,\\n        formState: \\\"failed\\\",\\n        attempts: item.attempts,\\n        now: clock().toISOString(),\\n      });\\n      ran.push(ranEntry(item, \\\"failed_terminal\\\", item.attempts, lookedUp.error.code));\\n      continue;\\n    }\\n\\n    let outcome: HandlerOutcome;\\n    try {\\n      outcome = await lookedUp.value(\\n        { openDb: () => db, provider, clock, config: deps.config },\\n        {\\n          workItemId: item.workItemId,\\n          kind: item.kind,\\n          sourceRef: item.sourceRef as unknown as Record<string, string>,\\n        },\\n      );\\n    } catch (cause) {\\n      // A throwing handler is a bug by the error contract, but the queue\\n      // must not wedge on it: route it through the normal retry path so it\\n      // counts attempts and exhausts visibly.\\n      const detail = cause instanceof Error ? cause.message : String(cause);\\n      outcome = { ok: false, retryable: true, reason: `handler threw: ${detail}` };\\n    }\\n\\n    if (outcome.ok) {\\n      const disposition = complete(\\n        db,\\n        item,\\n        outcome.forms ?? [],\\n        clock().toISOString(),\\n        outcome.onApplied,\\n      );\\n      ran.push(ranEntry(item, disposition, item.attempts));\\n      continue;\\n    }\\n    if (\\\"blocked\\\" in outcome) {\\n      failTerminal(db, item, {\\n        reason: outcome.reason,\\n        formState: \\\"blocked\\\",\\n        attempts: item.attempts + 1,\\n        now: clock().toISOString(),\\n      });\\n      ran.push(ranEntry(item, \\\"failed_terminal\\\", item.attempts + 1, outcome.reason));\\n      continue;\\n    }\\n    const failed = failAttempt(db, item, {\\n      reason: outcome.reason,\\n      retryable: outcome.retryable,\\n      now: clock().toISOString(),\\n      retry,\\n    });\\n    if (failed.terminal) {\\n      ran.push(ranEntry(item, \\\"failed_terminal\\\", failed.attempts, outcome.reason));\\n    }\\n    // Under budget the item went back to queued (possibly backing off); the\\n    // next claimNext re-reads the head — a backing-off head ends the drain\\n    // with \\\"waiting\\\" and gates everything behind it.\\n  }\\n\\n  const report: DrainReport = { ran, stoppedBecause, remaining: countLiveItems(db) };\\n  if (waitingUntil !== undefined) report.waitingUntil = waitingUntil;\\n  return report;\\n}\\n\\nfunction threadNotFound(filePath: string): { ok: false; error: ErrorResult } {\\n  return {\\n    ok: false,\\n    error: {\\n      errorClass: \\\"caller_error\\\",\\n      code: \\\"thread_not_found\\\",\\n      reason: `no thread file exists at ${filePath}`,\\n    },\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n// The drain operation against a thread file: open (validates + migrates),\\n// drain, close. Both the SDK's work.drain and the background loop's passes\\n// run through here — manual and background modes share one drain.\\nexport async function runDrain(\\n  filePath: string,\\n  deps: DrainDeps,\\n  opts?: { maxItems?: number },\\n): Promise<OpResult<DrainReport>> {\\n  if (!existsSync(filePath)) return threadNotFound(filePath);\\n  const opened = openThreadDatabase(filePath);\\n  if (!opened.ok) return opened;\\n  const db = opened.value;\\n  try {\\n    return { ok: true, value: await drainOpenDb(db, deps, opts) };\\n  } catch (cause) {\\n    const detail = cause instanceof Error ? cause.message : String(cause);\\n    return storageFailure(`drain failed: ${detail}`);\\n  } finally {\\n    db.close();\\n  }\\n}\\n\\ninterface ThreadDrainState {\\n  filePath: string;\\n  running: boolean;\\n  pending: boolean;\\n  passes: number; // test-only observability (TC-1.2); must not become API\\n  waiters: Array<() => void>;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface Scheduler {\\n  readonly mode: SchedulerMode;\\n  // Post-commit nudge that work was queued for a thread (DD-5). Manual mode:\\n  // no-op by contract. Background mode: starts a drain, or coalesces into\\n  // the pending flag if one is already running for the thread (AC-1.2).\\n  poke(threadId: string): void;\\n  // Thread-file open announcement (DD-10): learns threadId → filePath and,\\n  // on the first touch of a thread this process lifetime, schedules a\\n  // catch-up drain if the queue has leftover live work (AC-1.6).\\n  touch(filePath: string, db: DatabaseSync): void;\\n  // Resolves when the scheduler has no running or pending drain for the\\n  // thread (issue 3). Manual mode resolves immediately.\\n  drainSettled(threadId: string): Promise<void>;\\n  // Test-only observability for coalescing exactness (TC-1.2): drain passes\\n  // started for a thread. Named as a test hook on purpose — not API.\\n  testPassCount(threadId: string): number;\\n}\\n\\nfunction readThreadId(db: DatabaseSync): string | null {\\n  const row = db.prepare(\\\"SELECT thread_id FROM thread_metadata WHERE id = 1\\\").get() as\\n    | { thread_id: string }\\n    | undefined;\\n  return row?.thread_id ?? null;\\n}\\n\\n// Read a thread file's id without side effects (no migration, no touch) —\\n// drainSettled must observe scheduler state, never schedule work.\\nexport function peekThreadId(filePath: string): string | null {\\n  if (!existsSync(filePath)) return null;\\n  let db: DatabaseSync | undefined;\\n  try {\\n    db = new DatabaseSync(filePath, { readOnly: true });\\n    return readThreadId(db);\\n  } catch {\\n    return null;\\n  } finally {\\n    db?.close();\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nexport function createScheduler(mode: SchedulerMode, deps: DrainDeps): Scheduler {\\n  const states = new Map<string, ThreadDrainState>();\\n  const seen = new Set<string>(); // first-touch catch-up guard, process lifetime\\n\\n  function stateFor(threadId: string): ThreadDrainState {\\n    let st = states.get(threadId);\\n    if (st === undefined) {\\n      st = { filePath: \\\"\\\", running: false, pending: false, passes: 0, waiters: [] };\\n      states.set(threadId, st);\\n    }\\n    return st;\\n  }\\n\\n  async function runLoop(st: ThreadDrainState): Promise<void> {\\n    // Defer past the committing operation's synchronous tail so the drain\\n    // never contends with the transaction whose commit scheduled it.\\n    await new Promise((resolve) => scheduleMacrotask(resolve));\\n    try {\\n      do {\\n        st.pending = false;\\n        st.passes += 1;\\n        // A failed pass (storage error) has no caller to report to in\\n        // background mode; the durable rows are untouched and the next poke\\n        // or touch retries. A pass stopping on \\\"waiting\\\" likewise leaves the\\n        // backing-off head for the next nudge — the lease and eligibility\\n        // gates are durable, not timer state.\\n        await runDrain(st.filePath, deps);\\n      } while (st.pending);\\n    } finally {\\n      st.running = false;\\n      for (const waiter of st.waiters.splice(0)) waiter();\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  function schedule(threadId: string): void {\\n    const st = stateFor(threadId);\\n    if (st.filePath === \\\"\\\") return; // never touched here: no path to drain\\n    if (st.running) {\\n      st.pending = true; // burst coalesce: at most one further pass (AC-1.2)\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    st.running = true;\\n    void runLoop(st);\\n  }\\n\\n  return {\\n    mode,\\n    poke(threadId: string): void {\\n      if (mode !== \\\"background\\\") return;\\n      if (!deps.hasAnyHandler()) return;\\n      schedule(threadId);\\n    },\\n    touch(filePath: string, db: DatabaseSync): void {\\n      if (mode !== \\\"background\\\") return;\\n      const threadId = readThreadId(db);\\n      if (threadId === null) return;\\n      const st = stateFor(threadId);\\n      st.filePath = filePath;\\n      if (seen.has(threadId)) return;\\n      seen.add(threadId);\\n      // Catch-up (DD-10): leftover live work from a previous process runs on\\n      // first touch. One indexed query on the already-open handle keeps the\\n      // empty-queue case cheap.\\n      if (deps.hasAnyHandler() && countLiveItems(db) > 0) schedule(threadId);\\n    },\\n    drainSettled(threadId: string): Promise<void> {\\n      const st = states.get(threadId);\\n      if (st === undefined || (!st.running && !st.pending)) return Promise.resolve();\\n      return new Promise((resolve) => st.waiters.push(resolve));\\n    },\\n    testPassCount(threadId: string): number {\\n      return states.get(threadId)?.passes ?? 0;\\n    },\\n  };\\n}\\n\",\"numLines\":343,\"startLine\":1,\"totalLines\":343}}}\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/artifacts/fix/streams/001-fix-result.stdout.log:1070:{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"content_block_delta\",\"index\":1,\"delta\":{\"type\":\"input_json_delta\",\"partial_json\":\"andlers\\\\\\\\|export function\\\\\\\\|complete(\\\\\\\" test/fixtures/work-handlers.ts; echo \\\\\\\"=== complete()\"}},\"session_id\":\"b8d4e4d4-0320-4750-a113-f05b3f0942c7\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"22242163-c04d-4710-9756-23a821716287\"}\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/artifacts/fix/streams/001-fix-result.stdout.log:1071:{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"content_block_delta\",\"index\":1,\"delta\":{\"type\":\"input_json_delta\",\"partial_json\":\" callers in src+test ===\\\\\\\"; grep -rn \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\bcomplete(\\\\\\\" src test --include='*.ts' | grep\"}},\"session_id\":\"b8d4e4d4-0320-4750-a113-f05b3f0942c7\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"05864036-e133-4743-ac79-fe08928a7e21\"}\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/artifacts/fix/streams/001-fix-result.stdout.log:1073:{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"content_block_delta\",\"index\":1,\"delta\":{\"type\":\"input_json_delta\",\"partial_json\":\"\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Inspect fixtures and complete() callers\"}},\"session_id\":\"b8d4e4d4-0320-4750-a113-f05b3f0942c7\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"7dea6fb7-ba55-49fe-976b-792ea474f784\"}\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/artifacts/fix/streams/001-fix-result.stdout.log:1075:{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-4-8\",\"id\":\"msg_015L3KXsuduimu6tfcAHZeFR\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01HMssUNxtJGj2z92DAAWGJu\",\"name\":\"Bash\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"echo \\\"=== fixtures dir ===\\\"; ls test/fixtures/; echo \\\"=== registerTestWorkHandlers def ===\\\"; grep -rn \\\"registerTestWorkHandlers\\\\|export function\\\\|complete(\\\" test/fixtures/work-handlers.ts; echo \\\"=== complete() callers in src+test ===\\\"; grep -rn \\\"\\\\bcomplete(\\\" src test --include='*.ts' | grep -v \\\"// \\\"\",\"description\":\"Inspect fixtures and complete() callers\"},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":5508,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":138321,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":5508},\"output_tokens\":2,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"},\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"b8d4e4d4-0320-4750-a113-f05b3f0942c7\",\"uuid\":\"d28ff675-495d-404e-b806-e23bd50b87f9\",\"request_id\":\"req_011Cbw9Y2YGE81XbVyxpWPtC\"}\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/artifacts/fix/streams/001-fix-result.stdout.log:1078:{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01HMssUNxtJGj2z92DAAWGJu\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"=== fixtures dir ===\\ncorrupt.ts\\ndrain-runner.ts\\nindex.ts\\nintake-seam.ts\\nlegacy.ts\\nprovider-double.ts\\nthreads.ts\\nwork-handlers.ts\\n=== registerTestWorkHandlers def ===\\ntest/fixtures/work-handlers.ts:86:export function testWorkHandlers(\\ntest/fixtures/work-handlers.ts:120:export function registerTestWorkHandlers(\\n=== complete() callers in src+test ===\\nsrc/scheduler.ts:148:      const disposition = complete(\\nsrc/tech-utils/work-queue/index.ts:293:export function complete(\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"b8d4e4d4-0320-4750-a113-f05b3f0942c7\",\"uuid\":\"e0024e76-bf62-47f4-ad17-3edd568de726\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-06-11T08:21:44.405Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"stdout\":\"=== fixtures dir ===\\ncorrupt.ts\\ndrain-runner.ts\\nindex.ts\\nintake-seam.ts\\nlegacy.ts\\nprovider-double.ts\\nthreads.ts\\nwork-handlers.ts\\n=== registerTestWorkHandlers def ===\\ntest/fixtures/work-handlers.ts:86:export function testWorkHandlers(\\ntest/fixtures/work-handlers.ts:120:export function registerTestWorkHandlers(\\n=== complete() callers in src+test ===\\nsrc/scheduler.ts:148:      const disposition = complete(\\nsrc/tech-utils/work-queue/index.ts:293:export function complete(\",\"stderr\":\"\",\"interrupted\":false,\"isImage\":false,\"noOutputExpected\":false}}\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/artifacts/05-message-edit-cascade/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:93:{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_012apGwaeYfprrPsyVgnt2Pw\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"1\\t// Durable work-item mechanics: recording, ordered listing, and the enqueue\\n2\\t// wrapper that makes \\\"queueing is what schedules processing\\\" structural\\n3\\t// (DD-5). The util is domain-blind by the tech-arch capability rule — it\\n4\\t// knows the item vocabulary (owner, kind, sourceRef) but nothing about what\\n5\\t// a turn or a summary is; which kinds queue for which sources, and which\\n6\\t// derived forms a kind produces, is the owning domains' business (callers\\n7\\t// pass the form targets in). Writes run on the caller's handle inside the\\n8\\t// ambient transaction, so items commit (or roll back) with the batch. No\\n9\\t// public SDK surface: read-back reaches here only through the owning\\n10\\t// domains' listQueuedWork.\\n11\\timport type { DatabaseSync } from \\\"node:sqlite\\\";\\n12\\timport {\\n13\\t  createCommitHooks,\\n14\\t  fireSchedulerPoke,\\n15\\t  type OperationContext,\\n16\\t} from \\\"../../shared/context.js\\\";\\n17\\timport type {\\n18\\t  CompletionTx,\\n19\\t  FormKind,\\n20\\t  HandlerFormWrite,\\n21\\t  SubjectKind,\\n22\\t} from \\\"../../shared/derivation.js\\\";\\n23\\t\\n24\\texport type WorkOwner = \\\"messages\\\" | \\\"turns\\\";\\n25\\texport type WorkKind =\\n26\\t  | \\\"prompt_smoothing\\\"\\n27\\t  | \\\"tool_call_summary\\\"\\n28\\t  | \\\"tool_result_summary\\\"\\n29\\t  | \\\"turn_derivation\\\"\\n30\\t  | \\\"chunk_summary_detailed\\\"\\n31\\t  | \\\"chunk_summary_brief\\\";\\n32\\texport type WorkSourceRef = { messageId: string } | { turnId: string } | { chunkId: string };\\n33\\t\\n34\\t// The work-kind registry: owner and sourceRef semantics per the epic's Work\\n35\\t// Item contract. Mechanical metadata only — what a kind *means* stays with\\n36\\t// the owning domain's handler (registered at SDK construction, DD-6).\\n37\\texport const WORK_KIND_REGISTRY: Readonly<\\n38\\t  Record<WorkKind, { owner: WorkOwner; sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" | \\\"turnId\\\" | \\\"chunkId\\\" }>\\n39\\t> = {\\n40\\t  prompt_smoothing: { owner: \\\"messages\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" },\\n41\\t  tool_call_summary: { owner: \\\"messages\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" },\\n42\\t  tool_result_summary: { owner: \\\"messages\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" },\\n43\\t  turn_derivation: { owner: \\\"turns\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"turnId\\\" },\\n44\\t  chunk_summary_detailed: { owner: \\\"turns\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"chunkId\\\" },\\n45\\t  chunk_summary_brief: { owner: \\\"turns\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"chunkId\\\" },\\n46\\t};\\n47\\t\\n48\\texport interface WorkItemRecord {\\n49\\t  workItemId: string;\\n50\\t  owner: WorkOwner;\\n51\\t  kind: WorkKind;\\n52\\t  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n53\\t  status: \\\"queued\\\"; // the only status written before a drain claims (Epic 02 Story 1)\\n54\\t  queuedAt: string;\\n55\\t}\\n56\\t\\n57\\t// The derived forms an enqueue is scheduling work toward; the owning domain\\n58\\t// names them (the util stays meaning-blind). Stored in the row's payload so\\n59\\t// the drain's terminal paths (retry exhaustion, blocked source) can land the\\n60\\t// form's failed/blocked state without asking any domain what a kind means.\\n61\\texport interface EnqueueFormTarget {\\n62\\t  subjectKind: SubjectKind;\\n63\\t  subjectId: string;\\n64\\t  form: FormKind;\\n65\\t}\\n66\\t\\n67\\texport interface WorkItemInput {\\n68\\t  owner: WorkOwner;\\n69\\t  kind: WorkKind;\\n70\\t  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n71\\t  sourceVersion?: number; // defaults to 1 — first version of a fresh source\\n72\\t  forms?: readonly EnqueueFormTarget[]; // carried in payload for the drain's terminal paths\\n73\\t}\\n74\\t\\n75\\tfunction sourceIdOf(sourceRef: WorkSourceRef): string {\\n76\\t  if (\\\"messageId\\\" in sourceRef) return sourceRef.messageId;\\n77\\t  if (\\\"turnId\\\" in sourceRef) return sourceRef.turnId;\\n78\\t  return sourceRef.chunkId;\\n79\\t}\\n80\\t\\n81\\t// Deterministic id scoped to the source version (DD-1/DD-3): re-queueing the\\n82\\t// same kind for the same source *at the same version* is the same id —\\n83\\t// natural idempotency for the repair path — while a post-mutation\\n84\\t// replacement (next version) never collides with an in-flight pre-mutation\\n85\\t// item.\\n86\\texport function recordItem(\\n87\\t  db: DatabaseSync,\\n88\\t  input: WorkItemInput,\\n89\\t  queuedAt: string,\\n90\\t): WorkItemRecord {\\n91\\t  const sourceVersion = input.sourceVersion ?? 1;\\n92\\t  const workItemId = `w-${sourceIdOf(input.sourceRef)}-${input.kind}-v${sourceVersion}`;\\n93\\t  db.prepare(\\n94\\t    `INSERT INTO work_item (work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, status, queued_at, payload)\\n95\\t     VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, 'queued', ?, ?)`,\\n96\\t  ).run(\\n97\\t    workItemId,\\n98\\t    input.owner,\\n99\\t    input.kind,\\n100\\t    JSON.stringify(input.sourceRef),\\n101\\t    queuedAt,\\n102\\t    JSON.stringify({ sourceVersion, forms: input.forms ?? [] }),\\n103\\t  );\\n104\\t  return {\\n105\\t    workItemId,\\n106\\t    owner: input.owner,\\n107\\t    kind: input.kind,\\n108\\t    sourceRef: input.sourceRef,\\n109\\t    status: \\\"queued\\\",\\n110\\t    queuedAt,\\n111\\t  };\\n112\\t}\\n113\\t\\n114\\texport interface EnqueueInput extends WorkItemInput {\\n115\\t  forms: readonly EnqueueFormTarget[];\\n116\\t}\\n117\\t\\n118\\t// The one way work is scheduled (DD-5): the work row, the form's `pending`\\n119\\t// state row, and the scheduler poke all ride the ambient transaction — they\\n120\\t// commit together or vanish together. Enqueue is the *only* place a\\n121\\t// derived_form row is created (completion is UPDATE-only); re-enqueueing\\n122\\t// resets an existing row to pending at the enqueued source version.\\n123\\texport function enqueue(ctx: OperationContext, input: EnqueueInput): WorkItemRecord {\\n124\\t  const item = recordItem(ctx.db, input, ctx.clock().toISOString());\\n125\\t  const sourceVersion = input.sourceVersion ?? 1;\\n126\\t  const upsert = ctx.db.prepare(\\n127\\t    `INSERT INTO derived_form (subject_kind, subject_id, form, state, source_version)\\n128\\t     VALUES (?, ?, ?, 'pending', ?)\\n129\\t     ON CONFLICT (subject_kind, subject_id, form) DO UPDATE SET\\n130\\t       state = 'pending', content = NULL, reason = NULL, metadata = NULL,\\n131\\t       gaps = NULL, derived_at = NULL, source_version = excluded.source_version`,\\n132\\t  );\\n133\\t  for (const target of input.forms) {\\n134\\t    upsert.run(target.subjectKind, target.subjectId, target.form, sourceVersion);\\n135\\t  }\\n136\\t  ctx.onCommit(() => fireSchedulerPoke(ctx.threadId));\\n137\\t  return item;\\n138\\t}\\n139\\t\\n140\\tinterface RawWorkItemRow {\\n141\\t  work_item_id: string;\\n142\\t  owner: string;\\n143\\t  kind: string;\\n144\\t  source_ref: string;\\n145\\t  queued_at: string;\\n146\\t}\\n147\\t\\n148\\t// ── claim mechanics (Epic 02 Story 1, tech design §Mechanics) ─────\\n149\\t// All pure SQL against the caller's open handle, domain-blind. Claim,\\n150\\t// complete, and the terminal half of failAttempt each own one short\\n151\\t// BEGIN IMMEDIATE; the handler in between runs with no open transaction.\\n152\\t\\n153\\t// The claimed row as the drain holds it: mechanical fields plus the payload's\\n154\\t// source version and form targets. `kind` is a plain string here — a raw row\\n155\\t// with an unregistered kind must still be claimable so the drain can land it\\n156\\t// failed_terminal instead of skipping it (AC-1.8).\\n157\\texport interface ClaimedWorkItem {\\n158\\t  workItemId: string;\\n159\\t  owner: WorkOwner;\\n160\\t  kind: string;\\n161\\t  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n162\\t  attempts: number;\\n163\\t  queuedAt: string;\\n164\\t  sourceVersion: number;\\n165\\t  forms: EnqueueFormTarget[];\\n166\\t}\\n167\\t\\n168\\texport type ClaimOutcome =\\n169\\t  | { outcome: \\\"claimed\\\"; item: ClaimedWorkItem }\\n170\\t  | { outcome: \\\"empty\\\" }\\n171\\t  | { outcome: \\\"in_flight\\\"; claimExpiresAt: string }\\n172\\t  | { outcome: \\\"waiting\\\"; waitingUntil: string };\\n173\\t\\n174\\tinterface RawClaimRow {\\n175\\t  work_item_id: string;\\n176\\t  owner: string;\\n177\\t  kind: string;\\n178\\t  source_ref: string;\\n179\\t  attempts: number | bigint;\\n180\\t  queued_at: string;\\n181\\t  payload: string | null;\\n182\\t}\\n183\\t\\n184\\t// Form targets for pre-v5 rows, whose payload is NULL (Epic 01 wrote no\\n185\\t// payload column). Exactly the F-02 backfill's kind→form mapping (migration\\n186\\t// v5, shared/storage.ts): the backfill created pending derived_form rows at\\n187\\t// source version 1 for these kinds, and a terminal failure must find them —\\n188\\t// otherwise exhaustion would delete the work row and strand the backfilled\\n189\\t// form as pending forever, breaking AC-1.9/DD-1 for upgraded threads.\\n190\\tconst LEGACY_KIND_FORMS: Partial<\\n191\\t  Record<string, ReadonlyArray<{ subjectKind: SubjectKind; form: FormKind }>>\\n192\\t> = {\\n193\\t  prompt_smoothing: [{ subjectKind: \\\"message\\\", form: \\\"smoothed_prompt\\\" }],\\n194\\t  tool_result_summary: [{ subjectKind: \\\"message\\\", form: \\\"tool_result_summary\\\" }],\\n195\\t  turn_derivation: [\\n196\\t    { subjectKind: \\\"turn\\\", form: \\\"turn_rendering\\\" },\\n197\\t    { subjectKind: \\\"turn\\\", form: \\\"lower_band_projection\\\" },\\n198\\t  ],\\n199\\t};\\n200\\t\\n201\\tfunction legacyFormTargets(kind: string, sourceRef: WorkSourceRef): EnqueueFormTarget[] {\\n202\\t  const mapped = LEGACY_KIND_FORMS[kind];\\n203\\t  if (mapped === undefined) return [];\\n204\\t  const subjectId = sourceIdOf(sourceRef);\\n205\\t  return mapped.map((target) => ({ ...target, subjectId }));\\n206\\t}\\n207\\t\\n208\\tfunction toClaimedItem(row: RawClaimRow): ClaimedWorkItem {\\n209\\t  const sourceRef = JSON.parse(row.source_ref) as WorkSourceRef;\\n210\\t  const payload =\\n211\\t    row.payload === null\\n212\\t      ? // Pre-v5 row: version 1 (matching the backfill) and form targets\\n213\\t        // reconstructed from the backfill's own mapping.\\n214\\t        { sourceVersion: 1, forms: legacyFormTargets(row.kind, sourceRef) }\\n215\\t      : (JSON.parse(row.payload) as { sourceVersion?: number; forms?: EnqueueFormTarget[] });\\n216\\t  return {\\n217\\t    workItemId: row.work_item_id,\\n218\\t    owner: row.owner as WorkOwner,\\n219\\t    kind: row.kind,\\n220\\t    sourceRef,\\n221\\t    attempts: Number(row.attempts),\\n222\\t    queuedAt: row.queued_at,\\n223\\t    sourceVersion: payload.sourceVersion ?? 1,\\n224\\t    forms: payload.forms ?? [],\\n225\\t  };\\n226\\t}\\n227\\t\\n228\\t// Head-first, never skip-ahead: the claim decision is made against the oldest\\n229\\t// live row only — a later eligible row is never considered while an older\\n230\\t// live row exists. One atomic UPDATE under BEGIN IMMEDIATE (no read-then-\\n231\\t// write split: the race window is the bug class). The CASE increments\\n232\\t// `attempts` only when reclaiming an expired claimed row — a reclaim means a\\n233\\t// run died or hung, and the increment makes the crash visible in the report\\n234\\t// and counts it against the retry budget; normal claims never touch it.\\n235\\texport function claimNext(db: DatabaseSync, now: string, leaseDurationMs: number): ClaimOutcome {\\n236\\t  const expiresAt = new Date(Date.parse(now) + leaseDurationMs).toISOString();\\n237\\t  db.exec(\\\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\\\");\\n238\\t  try {\\n239\\t    const claimed = db\\n240\\t      .prepare(\\n241\\t        `UPDATE work_item SET status = 'claimed', claimed_at = ?, claim_expires_at = ?,\\n242\\t           attempts = attempts + CASE WHEN status = 'claimed' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END\\n243\\t         WHERE work_item_id = (\\n244\\t           SELECT work_item_id FROM (\\n245\\t             SELECT work_item_id, status, eligible_at, claim_expires_at\\n246\\t             FROM work_item WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed')\\n247\\t             ORDER BY rowid LIMIT 1\\n248\\t           )\\n249\\t           WHERE (status = 'queued' AND (eligible_at IS NULL OR eligible_at <= ?))\\n250\\t              OR (status = 'claimed' AND claim_expires_at <= ?)\\n251\\t         )\\n252\\t         RETURNING work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, attempts, queued_at, payload`,\\n253\\t      )\\n254\\t      .get(now, expiresAt, now, now) as unknown as RawClaimRow | undefined;\\n255\\t    if (claimed !== undefined) {\\n256\\t      db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n257\\t      return { outcome: \\\"claimed\\\", item: toClaimedItem(claimed) };\\n258\\t    }\\n259\\t    // No row claimable: re-read the head inside the same transaction to\\n260\\t    // classify the stop. A claimed head with a live lease gates the queue\\n261\\t    // (in_flight, AC-1.4); a queued head still backing off gates it too\\n262\\t    // (waiting) — strict ordering, never reordered around the head.\\n263\\t    const head = db\\n264\\t      .prepare(\\n265\\t        `SELECT status, eligible_at, claim_expires_at\\n266\\t         FROM work_item WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed')\\n267\\t         ORDER BY rowid LIMIT 1`,\\n268\\t      )\\n269\\t      .get() as unknown as\\n270\\t      | { status: string; eligible_at: string | null; claim_expires_at: string | null }\\n271\\t      | undefined;\\n272\\t    db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n273\\t    if (head === undefined) return { outcome: \\\"empty\\\" };\\n274\\t    if (head.status === \\\"claimed\\\") {\\n275\\t      return { outcome: \\\"in_flight\\\", claimExpiresAt: head.claim_expires_at ?? \\\"\\\" };\\n276\\t    }\\n277\\t    return { outcome: \\\"waiting\\\", waitingUntil: head.eligible_at ?? \\\"\\\" };\\n278\\t  } catch (cause) {\\n279\\t    db.exec(\\\"ROLLBACK;\\\");\\n280\\t    throw cause;\\n281\\t  }\\n282\\t}\\n283\\t\\n284\\t// Completion: the version-checked form writes and the item row's deletion in\\n285\\t// one short transaction (DD-1: terminal transition deletes the row; the\\n286\\t// disposition is reported in-memory, never stored). UPDATE-only, never\\n287\\t// upsert — the pending row exists from enqueue; a write that finds no row at\\n288\\t// the item's source version is discarded as stale (DD-3 truth table). The\\n289\\t// optional onApplied hook (Story 3) runs inside this same transaction, after\\n290\\t// the form writes and only when they landed — chunk placement and the\\n291\\t// close→summary enqueues ride the completion commit; its onCommit\\n292\\t// registrations (the enqueue pokes) flush after COMMIT and drop on rollback.\\n293\\texport function complete(\\n294\\t  db: DatabaseSync,\\n295\\t  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\\n296\\t  writes: readonly HandlerFormWrite[],\\n297\\t  derivedAt: string,\\n298\\t  onApplied?: (tx: CompletionTx) => void,\\n299\\t): \\\"done\\\" | \\\"stale_discarded\\\" {\\n300\\t  const hooks = createCommitHooks();\\n301\\t  db.exec(\\\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\\\");\\n302\\t  try {\\n303\\t    let hits = 0;\\n304\\t    const update = db.prepare(\\n305\\t      `UPDATE derived_form\\n306\\t       SET state = 'ready', content = ?, reason = NULL, metadata = ?, gaps = ?, derived_at = ?\\n307\\t       WHERE subject_kind = ? AND subject_id = ? AND form = ? AND source_version = ?`,\\n308\\t    );\\n309\\t    for (const write of writes) {\\n310\\t      const changed = update.run(\\n311\\t        write.content,\\n312\\t        write.metadata === undefined ? null : JSON.stringify(write.metadata),\\n313\\t        write.gaps === undefined ? null : JSON.stringify(write.gaps),\\n314\\t        derivedAt,\\n315\\t        write.subjectKind,\\n316\\t        write.subjectId,\\n317\\t        write.form,\\n318\\t        item.sourceVersion,\\n319\\t      );\\n320\\t      hits += Number(changed.changes);\\n321\\t    }\\n322\\t    const stale = writes.length > 0 && hits === 0;\\n323\\t    if (!stale && onApplied !== undefined) {\\n324\\t      onApplied({ db, onCommit: hooks.register });\\n325\\t    }\\n326\\t    db.prepare(`DELETE FROM work_item WHERE work_item_id = ?`).run(item.workItemId);\\n327\\t    db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n328\\t    hooks.flush();\\n329\\t    return stale ? \\\"stale_discarded\\\" : \\\"done\\\";\\n330\\t  } catch (cause) {\\n331\\t    db.exec(\\\"ROLLBACK;\\\");\\n332\\t    throw cause;\\n333\\t  }\\n334\\t}\\n335\\t\\n336\\texport interface RetryPolicy {\\n337\\t  budget: number;\\n338\\t  backoffBaseMs: number;\\n339\\t  backoffCapMs: number;\\n340\\t}\\n341\\t\\n342\\texport type FailAttemptResult =\\n343\\t  | { terminal: false; attempts: number; eligibleAt: string }\\n344\\t  | { terminal: true; attempts: number };\\n345\\t\\n346\\t// Terminal failure: the form rows land `failed` (or `blocked` for source\\n347\\t// damage) carrying the final reason plus attempts/last-error copied onto the\\n348\\t// form's metadata, and the item row is deleted — one transaction (DD-1).\\n349\\t// Form writes stay version-checked: if a mutation moved the source since this\\n350\\t// item was queued, the stale failure must not stamp the rebuilt form.\\n351\\texport function failTerminal(\\n352\\t  db: DatabaseSync,\\n353\\t  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\\n354\\t  opts: { reason: string; formState: \\\"failed\\\" | \\\"blocked\\\"; attempts: number; now: string },\\n355\\t): void {\\n356\\t  db.exec(\\\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\\\");\\n357\\t  try {\\n358\\t    const update = db.prepare(\\n359\\t      `UPDATE derived_form\\n360\\t       SET state = ?, content = NULL, reason = ?, metadata = ?, gaps = NULL, derived_at = ?\\n361\\t       WHERE subject_kind = ? AND subject_id = ? AND form = ? AND source_version = ?`,\\n362\\t    );\\n363\\t    const metadata = JSON.stringify({ attempts: opts.attempts, lastError: opts.reason });\\n364\\t    for (const target of item.forms) {\\n365\\t      update.run(\\n366\\t        opts.formState,\\n367\\t        opts.reason,\\n368\\t        metadata,\\n369\\t        opts.now,\\n370\\t        target.subjectKind,\\n371\\t        target.subjectId,\\n372\\t        target.form,\\n373\\t        item.sourceVersion,\\n374\\t      );\\n375\\t    }\\n376\\t    db.prepare(`DELETE FROM work_item WHERE work_item_id = ?`).run(item.workItemId);\\n377\\t    db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n378\\t  } catch (cause) {\\n379\\t    db.exec(\\\"ROLLBACK;\\\");\\n380\\t    throw cause;\\n381\\t  }\\n382\\t}\\n383\\t\\n384\\t// A failed run counts itself. Under budget and retryable: the row goes back\\n385\\t// to `queued` with attempts incremented and eligibility pushed out by backoff\\n386\\t// (min(base × 2^attempts, cap)) — retry is not a status (DD-1). At budget, or\\n387\\t// on a non-retryable failure, the item is terminal: form `failed` with the\\n388\\t// final reason, row deleted (AC-1.9).\\n389\\texport function failAttempt(\\n390\\t  db: DatabaseSync,\\n391\\t  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\\n392\\t  opts: { reason: string; retryable: boolean; now: string; retry: RetryPolicy },\\n393\\t): FailAttemptResult {\\n394\\t  const attempts = item.attempts + 1;\\n395\\t  if (opts.retryable && attempts < opts.retry.budget) {\\n396\\t    const backoffMs = Math.min(\\n397\\t      opts.retry.backoffBaseMs * 2 ** attempts,\\n398\\t      opts.retry.backoffCapMs,\\n399\\t    );\\n400\\t    const eligibleAt = new Date(Date.parse(opts.now) + backoffMs).toISOString();\\n401\\t    db.exec(\\\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\\\");\\n402\\t    try {\\n403\\t      db.prepare(\\n404\\t        `UPDATE work_item\\n405\\t         SET status = 'queued', attempts = ?, last_error = ?,\\n406\\t             claimed_at = NULL, claim_expires_at = NULL, eligible_at = ?\\n407\\t         WHERE work_item_id = ?`,\\n408\\t      ).run(attempts, opts.reason, eligibleAt, item.workItemId);\\n409\\t      db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n410\\t    } catch (cause) {\\n411\\t      db.exec(\\\"ROLLBACK;\\\");\\n412\\t      throw cause;\\n413\\t    }\\n414\\t    return { terminal: false, attempts, eligibleAt };\\n415\\t  }\\n416\\t  failTerminal(db, item, { reason: opts.reason, formState: \\\"failed\\\", attempts, now: opts.now });\\n417\\t  return { terminal: true, attempts };\\n418\\t}\\n419\\t\\n420\\t// Cascade tidy (tech design issue 1): delete still-queued items for the given\\n421\\t// (kind, sourceRef) targets, returning the deleted ids for the mutation\\n422\\t// result's `superseded` list. Runs inside the caller's ambient transaction —\\n423\\t// the cascade owns the BEGIN/COMMIT — so no transaction is opened here.\\n424\\t// Claimed items are deliberately left alone: the source-version check (DD-3)\\n425\\t// discards their stale completions. Ships here as mechanics; its only caller\\n426\\t// is Story 5's cascade.\\n427\\texport function supersedeQueued(\\n428\\t  db: DatabaseSync,\\n429\\t  targets: ReadonlyArray<{ kind: WorkKind; sourceRef: WorkSourceRef }>,\\n430\\t): string[] {\\n431\\t  const ids: string[] = [];\\n432\\t  const remove = db.prepare(\\n433\\t    `DELETE FROM work_item WHERE status = 'queued' AND kind = ? AND source_ref = ?\\n434\\t     RETURNING work_item_id`,\\n435\\t  );\\n436\\t  for (const target of targets) {\\n437\\t    const rows = remove.all(target.kind, JSON.stringify(target.sourceRef)) as unknown as Array<{\\n438\\t      work_item_id: string;\\n439\\t    }>;\\n440\\t    for (const row of rows) ids.push(row.work_item_id);\\n441\\t  }\\n442\\t  return ids;\\n443\\t}\\n444\\t\\n445\\t// The requeue no-op rule's EXISTS check (AC-4.5): is there a live item —\\n446\\t// queued or in flight — for this kind and source at this source version?\\n447\\t// Runs inside the caller's requeue transaction so the check and the enqueue\\n448\\t// commit together (anti-shim: a check-then-enqueue split across transactions\\n449\\t// reintroduces the duplicate-work race). Version-scoped on purpose: a\\n450\\t// claimed pre-mutation item at an older version is dying work whose\\n451\\t// completion DD-3 will discard — it must not block the repair.\\n452\\texport function hasLiveItem(\\n453\\t  db: DatabaseSync,\\n454\\t  kind: WorkKind,\\n455\\t  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef,\\n456\\t  sourceVersion: number,\\n457\\t): boolean {\\n458\\t  const row = db\\n459\\t    .prepare(\\n460\\t      `SELECT 1 FROM work_item\\n461\\t       WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed') AND kind = ? AND source_ref = ?\\n462\\t         AND COALESCE(json_extract(payload, '$.sourceVersion'), 1) = ?\\n463\\t       LIMIT 1`,\\n464\\t    )\\n465\\t    .get(kind, JSON.stringify(sourceRef), sourceVersion);\\n466\\t  return row !== undefined;\\n467\\t}\\n468\\t\\n469\\t// Live items left in the queue — the drain report's `remaining` and the\\n470\\t// repair report's join source (Story 4).\\n471\\texport function countLiveItems(db: DatabaseSync): number {\\n472\\t  const row = db\\n473\\t    .prepare(`SELECT COUNT(*) AS n FROM work_item WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed')`)\\n474\\t    .get() as unknown as { n: number | bigint };\\n475\\t  return Number(row.n);\\n476\\t}\\n477\\t\\n478\\texport interface QueueDetailRow {\\n479\\t  workItemId: string;\\n480\\t  owner: WorkOwner;\\n481\\t  kind: string;\\n482\\t  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n483\\t  status: \\\"queued\\\" | \\\"claimed\\\";\\n484\\t  attempts: number;\\n485\\t  lastError?: string;\\n486\\t  eligibleAt?: string;\\n487\\t  claimedAt?: string;\\n488\\t  claimExpiresAt?: string;\\n489\\t  queuedAt: string;\\n490\\t  sourceVersion: number;\\n491\\t}\\n492\\t\\n493\\t// Mechanical detail of every live row in queue order — what the repair\\n494\\t// report joins against derived_form (Flow 4) and what tests read to assert\\n495\\t// lease/backoff state without raw SQL at every call site.\\n496\\texport function queueDetail(db: DatabaseSync): QueueDetailRow[] {\\n497\\t  const rows = db\\n498\\t    .prepare(\\n499\\t      `SELECT work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, status, attempts, last_error,\\n500\\t              eligible_at, claimed_at, claim_expires_at, queued_at, payload\\n501\\t       FROM work_item ORDER BY rowid`,\\n502\\t    )\\n503\\t    .all() as unknown as Array<\\n504\\t    RawClaimRow & {\\n505\\t      status: string;\\n506\\t      last_error: string | null;\\n507\\t      eligible_at: string | null;\\n508\\t      claimed_at: string | null;\\n509\\t      claim_expires_at: string | null;\\n510\\t    }\\n511\\t  >;\\n512\\t  return rows.map((row) => {\\n513\\t    const item = toClaimedItem(row);\\n514\\t    const detail: QueueDetailRow = {\\n515\\t      workItemId: item.workItemId,\\n516\\t      owner: item.owner,\\n517\\t      kind: item.kind,\\n518\\t      sourceRef: item.sourceRef,\\n519\\t      status: row.status as \\\"queued\\\" | \\\"claimed\\\",\\n520\\t      attempts: item.attempts,\\n521\\t      queuedAt: item.queuedAt,\\n522\\t      sourceVersion: item.sourceVersion,\\n523\\t    };\\n524\\t    if (row.last_error !== null) detail.lastError = row.last_error;\\n525\\t    if (row.eligible_at !== null) detail.eligibleAt = row.eligible_at;\\n526\\t    if (row.claimed_at !== null) detail.claimedAt = row.claimed_at;\\n527\\t    if (row.claim_expires_at !== null) detail.claimExpiresAt = row.claim_expires_at;\\n528\\t    return detail;\\n529\\t  });\\n530\\t}\\n531\\t\\n532\\t// Listing in queue order (insertion order within the walk); each owning\\n533\\t// domain lists only its own items.\\n534\\texport function listItems(db: DatabaseSync, owner: WorkOwner): WorkItemRecord[] {\\n535\\t  const rows = db\\n536\\t    .prepare(\\n537\\t      `SELECT work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, queued_at\\n538\\t       FROM work_item WHERE owner = ? ORDER BY rowid`,\\n539\\t    )\\n540\\t    .all(owner) as unknown as RawWorkItemRow[];\\n541\\t  return rows.map((row) => ({\\n542\\t    workItemId: row.work_item_id,\\n543\\t    owner: row.owner as WorkOwner,\\n544\\t    kind: row.kind as WorkKind,\\n545\\t    sourceRef: JSON.parse(row.source_ref) as WorkSourceRef,\\n546\\t    status: \\\"queued\\\",\\n547\\t    queuedAt: row.queued_at,\\n548\\t  }));\\n549\\t}\\n550\\t\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"11cfbce6-8c6f-47e4-8ae9-6d1d829e88b4\",\"uuid\":\"db339eba-41c6-4aa6-8ef0-0f4cf4141586\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-06-11T05:57:47.193Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/src/tech-utils/work-queue/index.ts\",\"content\":\"// Durable work-item mechanics: recording, ordered listing, and the enqueue\\n// wrapper that makes \\\"queueing is what schedules processing\\\" structural\\n// (DD-5). The util is domain-blind by the tech-arch capability rule — it\\n// knows the item vocabulary (owner, kind, sourceRef) but nothing about what\\n// a turn or a summary is; which kinds queue for which sources, and which\\n// derived forms a kind produces, is the owning domains' business (callers\\n// pass the form targets in). Writes run on the caller's handle inside the\\n// ambient transaction, so items commit (or roll back) with the batch. No\\n// public SDK surface: read-back reaches here only through the owning\\n// domains' listQueuedWork.\\nimport type { DatabaseSync } from \\\"node:sqlite\\\";\\nimport {\\n  createCommitHooks,\\n  fireSchedulerPoke,\\n  type OperationContext,\\n} from \\\"../../shared/context.js\\\";\\nimport type {\\n  CompletionTx,\\n  FormKind,\\n  HandlerFormWrite,\\n  SubjectKind,\\n} from \\\"../../shared/derivation.js\\\";\\n\\nexport type WorkOwner = \\\"messages\\\" | \\\"turns\\\";\\nexport type WorkKind =\\n  | \\\"prompt_smoothing\\\"\\n  | \\\"tool_call_summary\\\"\\n  | \\\"tool_result_summary\\\"\\n  | \\\"turn_derivation\\\"\\n  | \\\"chunk_summary_detailed\\\"\\n  | \\\"chunk_summary_brief\\\";\\nexport type WorkSourceRef = { messageId: string } | { turnId: string } | { chunkId: string };\\n\\n// The work-kind registry: owner and sourceRef semantics per the epic's Work\\n// Item contract. Mechanical metadata only — what a kind *means* stays with\\n// the owning domain's handler (registered at SDK construction, DD-6).\\nexport const WORK_KIND_REGISTRY: Readonly<\\n  Record<WorkKind, { owner: WorkOwner; sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" | \\\"turnId\\\" | \\\"chunkId\\\" }>\\n> = {\\n  prompt_smoothing: { owner: \\\"messages\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" },\\n  tool_call_summary: { owner: \\\"messages\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" },\\n  tool_result_summary: { owner: \\\"messages\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"messageId\\\" },\\n  turn_derivation: { owner: \\\"turns\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"turnId\\\" },\\n  chunk_summary_detailed: { owner: \\\"turns\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"chunkId\\\" },\\n  chunk_summary_brief: { owner: \\\"turns\\\", sourceRefKey: \\\"chunkId\\\" },\\n};\\n\\nexport interface WorkItemRecord {\\n  workItemId: string;\\n  owner: WorkOwner;\\n  kind: WorkKind;\\n  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n  status: \\\"queued\\\"; // the only status written before a drain claims (Epic 02 Story 1)\\n  queuedAt: string;\\n}\\n\\n// The derived forms an enqueue is scheduling work toward; the owning domain\\n// names them (the util stays meaning-blind). Stored in the row's payload so\\n// the drain's terminal paths (retry exhaustion, blocked source) can land the\\n// form's failed/blocked state without asking any domain what a kind means.\\nexport interface EnqueueFormTarget {\\n  subjectKind: SubjectKind;\\n  subjectId: string;\\n  form: FormKind;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface WorkItemInput {\\n  owner: WorkOwner;\\n  kind: WorkKind;\\n  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n  sourceVersion?: number; // defaults to 1 — first version of a fresh source\\n  forms?: readonly EnqueueFormTarget[]; // carried in payload for the drain's terminal paths\\n}\\n\\nfunction sourceIdOf(sourceRef: WorkSourceRef): string {\\n  if (\\\"messageId\\\" in sourceRef) return sourceRef.messageId;\\n  if (\\\"turnId\\\" in sourceRef) return sourceRef.turnId;\\n  return sourceRef.chunkId;\\n}\\n\\n// Deterministic id scoped to the source version (DD-1/DD-3): re-queueing the\\n// same kind for the same source *at the same version* is the same id —\\n// natural idempotency for the repair path — while a post-mutation\\n// replacement (next version) never collides with an in-flight pre-mutation\\n// item.\\nexport function recordItem(\\n  db: DatabaseSync,\\n  input: WorkItemInput,\\n  queuedAt: string,\\n): WorkItemRecord {\\n  const sourceVersion = input.sourceVersion ?? 1;\\n  const workItemId = `w-${sourceIdOf(input.sourceRef)}-${input.kind}-v${sourceVersion}`;\\n  db.prepare(\\n    `INSERT INTO work_item (work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, status, queued_at, payload)\\n     VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, 'queued', ?, ?)`,\\n  ).run(\\n    workItemId,\\n    input.owner,\\n    input.kind,\\n    JSON.stringify(input.sourceRef),\\n    queuedAt,\\n    JSON.stringify({ sourceVersion, forms: input.forms ?? [] }),\\n  );\\n  return {\\n    workItemId,\\n    owner: input.owner,\\n    kind: input.kind,\\n    sourceRef: input.sourceRef,\\n    status: \\\"queued\\\",\\n    queuedAt,\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nexport interface EnqueueInput extends WorkItemInput {\\n  forms: readonly EnqueueFormTarget[];\\n}\\n\\n// The one way work is scheduled (DD-5): the work row, the form's `pending`\\n// state row, and the scheduler poke all ride the ambient transaction — they\\n// commit together or vanish together. Enqueue is the *only* place a\\n// derived_form row is created (completion is UPDATE-only); re-enqueueing\\n// resets an existing row to pending at the enqueued source version.\\nexport function enqueue(ctx: OperationContext, input: EnqueueInput): WorkItemRecord {\\n  const item = recordItem(ctx.db, input, ctx.clock().toISOString());\\n  const sourceVersion = input.sourceVersion ?? 1;\\n  const upsert = ctx.db.prepare(\\n    `INSERT INTO derived_form (subject_kind, subject_id, form, state, source_version)\\n     VALUES (?, ?, ?, 'pending', ?)\\n     ON CONFLICT (subject_kind, subject_id, form) DO UPDATE SET\\n       state = 'pending', content = NULL, reason = NULL, metadata = NULL,\\n       gaps = NULL, derived_at = NULL, source_version = excluded.source_version`,\\n  );\\n  for (const target of input.forms) {\\n    upsert.run(target.subjectKind, target.subjectId, target.form, sourceVersion);\\n  }\\n  ctx.onCommit(() => fireSchedulerPoke(ctx.threadId));\\n  return item;\\n}\\n\\ninterface RawWorkItemRow {\\n  work_item_id: string;\\n  owner: string;\\n  kind: string;\\n  source_ref: string;\\n  queued_at: string;\\n}\\n\\n// ── claim mechanics (Epic 02 Story 1, tech design §Mechanics) ─────\\n// All pure SQL against the caller's open handle, domain-blind. Claim,\\n// complete, and the terminal half of failAttempt each own one short\\n// BEGIN IMMEDIATE; the handler in between runs with no open transaction.\\n\\n// The claimed row as the drain holds it: mechanical fields plus the payload's\\n// source version and form targets. `kind` is a plain string here — a raw row\\n// with an unregistered kind must still be claimable so the drain can land it\\n// failed_terminal instead of skipping it (AC-1.8).\\nexport interface ClaimedWorkItem {\\n  workItemId: string;\\n  owner: WorkOwner;\\n  kind: string;\\n  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n  attempts: number;\\n  queuedAt: string;\\n  sourceVersion: number;\\n  forms: EnqueueFormTarget[];\\n}\\n\\nexport type ClaimOutcome =\\n  | { outcome: \\\"claimed\\\"; item: ClaimedWorkItem }\\n  | { outcome: \\\"empty\\\" }\\n  | { outcome: \\\"in_flight\\\"; claimExpiresAt: string }\\n  | { outcome: \\\"waiting\\\"; waitingUntil: string };\\n\\ninterface RawClaimRow {\\n  work_item_id: string;\\n  owner: string;\\n  kind: string;\\n  source_ref: string;\\n  attempts: number | bigint;\\n  queued_at: string;\\n  payload: string | null;\\n}\\n\\n// Form targets for pre-v5 rows, whose payload is NULL (Epic 01 wrote no\\n// payload column). Exactly the F-02 backfill's kind→form mapping (migration\\n// v5, shared/storage.ts): the backfill created pending derived_form rows at\\n// source version 1 for these kinds, and a terminal failure must find them —\\n// otherwise exhaustion would delete the work row and strand the backfilled\\n// form as pending forever, breaking AC-1.9/DD-1 for upgraded threads.\\nconst LEGACY_KIND_FORMS: Partial<\\n  Record<string, ReadonlyArray<{ subjectKind: SubjectKind; form: FormKind }>>\\n> = {\\n  prompt_smoothing: [{ subjectKind: \\\"message\\\", form: \\\"smoothed_prompt\\\" }],\\n  tool_result_summary: [{ subjectKind: \\\"message\\\", form: \\\"tool_result_summary\\\" }],\\n  turn_derivation: [\\n    { subjectKind: \\\"turn\\\", form: \\\"turn_rendering\\\" },\\n    { subjectKind: \\\"turn\\\", form: \\\"lower_band_projection\\\" },\\n  ],\\n};\\n\\nfunction legacyFormTargets(kind: string, sourceRef: WorkSourceRef): EnqueueFormTarget[] {\\n  const mapped = LEGACY_KIND_FORMS[kind];\\n  if (mapped === undefined) return [];\\n  const subjectId = sourceIdOf(sourceRef);\\n  return mapped.map((target) => ({ ...target, subjectId }));\\n}\\n\\nfunction toClaimedItem(row: RawClaimRow): ClaimedWorkItem {\\n  const sourceRef = JSON.parse(row.source_ref) as WorkSourceRef;\\n  const payload =\\n    row.payload === null\\n      ? // Pre-v5 row: version 1 (matching the backfill) and form targets\\n        // reconstructed from the backfill's own mapping.\\n        { sourceVersion: 1, forms: legacyFormTargets(row.kind, sourceRef) }\\n      : (JSON.parse(row.payload) as { sourceVersion?: number; forms?: EnqueueFormTarget[] });\\n  return {\\n    workItemId: row.work_item_id,\\n    owner: row.owner as WorkOwner,\\n    kind: row.kind,\\n    sourceRef,\\n    attempts: Number(row.attempts),\\n    queuedAt: row.queued_at,\\n    sourceVersion: payload.sourceVersion ?? 1,\\n    forms: payload.forms ?? [],\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n// Head-first, never skip-ahead: the claim decision is made against the oldest\\n// live row only — a later eligible row is never considered while an older\\n// live row exists. One atomic UPDATE under BEGIN IMMEDIATE (no read-then-\\n// write split: the race window is the bug class). The CASE increments\\n// `attempts` only when reclaiming an expired claimed row — a reclaim means a\\n// run died or hung, and the increment makes the crash visible in the report\\n// and counts it against the retry budget; normal claims never touch it.\\nexport function claimNext(db: DatabaseSync, now: string, leaseDurationMs: number): ClaimOutcome {\\n  const expiresAt = new Date(Date.parse(now) + leaseDurationMs).toISOString();\\n  db.exec(\\\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\\\");\\n  try {\\n    const claimed = db\\n      .prepare(\\n        `UPDATE work_item SET status = 'claimed', claimed_at = ?, claim_expires_at = ?,\\n           attempts = attempts + CASE WHEN status = 'claimed' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END\\n         WHERE work_item_id = (\\n           SELECT work_item_id FROM (\\n             SELECT work_item_id, status, eligible_at, claim_expires_at\\n             FROM work_item WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed')\\n             ORDER BY rowid LIMIT 1\\n           )\\n           WHERE (status = 'queued' AND (eligible_at IS NULL OR eligible_at <= ?))\\n              OR (status = 'claimed' AND claim_expires_at <= ?)\\n         )\\n         RETURNING work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, attempts, queued_at, payload`,\\n      )\\n      .get(now, expiresAt, now, now) as unknown as RawClaimRow | undefined;\\n    if (claimed !== undefined) {\\n      db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n      return { outcome: \\\"claimed\\\", item: toClaimedItem(claimed) };\\n    }\\n    // No row claimable: re-read the head inside the same transaction to\\n    // classify the stop. A claimed head with a live lease gates the queue\\n    // (in_flight, AC-1.4); a queued head still backing off gates it too\\n    // (waiting) — strict ordering, never reordered around the head.\\n    const head = db\\n      .prepare(\\n        `SELECT status, eligible_at, claim_expires_at\\n         FROM work_item WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed')\\n         ORDER BY rowid LIMIT 1`,\\n      )\\n      .get() as unknown as\\n      | { status: string; eligible_at: string | null; claim_expires_at: string | null }\\n      | undefined;\\n    db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n    if (head === undefined) return { outcome: \\\"empty\\\" };\\n    if (head.status === \\\"claimed\\\") {\\n      return { outcome: \\\"in_flight\\\", claimExpiresAt: head.claim_expires_at ?? \\\"\\\" };\\n    }\\n    return { outcome: \\\"waiting\\\", waitingUntil: head.eligible_at ?? \\\"\\\" };\\n  } catch (cause) {\\n    db.exec(\\\"ROLLBACK;\\\");\\n    throw cause;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Completion: the version-checked form writes and the item row's deletion in\\n// one short transaction (DD-1: terminal transition deletes the row; the\\n// disposition is reported in-memory, never stored). UPDATE-only, never\\n// upsert — the pending row exists from enqueue; a write that finds no row at\\n// the item's source version is discarded as stale (DD-3 truth table). The\\n// optional onApplied hook (Story 3) runs inside this same transaction, after\\n// the form writes and only when they landed — chunk placement and the\\n// close→summary enqueues ride the completion commit; its onCommit\\n// registrations (the enqueue pokes) flush after COMMIT and drop on rollback.\\nexport function complete(\\n  db: DatabaseSync,\\n  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\\n  writes: readonly HandlerFormWrite[],\\n  derivedAt: string,\\n  onApplied?: (tx: CompletionTx) => void,\\n): \\\"done\\\" | \\\"stale_discarded\\\" {\\n  const hooks = createCommitHooks();\\n  db.exec(\\\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\\\");\\n  try {\\n    let hits = 0;\\n    const update = db.prepare(\\n      `UPDATE derived_form\\n       SET state = 'ready', content = ?, reason = NULL, metadata = ?, gaps = ?, derived_at = ?\\n       WHERE subject_kind = ? AND subject_id = ? AND form = ? AND source_version = ?`,\\n    );\\n    for (const write of writes) {\\n      const changed = update.run(\\n        write.content,\\n        write.metadata === undefined ? null : JSON.stringify(write.metadata),\\n        write.gaps === undefined ? null : JSON.stringify(write.gaps),\\n        derivedAt,\\n        write.subjectKind,\\n        write.subjectId,\\n        write.form,\\n        item.sourceVersion,\\n      );\\n      hits += Number(changed.changes);\\n    }\\n    const stale = writes.length > 0 && hits === 0;\\n    if (!stale && onApplied !== undefined) {\\n      onApplied({ db, onCommit: hooks.register });\\n    }\\n    db.prepare(`DELETE FROM work_item WHERE work_item_id = ?`).run(item.workItemId);\\n    db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n    hooks.flush();\\n    return stale ? \\\"stale_discarded\\\" : \\\"done\\\";\\n  } catch (cause) {\\n    db.exec(\\\"ROLLBACK;\\\");\\n    throw cause;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nexport interface RetryPolicy {\\n  budget: number;\\n  backoffBaseMs: number;\\n  backoffCapMs: number;\\n}\\n\\nexport type FailAttemptResult =\\n  | { terminal: false; attempts: number; eligibleAt: string }\\n  | { terminal: true; attempts: number };\\n\\n// Terminal failure: the form rows land `failed` (or `blocked` for source\\n// damage) carrying the final reason plus attempts/last-error copied onto the\\n// form's metadata, and the item row is deleted — one transaction (DD-1).\\n// Form writes stay version-checked: if a mutation moved the source since this\\n// item was queued, the stale failure must not stamp the rebuilt form.\\nexport function failTerminal(\\n  db: DatabaseSync,\\n  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\\n  opts: { reason: string; formState: \\\"failed\\\" | \\\"blocked\\\"; attempts: number; now: string },\\n): void {\\n  db.exec(\\\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\\\");\\n  try {\\n    const update = db.prepare(\\n      `UPDATE derived_form\\n       SET state = ?, content = NULL, reason = ?, metadata = ?, gaps = NULL, derived_at = ?\\n       WHERE subject_kind = ? AND subject_id = ? AND form = ? AND source_version = ?`,\\n    );\\n    const metadata = JSON.stringify({ attempts: opts.attempts, lastError: opts.reason });\\n    for (const target of item.forms) {\\n      update.run(\\n        opts.formState,\\n        opts.reason,\\n        metadata,\\n        opts.now,\\n        target.subjectKind,\\n        target.subjectId,\\n        target.form,\\n        item.sourceVersion,\\n      );\\n    }\\n    db.prepare(`DELETE FROM work_item WHERE work_item_id = ?`).run(item.workItemId);\\n    db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n  } catch (cause) {\\n    db.exec(\\\"ROLLBACK;\\\");\\n    throw cause;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// A failed run counts itself. Under budget and retryable: the row goes back\\n// to `queued` with attempts incremented and eligibility pushed out by backoff\\n// (min(base × 2^attempts, cap)) — retry is not a status (DD-1). At budget, or\\n// on a non-retryable failure, the item is terminal: form `failed` with the\\n// final reason, row deleted (AC-1.9).\\nexport function failAttempt(\\n  db: DatabaseSync,\\n  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\\n  opts: { reason: string; retryable: boolean; now: string; retry: RetryPolicy },\\n): FailAttemptResult {\\n  const attempts = item.attempts + 1;\\n  if (opts.retryable && attempts < opts.retry.budget) {\\n    const backoffMs = Math.min(\\n      opts.retry.backoffBaseMs * 2 ** attempts,\\n      opts.retry.backoffCapMs,\\n    );\\n    const eligibleAt = new Date(Date.parse(opts.now) + backoffMs).toISOString();\\n    db.exec(\\\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\\\");\\n    try {\\n      db.prepare(\\n        `UPDATE work_item\\n         SET status = 'queued', attempts = ?, last_error = ?,\\n             claimed_at = NULL, claim_expires_at = NULL, eligible_at = ?\\n         WHERE work_item_id = ?`,\\n      ).run(attempts, opts.reason, eligibleAt, item.workItemId);\\n      db.exec(\\\"COMMIT;\\\");\\n    } catch (cause) {\\n      db.exec(\\\"ROLLBACK;\\\");\\n      throw cause;\\n    }\\n    return { terminal: false, attempts, eligibleAt };\\n  }\\n  failTerminal(db, item, { reason: opts.reason, formState: \\\"failed\\\", attempts, now: opts.now });\\n  return { terminal: true, attempts };\\n}\\n\\n// Cascade tidy (tech design issue 1): delete still-queued items for the given\\n// (kind, sourceRef) targets, returning the deleted ids for the mutation\\n// result's `superseded` list. Runs inside the caller's ambient transaction —\\n// the cascade owns the BEGIN/COMMIT — so no transaction is opened here.\\n// Claimed items are deliberately left alone: the source-version check (DD-3)\\n// discards their stale completions. Ships here as mechanics; its only caller\\n// is Story 5's cascade.\\nexport function supersedeQueued(\\n  db: DatabaseSync,\\n  targets: ReadonlyArray<{ kind: WorkKind; sourceRef: WorkSourceRef }>,\\n): string[] {\\n  const ids: string[] = [];\\n  const remove = db.prepare(\\n    `DELETE FROM work_item WHERE status = 'queued' AND kind = ? AND source_ref = ?\\n     RETURNING work_item_id`,\\n  );\\n  for (const target of targets) {\\n    const rows = remove.all(target.kind, JSON.stringify(target.sourceRef)) as unknown as Array<{\\n      work_item_id: string;\\n    }>;\\n    for (const row of rows) ids.push(row.work_item_id);\\n  }\\n  return ids;\\n}\\n\\n// The requeue no-op rule's EXISTS check (AC-4.5): is there a live item —\\n// queued or in flight — for this kind and source at this source version?\\n// Runs inside the caller's requeue transaction so the check and the enqueue\\n// commit together (anti-shim: a check-then-enqueue split across transactions\\n// reintroduces the duplicate-work race). Version-scoped on purpose: a\\n// claimed pre-mutation item at an older version is dying work whose\\n// completion DD-3 will discard — it must not block the repair.\\nexport function hasLiveItem(\\n  db: DatabaseSync,\\n  kind: WorkKind,\\n  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef,\\n  sourceVersion: number,\\n): boolean {\\n  const row = db\\n    .prepare(\\n      `SELECT 1 FROM work_item\\n       WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed') AND kind = ? AND source_ref = ?\\n         AND COALESCE(json_extract(payload, '$.sourceVersion'), 1) = ?\\n       LIMIT 1`,\\n    )\\n    .get(kind, JSON.stringify(sourceRef), sourceVersion);\\n  return row !== undefined;\\n}\\n\\n// Live items left in the queue — the drain report's `remaining` and the\\n// repair report's join source (Story 4).\\nexport function countLiveItems(db: DatabaseSync): number {\\n  const row = db\\n    .prepare(`SELECT COUNT(*) AS n FROM work_item WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed')`)\\n    .get() as unknown as { n: number | bigint };\\n  return Number(row.n);\\n}\\n\\nexport interface QueueDetailRow {\\n  workItemId: string;\\n  owner: WorkOwner;\\n  kind: string;\\n  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n  status: \\\"queued\\\" | \\\"claimed\\\";\\n  attempts: number;\\n  lastError?: string;\\n  eligibleAt?: string;\\n  claimedAt?: string;\\n  claimExpiresAt?: string;\\n  queuedAt: string;\\n  sourceVersion: number;\\n}\\n\\n// Mechanical detail of every live row in queue order — what the repair\\n// report joins against derived_form (Flow 4) and what tests read to assert\\n// lease/backoff state without raw SQL at every call site.\\nexport function queueDetail(db: DatabaseSync): QueueDetailRow[] {\\n  const rows = db\\n    .prepare(\\n      `SELECT work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, status, attempts, last_error,\\n              eligible_at, claimed_at, claim_expires_at, queued_at, payload\\n       FROM work_item ORDER BY rowid`,\\n    )\\n    .all() as unknown as Array<\\n    RawClaimRow & {\\n      status: string;\\n      last_error: string | null;\\n      eligible_at: string | null;\\n      claimed_at: string | null;\\n      claim_expires_at: string | null;\\n    }\\n  >;\\n  return rows.map((row) => {\\n    const item = toClaimedItem(row);\\n    const detail: QueueDetailRow = {\\n      workItemId: item.workItemId,\\n      owner: item.owner,\\n      kind: item.kind,\\n      sourceRef: item.sourceRef,\\n      status: row.status as \\\"queued\\\" | \\\"claimed\\\",\\n      attempts: item.attempts,\\n      queuedAt: item.queuedAt,\\n      sourceVersion: item.sourceVersion,\\n    };\\n    if (row.last_error !== null) detail.lastError = row.last_error;\\n    if (row.eligible_at !== null) detail.eligibleAt = row.eligible_at;\\n    if (row.claimed_at !== null) detail.claimedAt = row.claimed_at;\\n    if (row.claim_expires_at !== null) detail.claimExpiresAt = row.claim_expires_at;\\n    return detail;\\n  });\\n}\\n\\n// Listing in queue order (insertion order within the walk); each owning\\n// domain lists only its own items.\\nexport function listItems(db: DatabaseSync, owner: WorkOwner): WorkItemRecord[] {\\n  const rows = db\\n    .prepare(\\n      `SELECT work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, queued_at\\n       FROM work_item WHERE owner = ? ORDER BY rowid`,\\n    )\\n    .all(owner) as unknown as RawWorkItemRow[];\\n  return rows.map((row) => ({\\n    workItemId: row.work_item_id,\\n    owner: row.owner as WorkOwner,\\n    kind: row.kind as WorkKind,\\n    sourceRef: JSON.parse(row.source_ref) as WorkSourceRef,\\n    status: \\\"queued\\\",\\n    queuedAt: row.queued_at,\\n  }));\\n}\\n\",\"numLines\":550,\"startLine\":1,\"totalLines\":550}}}\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/artifacts/05-message-edit-cascade/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:424:{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_019waJonJhhsamRMjkG2P6Ud\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"1\\t// SDK-internal scheduler and drain (DD-4): the one component holding\\n2\\t// cross-operation in-memory state. The drain loop lives here per the tech\\n3\\t// design's placement — claim under BEGIN IMMEDIATE, dispatch through the\\n4\\t// handler map with no open transaction (the provider call lives there),\\n5\\t// complete in a second short transaction. Background mode adds per-thread\\n6\\t// single-flight with pending-flag coalescing, post-commit pokes, first-touch\\n7\\t// catch-up (DD-10), and drainSettled (issue 3). The in-memory state is\\n8\\t// advisory only: cross-process safety comes from the durable lease alone, so\\n9\\t// a fresh handle sees identical drain behavior — the queue is the rows.\\n10\\timport { existsSync } from \\\"node:fs\\\";\\n11\\timport { setImmediate as scheduleMacrotask } from \\\"node:timers\\\";\\n12\\timport { DatabaseSync } from \\\"node:sqlite\\\";\\n13\\timport { openThreadDatabase } from \\\"./domains/threads/index.js\\\";\\n14\\timport type {\\n15\\t  HandlerOutcome,\\n16\\t  ResolvedSdkConfig,\\n17\\t  WorkHandler,\\n18\\t} from \\\"./shared/derivation.js\\\";\\n19\\timport { storageFailure, type ErrorResult, type OpResult } from \\\"./shared/errors.js\\\";\\n20\\timport {\\n21\\t  claimNext,\\n22\\t  complete,\\n23\\t  countLiveItems,\\n24\\t  failAttempt,\\n25\\t  failTerminal,\\n26\\t  type ClaimedWorkItem,\\n27\\t  type WorkKind,\\n28\\t  type WorkSourceRef,\\n29\\t} from \\\"./tech-utils/work-queue/index.js\\\";\\n30\\t\\n31\\texport type SchedulerMode = \\\"background\\\" | \\\"manual\\\";\\n32\\t\\n33\\t// Report shape per tech design §Interfaces. `superseded` never appears here:\\n34\\t// the cascade deletes superseded items and reports them on the mutation\\n35\\t// result; a drain never sees them.\\n36\\texport interface DrainReport {\\n37\\t  ran: Array<{\\n38\\t    workItemId: string;\\n39\\t    kind: WorkKind;\\n40\\t    sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n41\\t    disposition: \\\"done\\\" | \\\"failed_terminal\\\" | \\\"stale_discarded\\\";\\n42\\t    attempts: number;\\n43\\t    reason?: string;\\n44\\t  }>;\\n45\\t  stoppedBecause: \\\"empty\\\" | \\\"in_flight\\\" | \\\"waiting\\\" | \\\"max_items\\\";\\n46\\t  waitingUntil?: string; // head's eligible_at when stoppedBecause = \\\"waiting\\\"\\n47\\t  remaining: number; // live items left behind the stop point\\n48\\t}\\n49\\t\\n50\\texport interface DrainDeps {\\n51\\t  lookupHandler: (kind: string) => OpResult<WorkHandler>;\\n52\\t  // Whether any handler is registered at all. Background scheduling is gated\\n53\\t  // on this, fail-closed: with an empty map a background drain could only\\n54\\t  // turn queued rows into failed_terminal — destruction, not processing — so\\n55\\t  // pokes and catch-up stay inert until a handler table is populated\\n56\\t  // (Stories 2–3 in production; explicit registration in tests).\\n57\\t  hasAnyHandler: () => boolean;\\n58\\t  config: ResolvedSdkConfig;\\n59\\t}\\n60\\t\\n61\\tfunction ranEntry(\\n62\\t  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\\n63\\t  disposition: \\\"done\\\" | \\\"failed_terminal\\\" | \\\"stale_discarded\\\",\\n64\\t  attempts: number,\\n65\\t  reason?: string,\\n66\\t): DrainReport[\\\"ran\\\"][number] {\\n67\\t  const entry: DrainReport[\\\"ran\\\"][number] = {\\n68\\t    workItemId: item.workItemId,\\n69\\t    kind: item.kind as WorkKind,\\n70\\t    sourceRef: item.sourceRef,\\n71\\t    disposition,\\n72\\t    attempts,\\n73\\t  };\\n74\\t  if (reason !== undefined) entry.reason = reason;\\n75\\t  return entry;\\n76\\t}\\n77\\t\\n78\\t// The drain loop against an open handle. Claim → dispatch → complete, one\\n79\\t// item at a time, until the head stops it (empty / in_flight / waiting) or\\n80\\t// maxItems is reached. The handler runs with NO open transaction; the only\\n81\\t// exits from a handler failure are failAttempt and the terminal paths — the\\n82\\t// drain never catches an error and records success.\\n83\\texport async function drainOpenDb(\\n84\\t  db: DatabaseSync,\\n85\\t  deps: DrainDeps,\\n86\\t  opts?: { maxItems?: number },\\n87\\t): Promise<DrainReport> {\\n88\\t  const { clock, lease, retry, provider } = deps.config;\\n89\\t  const ran: DrainReport[\\\"ran\\\"] = [];\\n90\\t  let stoppedBecause: DrainReport[\\\"stoppedBecause\\\"];\\n91\\t  let waitingUntil: string | undefined;\\n92\\t\\n93\\t  for (;;) {\\n94\\t    if (opts?.maxItems !== undefined && ran.length >= opts.maxItems) {\\n95\\t      stoppedBecause = \\\"max_items\\\";\\n96\\t      break;\\n97\\t    }\\n98\\t    const claim = claimNext(db, clock().toISOString(), lease.durationMs);\\n99\\t    if (claim.outcome === \\\"empty\\\") {\\n100\\t      stoppedBecause = \\\"empty\\\";\\n101\\t      break;\\n102\\t    }\\n103\\t    if (claim.outcome === \\\"in_flight\\\") {\\n104\\t      stoppedBecause = \\\"in_flight\\\";\\n105\\t      break;\\n106\\t    }\\n107\\t    if (claim.outcome === \\\"waiting\\\") {\\n108\\t      stoppedBecause = \\\"waiting\\\";\\n109\\t      waitingUntil = claim.waitingUntil;\\n110\\t      break;\\n111\\t    }\\n112\\t    const item = claim.item;\\n113\\t\\n114\\t    const lookedUp = deps.lookupHandler(item.kind);\\n115\\t    if (!lookedUp.ok) {\\n116\\t      // Unregistered kind: the normal terminal transaction, not a try/catch\\n117\\t      // skip — failed_terminal with the stable code, form (if resolvable\\n118\\t      // from the payload) lands failed, and the drain continues (AC-1.8).\\n119\\t      failTerminal(db, item, {\\n120\\t        reason: lookedUp.error.code,\\n121\\t        formState: \\\"failed\\\",\\n122\\t        attempts: item.attempts,\\n123\\t        now: clock().toISOString(),\\n124\\t      });\\n125\\t      ran.push(ranEntry(item, \\\"failed_terminal\\\", item.attempts, lookedUp.error.code));\\n126\\t      continue;\\n127\\t    }\\n128\\t\\n129\\t    let outcome: HandlerOutcome;\\n130\\t    try {\\n131\\t      outcome = await lookedUp.value(\\n132\\t        { openDb: () => db, provider, clock, config: deps.config },\\n133\\t        {\\n134\\t          workItemId: item.workItemId,\\n135\\t          kind: item.kind,\\n136\\t          sourceRef: item.sourceRef as unknown as Record<string, string>,\\n137\\t        },\\n138\\t      );\\n139\\t    } catch (cause) {\\n140\\t      // A throwing handler is a bug by the error contract, but the queue\\n141\\t      // must not wedge on it: route it through the normal retry path so it\\n142\\t      // counts attempts and exhausts visibly.\\n143\\t      const detail = cause instanceof Error ? cause.message : String(cause);\\n144\\t      outcome = { ok: false, retryable: true, reason: `handler threw: ${detail}` };\\n145\\t    }\\n146\\t\\n147\\t    if (outcome.ok) {\\n148\\t      const disposition = complete(\\n149\\t        db,\\n150\\t        item,\\n151\\t        outcome.forms ?? [],\\n152\\t        clock().toISOString(),\\n153\\t        outcome.onApplied,\\n154\\t      );\\n155\\t      ran.push(ranEntry(item, disposition, item.attempts));\\n156\\t      continue;\\n157\\t    }\\n158\\t    if (\\\"blocked\\\" in outcome) {\\n159\\t      failTerminal(db, item, {\\n160\\t        reason: outcome.reason,\\n161\\t        formState: \\\"blocked\\\",\\n162\\t        attempts: item.attempts + 1,\\n163\\t        now: clock().toISOString(),\\n164\\t      });\\n165\\t      ran.push(ranEntry(item, \\\"failed_terminal\\\", item.attempts + 1, outcome.reason));\\n166\\t      continue;\\n167\\t    }\\n168\\t    const failed = failAttempt(db, item, {\\n169\\t      reason: outcome.reason,\\n170\\t      retryable: outcome.retryable,\\n171\\t      now: clock().toISOString(),\\n172\\t      retry,\\n173\\t    });\\n174\\t    if (failed.terminal) {\\n175\\t      ran.push(ranEntry(item, \\\"failed_terminal\\\", failed.attempts, outcome.reason));\\n176\\t    }\\n177\\t    // Under budget the item went back to queued (possibly backing off); the\\n178\\t    // next claimNext re-reads the head — a backing-off head ends the drain\\n179\\t    // with \\\"waiting\\\" and gates everything behind it.\\n180\\t  }\\n181\\t\\n182\\t  const report: DrainReport = { ran, stoppedBecause, remaining: countLiveItems(db) };\\n183\\t  if (waitingUntil !== undefined) report.waitingUntil = waitingUntil;\\n184\\t  return report;\\n185\\t}\\n186\\t\\n187\\tfunction threadNotFound(filePath: string): { ok: false; error: ErrorResult } {\\n188\\t  return {\\n189\\t    ok: false,\\n190\\t    error: {\\n191\\t      errorClass: \\\"caller_error\\\",\\n192\\t      code: \\\"thread_not_found\\\",\\n193\\t      reason: `no thread file exists at ${filePath}`,\\n194\\t    },\\n195\\t  };\\n196\\t}\\n197\\t\\n198\\t// The drain operation against a thread file: open (validates + migrates),\\n199\\t// drain, close. Both the SDK's work.drain and the background loop's passes\\n200\\t// run through here — manual and background modes share one drain.\\n201\\texport async function runDrain(\\n202\\t  filePath: string,\\n203\\t  deps: DrainDeps,\\n204\\t  opts?: { maxItems?: number },\\n205\\t): Promise<OpResult<DrainReport>> {\\n206\\t  if (!existsSync(filePath)) return threadNotFound(filePath);\\n207\\t  const opened = openThreadDatabase(filePath);\\n208\\t  if (!opened.ok) return opened;\\n209\\t  const db = opened.value;\\n210\\t  try {\\n211\\t    return { ok: true, value: await drainOpenDb(db, deps, opts) };\\n212\\t  } catch (cause) {\\n213\\t    const detail = cause instanceof Error ? cause.message : String(cause);\\n214\\t    return storageFailure(`drain failed: ${detail}`);\\n215\\t  } finally {\\n216\\t    db.close();\\n217\\t  }\\n218\\t}\\n219\\t\\n220\\tinterface ThreadDrainState {\\n221\\t  filePath: string;\\n222\\t  running: boolean;\\n223\\t  pending: boolean;\\n224\\t  passes: number; // test-only observability (TC-1.2); must not become API\\n225\\t  waiters: Array<() => void>;\\n226\\t}\\n227\\t\\n228\\texport interface Scheduler {\\n229\\t  readonly mode: SchedulerMode;\\n230\\t  // Post-commit nudge that work was queued for a thread (DD-5). Manual mode:\\n231\\t  // no-op by contract. Background mode: starts a drain, or coalesces into\\n232\\t  // the pending flag if one is already running for the thread (AC-1.2).\\n233\\t  poke(threadId: string): void;\\n234\\t  // Thread-file open announcement (DD-10): learns threadId → filePath and,\\n235\\t  // on the first touch of a thread this process lifetime, schedules a\\n236\\t  // catch-up drain if the queue has leftover live work (AC-1.6).\\n237\\t  touch(filePath: string, db: DatabaseSync): void;\\n238\\t  // Resolves when the scheduler has no running or pending drain for the\\n239\\t  // thread (issue 3). Manual mode resolves immediately.\\n240\\t  drainSettled(threadId: string): Promise<void>;\\n241\\t  // Test-only observability for coalescing exactness (TC-1.2): drain passes\\n242\\t  // started for a thread. Named as a test hook on purpose — not API.\\n243\\t  testPassCount(threadId: string): number;\\n244\\t}\\n245\\t\\n246\\tfunction readThreadId(db: DatabaseSync): string | null {\\n247\\t  const row = db.prepare(\\\"SELECT thread_id FROM thread_metadata WHERE id = 1\\\").get() as\\n248\\t    | { thread_id: string }\\n249\\t    | undefined;\\n250\\t  return row?.thread_id ?? null;\\n251\\t}\\n252\\t\\n253\\t// Read a thread file's id without side effects (no migration, no touch) —\\n254\\t// drainSettled must observe scheduler state, never schedule work.\\n255\\texport function peekThreadId(filePath: string): string | null {\\n256\\t  if (!existsSync(filePath)) return null;\\n257\\t  let db: DatabaseSync | undefined;\\n258\\t  try {\\n259\\t    db = new DatabaseSync(filePath, { readOnly: true });\\n260\\t    return readThreadId(db);\\n261\\t  } catch {\\n262\\t    return null;\\n263\\t  } finally {\\n264\\t    db?.close();\\n265\\t  }\\n266\\t}\\n267\\t\\n268\\texport function createScheduler(mode: SchedulerMode, deps: DrainDeps): Scheduler {\\n269\\t  const states = new Map<string, ThreadDrainState>();\\n270\\t  const seen = new Set<string>(); // first-touch catch-up guard, process lifetime\\n271\\t\\n272\\t  function stateFor(threadId: string): ThreadDrainState {\\n273\\t    let st = states.get(threadId);\\n274\\t    if (st === undefined) {\\n275\\t      st = { filePath: \\\"\\\", running: false, pending: false, passes: 0, waiters: [] };\\n276\\t      states.set(threadId, st);\\n277\\t    }\\n278\\t    return st;\\n279\\t  }\\n280\\t\\n281\\t  async function runLoop(st: ThreadDrainState): Promise<void> {\\n282\\t    // Defer past the committing operation's synchronous tail so the drain\\n283\\t    // never contends with the transaction whose commit scheduled it.\\n284\\t    await new Promise((resolve) => scheduleMacrotask(resolve));\\n285\\t    try {\\n286\\t      do {\\n287\\t        st.pending = false;\\n288\\t        st.passes += 1;\\n289\\t        // A failed pass (storage error) has no caller to report to in\\n290\\t        // background mode; the durable rows are untouched and the next poke\\n291\\t        // or touch retries. A pass stopping on \\\"waiting\\\" likewise leaves the\\n292\\t        // backing-off head for the next nudge — the lease and eligibility\\n293\\t        // gates are durable, not timer state.\\n294\\t        await runDrain(st.filePath, deps);\\n295\\t      } while (st.pending);\\n296\\t    } finally {\\n297\\t      st.running = false;\\n298\\t      for (const waiter of st.waiters.splice(0)) waiter();\\n299\\t    }\\n300\\t  }\\n301\\t\\n302\\t  function schedule(threadId: string): void {\\n303\\t    const st = stateFor(threadId);\\n304\\t    if (st.filePath === \\\"\\\") return; // never touched here: no path to drain\\n305\\t    if (st.running) {\\n306\\t      st.pending = true; // burst coalesce: at most one further pass (AC-1.2)\\n307\\t      return;\\n308\\t    }\\n309\\t    st.running = true;\\n310\\t    void runLoop(st);\\n311\\t  }\\n312\\t\\n313\\t  return {\\n314\\t    mode,\\n315\\t    poke(threadId: string): void {\\n316\\t      if (mode !== \\\"background\\\") return;\\n317\\t      if (!deps.hasAnyHandler()) return;\\n318\\t      schedule(threadId);\\n319\\t    },\\n320\\t    touch(filePath: string, db: DatabaseSync): void {\\n321\\t      if (mode !== \\\"background\\\") return;\\n322\\t      const threadId = readThreadId(db);\\n323\\t      if (threadId === null) return;\\n324\\t      const st = stateFor(threadId);\\n325\\t      st.filePath = filePath;\\n326\\t      if (seen.has(threadId)) return;\\n327\\t      seen.add(threadId);\\n328\\t      // Catch-up (DD-10): leftover live work from a previous process runs on\\n329\\t      // first touch. One indexed query on the already-open handle keeps the\\n330\\t      // empty-queue case cheap.\\n331\\t      if (deps.hasAnyHandler() && countLiveItems(db) > 0) schedule(threadId);\\n332\\t    },\\n333\\t    drainSettled(threadId: string): Promise<void> {\\n334\\t      const st = states.get(threadId);\\n335\\t      if (st === undefined || (!st.running && !st.pending)) return Promise.resolve();\\n336\\t      return new Promise((resolve) => st.waiters.push(resolve));\\n337\\t    },\\n338\\t    testPassCount(threadId: string): number {\\n339\\t      return states.get(threadId)?.passes ?? 0;\\n340\\t    },\\n341\\t  };\\n342\\t}\\n343\\t\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"11cfbce6-8c6f-47e4-8ae9-6d1d829e88b4\",\"uuid\":\"087c7f6e-a7af-4bf5-acf5-b5a5fe398dd0\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-06-11T05:59:38.608Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/src/scheduler.ts\",\"content\":\"// SDK-internal scheduler and drain (DD-4): the one component holding\\n// cross-operation in-memory state. The drain loop lives here per the tech\\n// design's placement — claim under BEGIN IMMEDIATE, dispatch through the\\n// handler map with no open transaction (the provider call lives there),\\n// complete in a second short transaction. Background mode adds per-thread\\n// single-flight with pending-flag coalescing, post-commit pokes, first-touch\\n// catch-up (DD-10), and drainSettled (issue 3). The in-memory state is\\n// advisory only: cross-process safety comes from the durable lease alone, so\\n// a fresh handle sees identical drain behavior — the queue is the rows.\\nimport { existsSync } from \\\"node:fs\\\";\\nimport { setImmediate as scheduleMacrotask } from \\\"node:timers\\\";\\nimport { DatabaseSync } from \\\"node:sqlite\\\";\\nimport { openThreadDatabase } from \\\"./domains/threads/index.js\\\";\\nimport type {\\n  HandlerOutcome,\\n  ResolvedSdkConfig,\\n  WorkHandler,\\n} from \\\"./shared/derivation.js\\\";\\nimport { storageFailure, type ErrorResult, type OpResult } from \\\"./shared/errors.js\\\";\\nimport {\\n  claimNext,\\n  complete,\\n  countLiveItems,\\n  failAttempt,\\n  failTerminal,\\n  type ClaimedWorkItem,\\n  type WorkKind,\\n  type WorkSourceRef,\\n} from \\\"./tech-utils/work-queue/index.js\\\";\\n\\nexport type SchedulerMode = \\\"background\\\" | \\\"manual\\\";\\n\\n// Report shape per tech design §Interfaces. `superseded` never appears here:\\n// the cascade deletes superseded items and reports them on the mutation\\n// result; a drain never sees them.\\nexport interface DrainReport {\\n  ran: Array<{\\n    workItemId: string;\\n    kind: WorkKind;\\n    sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\\n    disposition: \\\"done\\\" | \\\"failed_terminal\\\" | \\\"stale_discarded\\\";\\n    attempts: number;\\n    reason?: string;\\n  }>;\\n  stoppedBecause: \\\"empty\\\" | \\\"in_flight\\\" | \\\"waiting\\\" | \\\"max_items\\\";\\n  waitingUntil?: string; // head's eligible_at when stoppedBecause = \\\"waiting\\\"\\n  remaining: number; // live items left behind the stop point\\n}\\n\\nexport interface DrainDeps {\\n  lookupHandler: (kind: string) => OpResult<WorkHandler>;\\n  // Whether any handler is registered at all. Background scheduling is gated\\n  // on this, fail-closed: with an empty map a background drain could only\\n  // turn queued rows into failed_terminal — destruction, not processing — so\\n  // pokes and catch-up stay inert until a handler table is populated\\n  // (Stories 2–3 in production; explicit registration in tests).\\n  hasAnyHandler: () => boolean;\\n  config: ResolvedSdkConfig;\\n}\\n\\nfunction ranEntry(\\n  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\\n  disposition: \\\"done\\\" | \\\"failed_terminal\\\" | \\\"stale_discarded\\\",\\n  attempts: number,\\n  reason?: string,\\n): DrainReport[\\\"ran\\\"][number] {\\n  const entry: DrainReport[\\\"ran\\\"][number] = {\\n    workItemId: item.workItemId,\\n    kind: item.kind as WorkKind,\\n    sourceRef: item.sourceRef,\\n    disposition,\\n    attempts,\\n  };\\n  if (reason !== undefined) entry.reason = reason;\\n  return entry;\\n}\\n\\n// The drain loop against an open handle. Claim → dispatch → complete, one\\n// item at a time, until the head stops it (empty / in_flight / waiting) or\\n// maxItems is reached. The handler runs with NO open transaction; the only\\n// exits from a handler failure are failAttempt and the terminal paths — the\\n// drain never catches an error and records success.\\nexport async function drainOpenDb(\\n  db: DatabaseSync,\\n  deps: DrainDeps,\\n  opts?: { maxItems?: number },\\n): Promise<DrainReport> {\\n  const { clock, lease, retry, provider } = deps.config;\\n  const ran: DrainReport[\\\"ran\\\"] = [];\\n  let stoppedBecause: DrainReport[\\\"stoppedBecause\\\"];\\n  let waitingUntil: string | undefined;\\n\\n  for (;;) {\\n    if (opts?.maxItems !== undefined && ran.length >= opts.maxItems) {\\n      stoppedBecause = \\\"max_items\\\";\\n      break;\\n    }\\n    const claim = claimNext(db, clock().toISOString(), lease.durationMs);\\n    if (claim.outcome === \\\"empty\\\") {\\n      stoppedBecause = \\\"empty\\\";\\n      break;\\n    }\\n    if (claim.outcome === \\\"in_flight\\\") {\\n      stoppedBecause = \\\"in_flight\\\";\\n      break;\\n    }\\n    if (claim.outcome === \\\"waiting\\\") {\\n      stoppedBecause = \\\"waiting\\\";\\n      waitingUntil = claim.waitingUntil;\\n      break;\\n    }\\n    const item = claim.item;\\n\\n    const lookedUp = deps.lookupHandler(item.kind);\\n    if (!lookedUp.ok) {\\n      // Unregistered kind: the normal terminal transaction, not a try/catch\\n      // skip — failed_terminal with the stable code, form (if resolvable\\n      // from the payload) lands failed, and the drain continues (AC-1.8).\\n      failTerminal(db, item, {\\n        reason: lookedUp.error.code,\\n        formState: \\\"failed\\\",\\n        attempts: item.attempts,\\n        now: clock().toISOString(),\\n      });\\n      ran.push(ranEntry(item, \\\"failed_terminal\\\", item.attempts, lookedUp.error.code));\\n      continue;\\n    }\\n\\n    let outcome: HandlerOutcome;\\n    try {\\n      outcome = await lookedUp.value(\\n        { openDb: () => db, provider, clock, config: deps.config },\\n        {\\n          workItemId: item.workItemId,\\n          kind: item.kind,\\n          sourceRef: item.sourceRef as unknown as Record<string, string>,\\n        },\\n      );\\n    } catch (cause) {\\n      // A throwing handler is a bug by the error contract, but the queue\\n      // must not wedge on it: route it through the normal retry path so it\\n      // counts attempts and exhausts visibly.\\n      const detail = cause instanceof Error ? cause.message : String(cause);\\n      outcome = { ok: false, retryable: true, reason: `handler threw: ${detail}` };\\n    }\\n\\n    if (outcome.ok) {\\n      const disposition = complete(\\n        db,\\n        item,\\n        outcome.forms ?? [],\\n        clock().toISOString(),\\n        outcome.onApplied,\\n      );\\n      ran.push(ranEntry(item, disposition, item.attempts));\\n      continue;\\n    }\\n    if (\\\"blocked\\\" in outcome) {\\n      failTerminal(db, item, {\\n        reason: outcome.reason,\\n        formState: \\\"blocked\\\",\\n        attempts: item.attempts + 1,\\n        now: clock().toISOString(),\\n      });\\n      ran.push(ranEntry(item, \\\"failed_terminal\\\", item.attempts + 1, outcome.reason));\\n      continue;\\n    }\\n    const failed = failAttempt(db, item, {\\n      reason: outcome.reason,\\n      retryable: outcome.retryable,\\n      now: clock().toISOString(),\\n      retry,\\n    });\\n    if (failed.terminal) {\\n      ran.push(ranEntry(item, \\\"failed_terminal\\\", failed.attempts, outcome.reason));\\n    }\\n    // Under budget the item went back to queued (possibly backing off); the\\n    // next claimNext re-reads the head — a backing-off head ends the drain\\n    // with \\\"waiting\\\" and gates everything behind it.\\n  }\\n\\n  const report: DrainReport = { ran, stoppedBecause, remaining: countLiveItems(db) };\\n  if (waitingUntil !== undefined) report.waitingUntil = waitingUntil;\\n  return report;\\n}\\n\\nfunction threadNotFound(filePath: string): { ok: false; error: ErrorResult } {\\n  return {\\n    ok: false,\\n    error: {\\n      errorClass: \\\"caller_error\\\",\\n      code: \\\"thread_not_found\\\",\\n      reason: `no thread file exists at ${filePath}`,\\n    },\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n// The drain operation against a thread file: open (validates + migrates),\\n// drain, close. Both the SDK's work.drain and the background loop's passes\\n// run through here — manual and background modes share one drain.\\nexport async function runDrain(\\n  filePath: string,\\n  deps: DrainDeps,\\n  opts?: { maxItems?: number },\\n): Promise<OpResult<DrainReport>> {\\n  if (!existsSync(filePath)) return threadNotFound(filePath);\\n  const opened = openThreadDatabase(filePath);\\n  if (!opened.ok) return opened;\\n  const db = opened.value;\\n  try {\\n    return { ok: true, value: await drainOpenDb(db, deps, opts) };\\n  } catch (cause) {\\n    const detail = cause instanceof Error ? cause.message : String(cause);\\n    return storageFailure(`drain failed: ${detail}`);\\n  } finally {\\n    db.close();\\n  }\\n}\\n\\ninterface ThreadDrainState {\\n  filePath: string;\\n  running: boolean;\\n  pending: boolean;\\n  passes: number; // test-only observability (TC-1.2); must not become API\\n  waiters: Array<() => void>;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface Scheduler {\\n  readonly mode: SchedulerMode;\\n  // Post-commit nudge that work was queued for a thread (DD-5). Manual mode:\\n  // no-op by contract. Background mode: starts a drain, or coalesces into\\n  // the pending flag if one is already running for the thread (AC-1.2).\\n  poke(threadId: string): void;\\n  // Thread-file open announcement (DD-10): learns threadId → filePath and,\\n  // on the first touch of a thread this process lifetime, schedules a\\n  // catch-up drain if the queue has leftover live work (AC-1.6).\\n  touch(filePath: string, db: DatabaseSync): void;\\n  // Resolves when the scheduler has no running or pending drain for the\\n  // thread (issue 3). Manual mode resolves immediately.\\n  drainSettled(threadId: string): Promise<void>;\\n  // Test-only observability for coalescing exactness (TC-1.2): drain passes\\n  // started for a thread. Named as a test hook on purpose — not API.\\n  testPassCount(threadId: string): number;\\n}\\n\\nfunction readThreadId(db: DatabaseSync): string | null {\\n  const row = db.prepare(\\\"SELECT thread_id FROM thread_metadata WHERE id = 1\\\").get() as\\n    | { thread_id: string }\\n    | undefined;\\n  return row?.thread_id ?? null;\\n}\\n\\n// Read a thread file's id without side effects (no migration, no touch) —\\n// drainSettled must observe scheduler state, never schedule work.\\nexport function peekThreadId(filePath: string): string | null {\\n  if (!existsSync(filePath)) return null;\\n  let db: DatabaseSync | undefined;\\n  try {\\n    db = new DatabaseSync(filePath, { readOnly: true });\\n    return readThreadId(db);\\n  } catch {\\n    return null;\\n  } finally {\\n    db?.close();\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nexport function createScheduler(mode: SchedulerMode, deps: DrainDeps): Scheduler {\\n  const states = new Map<string, ThreadDrainState>();\\n  const seen = new Set<string>(); // first-touch catch-up guard, process lifetime\\n\\n  function stateFor(threadId: string): ThreadDrainState {\\n    let st = states.get(threadId);\\n    if (st === undefined) {\\n      st = { filePath: \\\"\\\", running: false, pending: false, passes: 0, waiters: [] };\\n      states.set(threadId, st);\\n    }\\n    return st;\\n  }\\n\\n  async function runLoop(st: ThreadDrainState): Promise<void> {\\n    // Defer past the committing operation's synchronous tail so the drain\\n    // never contends with the transaction whose commit scheduled it.\\n    await new Promise((resolve) => scheduleMacrotask(resolve));\\n    try {\\n      do {\\n        st.pending = false;\\n        st.passes += 1;\\n        // A failed pass (storage error) has no caller to report to in\\n        // background mode; the durable rows are untouched and the next poke\\n        // or touch retries. A pass stopping on \\\"waiting\\\" likewise leaves the\\n        // backing-off head for the next nudge — the lease and eligibility\\n        // gates are durable, not timer state.\\n        await runDrain(st.filePath, deps);\\n      } while (st.pending);\\n    } finally {\\n      st.running = false;\\n      for (const waiter of st.waiters.splice(0)) waiter();\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  function schedule(threadId: string): void {\\n    const st = stateFor(threadId);\\n    if (st.filePath === \\\"\\\") return; // never touched here: no path to drain\\n    if (st.running) {\\n      st.pending = true; // burst coalesce: at most one further pass (AC-1.2)\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    st.running = true;\\n    void runLoop(st);\\n  }\\n\\n  return {\\n    mode,\\n    poke(threadId: string): void {\\n      if (mode !== \\\"background\\\") return;\\n      if (!deps.hasAnyHandler()) return;\\n      schedule(threadId);\\n    },\\n    touch(filePath: string, db: DatabaseSync): void {\\n      if (mode !== \\\"background\\\") return;\\n      const threadId = readThreadId(db);\\n      if (threadId === null) return;\\n      const st = stateFor(threadId);\\n      st.filePath = filePath;\\n      if (seen.has(threadId)) return;\\n      seen.add(threadId);\\n      // Catch-up (DD-10): leftover live work from a previous process runs on\\n      // first touch. One indexed query on the already-open handle keeps the\\n      // empty-queue case cheap.\\n      if (deps.hasAnyHandler() && countLiveItems(db) > 0) schedule(threadId);\\n    },\\n    drainSettled(threadId: string): Promise<void> {\\n      const st = states.get(threadId);\\n      if (st === undefined || (!st.running && !st.pending)) return Promise.resolve();\\n      return new Promise((resolve) => st.waiters.push(resolve));\\n    },\\n    testPassCount(threadId: string): number {\\n      return states.get(threadId)?.passes ?? 0;\\n    },\\n  };\\n}\\n\",\"numLines\":343,\"startLine\":1,\"totalLines\":343}}}\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/artifacts/05-message-edit-cascade/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:2103:{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"content_block_delta\",\"index\":0,\"delta\":{\"type\":\"text_delta\",\"text\":\" (existing single write path in work-queue complete()) is provoked by TC-5.4's\"}},\"session_id\":\"11cfbce6-8c6f-47e4-8ae9-6d1d829e88b4\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"8040602e-de67-432d-b4b8-0deb77903cdf\"}\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/artifacts/05-message-edit-cascade/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:2166:{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-fable-5\",\"id\":\"msg_01AeWAPLYfSXNLJgGmFttiKi\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"{\\n  \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"ready-for-verification\\\",\\n  \\\"planSummary\\\": \\\"Implemented Story 5 (message edit and cascade): a new shared cascade module (src/domains/messages/internal/cascade.ts) derives the clear-set structurally from the message → turn → chunk walk over existing derived_form rows, supersede-deletes still-queued old-version items (reported on the result), and enqueues replacements at each group's max source version + 1 inside the mutation's transaction. messages.edit validates through the deleted-filtered read (turn_open / message_not_found refusals), applies content + blocks + token re-stamp via new store.ts helpers (readMutableMessage / applyMessageEdit mirroring projectEvent's counted fields), runs the cascade, and returns the MutationResult — one synchronous commit, with enqueue pokes riding onCommit so background mode rebuilds with no further call. The source-version check (existing single write path in work-queue complete()) is provoked by TC-5.4's delayed-double recipe: a real claimed pre-edit item held across the edit completes after the post-edit replacement is queued and discards as stale_discarded. CLI: new src/cli/messages-mutate.ts and `lhc messages edit --message/--message-id --content` wiring with parity proven through the spawned binary.\\\",\\n  \\\"changedFiles\\\": [\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/internal/cascade.ts\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"NEW: walk-and-clear cascade module (DD-8) — structural chain enumeration, supersede-delete of queued old-version items, replacement enqueues at next source version\\\" },\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/internal/store.ts\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"edit's validation read (readMutableMessage, deleted-filtered, joined turn status) and record apply (applyMessageEdit: block content update + token re-stamp via the one estimator)\\\" },\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/index.ts\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"messages.edit operation (one runInTransaction: validate → apply → cascade) and the MutationResult interface\\\" },\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/src/cli/messages-mutate.ts\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"NEW per §Placement: runMessagesEdit mirroring the SDK operation one-for-one\\\" },\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"`lhc messages edit` command wiring, --message/--message-id and --content flags, HELP line\\\" },\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/src/sdk.ts\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"export MutationResult type on the public surface\\\" },\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/vitest.config.ts\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"exclude the new cli-process-mutations suite outside LHC_PROCESS_SUITE=1, matching the other process suites\\\" },\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/test/mutations.test.ts\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"NEW: TC-5.1 through TC-5.5 plus the induced-failure atomicity leg and a background-mode edit-and-walk-away production-path test\\\" },\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/test/cli-process-mutations.test.ts\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"NEW: TC-5.6 SDK/CLI twin parity (result JSON, cascade rows, read-back) plus CLI refusal parity\\\" },\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/test/red-manifest.json\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"recorded the two new test files' hashes (green may add files; the record step locks them)\\\" }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"tests\\\": {\\n    \\\"added\\\": [\\n      \\\"test/mutations.test.ts — TC-5.1: synchronous content/blocks/estimate update, cleared + queued named on the result, rebuild derives from edited content\\\",\\n      \\\"test/mutations.test.ts — TC-5.1 atomicity (architecture risk): induced cascade failure (planted claimed row at the replacement id) rolls back the content change too; full snapshot byte-stable\\\",\\n      \\\"test/mutations.test.ts — TC-5.2 (architecture risk): two-chunk thread, cleared set exactly 5 forms (message + turn's 2 + chunk 1's 2); every form outside the chain deep-equal pre-edit including source version\\\",\\n      \\\"test/mutations.test.ts — TC-5.3: post-return all dependents pending, replacement ids carry the new source version; second edit supersedes the still-queued first wave, rows deleted, ids reported\\\",\\n      \\\"test/mutations.test.ts — TC-5.4 (epic's named architecture-risk test): delayed double holds a claimed pre-edit smoothing across the edit; old and replacement items coexist (version-scoped ids); drain report shows stale_discarded then done; exactly one ready row from post-edit content\\\",\\n      \\\"test/mutations.test.ts — TC-5.5: turn_open and message_not_found refusals plus deleted-target-reads-as-message_not_found; snapshot (messages + forms + queue) identical after each\\\",\\n      \\\"test/mutations.test.ts — background-mode edit-and-walk-away: cascade pokes ride the commit; rebuilds run with no further call (production path proof)\\\",\\n      \\\"test/cli-process-mutations.test.ts — TC-5.6: same edit via SDK and spawned CLI on twin fixtures: identical result JSON, identical queue rows (minus wall-clock queuedAt), identical message and form read-back\\\",\\n      \\\"test/cli-process-mutations.test.ts — TC-5.6 refusal parity: CLI exits 1 with the same stable message_not_found code; record unchanged\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"modified\\\": [],\\n    \\\"removed\\\": [],\\n    \\\"totalAfterStory\\\": 217,\\n    \\\"deltaFromPriorBaseline\\\": 9\\n  },\\n  \\\"gatesRun\\\": [\\n    { \\\"command\\\": \\\"pnpm run green-verify\\\", \\\"result\\\": \\\"pass\\\" },\\n    { \\\"command\\\": \\\"pnpm run verify-all\\\", \\\"result\\\": \\\"pass\\\" },\\n    { \\\"command\\\": \\\"pnpm vitest run test/mutations.test.ts\\\", \\\"result\\\": \\\"pass\\\" },\\n    { \\\"command\\\": \\\"LHC_PROCESS_SUITE=1 pnpm vitest run test/cli-process-mutations.test.ts\\\", \\\"result\\\": \\\"pass\\\" }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"selfReview\\\": {\\n    \\\"findingsFixed\\\": [\\n      \\\"Atomicity fault injection initially targeted a queued conflicting row, which the cascade's supersede step would have deleted before the enqueue; switched to a claimed row so the replacement insert genuinely collides mid-cascade and forces the rollback path\\\",\\n      \\\"Cascade source-version bump computed per rebuild group as max(existing versions) + 1 rather than per-form +1, so the two turn forms (one shared turn_derivation item) can never enqueue at diverged versions\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"findingsSurfaced\\\": [\\n      \\\"TC-5.4 timing: the edit must land inside the delayed double's 400ms provider window after the poll observes the handler start; edit completes in single-digit milliseconds so the margin is wide, but an extremely loaded CI machine could in principle let the v1 completion land first and fail the mid-flight coexistence assertion\\\",\\n      \\\"messages.listMessages and turns reads do not yet filter deleted_at (Story 6 scope); TC-5.5's deleted-target leg stamps deleted_at below the SDK and only asserts the edit refusal reads through the filtered validation view\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  \\\"openQuestions\\\": [],\\n  \\\"specDeviations\\\": [\\n    \\\"TC-5.6's CLI parity legs live in a new test/cli-process-mutations.test.ts instead of test/cli-process-work.test.ts named by the story's test mapping: that file is hash-locked by the Story 1 red manifest (green may add files, not edit) — same precedent Story 4 set with cli-process-report-repair.test.ts\\\",\\n    \\\"TC-5.4 holds the claimed pre-edit item via an unawaited manual-mode work.drain promise rather than background mode + drainSettled, so the drain report's stale_discarded disposition is directly assertable (the test plan's storage-contract note requires that assertion); the claimed-item-held-across-the-edit overlap is identical, and background mode is separately proven by the edit-and-walk-away test\\\",\\n    \\\"CLI accepts both --message (the story's and tech design's spelling) and --message-id (the house convention used by report/requeue); both parse into the same input\\\",\\n    \\\"For tool_call blocks the edit's content string lands verbatim as the new arguments value (estimate re-stamped from its serialization, mirroring projection); the design's edit signature is content: string for all kinds and only text-kind edits are TC-covered — noted for the Story 6/verifier record\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"recommendedNextStep\\\": \\\"Proceed to story verification; then Story 6 (delete) can reuse cascadeFromMessage's walk with the dropped-forms close path\\\"\\n}\"}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":2297,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":224158,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":2297},\"output_tokens\":1,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"},\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"11cfbce6-8c6f-47e4-8ae9-6d1d829e88b4\",\"uuid\":\"1708851b-6a0a-48ac-a549-95f12948e7ff\",\"request_id\":\"req_011CbvyoCU4oJP7W8zpctFNg\"}\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/artifacts/05-message-edit-cascade/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:2170:{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":1029170,\"duration_api_ms\":1005883,\"ttft_ms\":5787,\"ttft_stream_ms\":5786,\"time_to_request_ms\":9,\"num_turns\":77,\"result\":\"{\\n  \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"ready-for-verification\\\",\\n  \\\"planSummary\\\": \\\"Implemented Story 5 (message edit and cascade): a new shared cascade module (src/domains/messages/internal/cascade.ts) derives the clear-set structurally from the message → turn → chunk walk over existing derived_form rows, supersede-deletes still-queued old-version items (reported on the result), and enqueues replacements at each group's max source version + 1 inside the mutation's transaction. messages.edit validates through the deleted-filtered read (turn_open / message_not_found refusals), applies content + blocks + token re-stamp via new store.ts helpers (readMutableMessage / applyMessageEdit mirroring projectEvent's counted fields), runs the cascade, and returns the MutationResult — one synchronous commit, with enqueue pokes riding onCommit so background mode rebuilds with no further call. The source-version check (existing single write path in work-queue complete()) is provoked by TC-5.4's delayed-double recipe: a real claimed pre-edit item held across the edit completes after the post-edit replacement is queued and discards as stale_discarded. CLI: new src/cli/messages-mutate.ts and `lhc messages edit --message/--message-id --content` wiring with parity proven through the spawned binary.\\\",\\n  \\\"changedFiles\\\": [\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/internal/cascade.ts\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"NEW: walk-and-clear cascade module (DD-8) — structural chain enumeration, supersede-delete of queued old-version items, replacement enqueues at next source version\\\" },\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/internal/store.ts\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"edit's validation read (readMutableMessage, deleted-filtered, joined turn status) and record apply (applyMessageEdit: block content update + token re-stamp via the one estimator)\\\" },\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/index.ts\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"messages.edit operation (one runInTransaction: validate → apply → cascade) and the MutationResult interface\\\" },\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/src/cli/messages-mutate.ts\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"NEW per §Placement: runMessagesEdit mirroring the SDK operation one-for-one\\\" },\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"`lhc messages edit` command wiring, --message/--message-id and --content flags, HELP line\\\" },\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/src/sdk.ts\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"export MutationResult type on the public surface\\\" },\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/vitest.config.ts\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"exclude the new cli-process-mutations suite outside LHC_PROCESS_SUITE=1, matching the other process suites\\\" },\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/test/mutations.test.ts\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"NEW: TC-5.1 through TC-5.5 plus the induced-failure atomicity leg and a background-mode edit-and-walk-away production-path test\\\" },\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/test/cli-process-mutations.test.ts\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"NEW: TC-5.6 SDK/CLI twin parity (result JSON, cascade rows, read-back) plus CLI refusal parity\\\" },\\n    { \\\"path\\\": \\\"packages/lhc/test/red-manifest.json\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"recorded the two new test files' hashes (green may add files; the record step locks them)\\\" }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"tests\\\": {\\n    \\\"added\\\": [\\n      \\\"test/mutations.test.ts — TC-5.1: synchronous content/blocks/estimate update, cleared + queued named on the result, rebuild derives from edited content\\\",\\n      \\\"test/mutations.test.ts — TC-5.1 atomicity (architecture risk): induced cascade failure (planted claimed row at the replacement id) rolls back the content change too; full snapshot byte-stable\\\",\\n      \\\"test/mutations.test.ts — TC-5.2 (architecture risk): two-chunk thread, cleared set exactly 5 forms (message + turn's 2 + chunk 1's 2); every form outside the chain deep-equal pre-edit including source version\\\",\\n      \\\"test/mutations.test.ts — TC-5.3: post-return all dependents pending, replacement ids carry the new source version; second edit supersedes the still-queued first wave, rows deleted, ids reported\\\",\\n      \\\"test/mutations.test.ts — TC-5.4 (epic's named architecture-risk test): delayed double holds a claimed pre-edit smoothing across the edit; old and replacement items coexist (version-scoped ids); drain report shows stale_discarded then done; exactly one ready row from post-edit content\\\",\\n      \\\"test/mutations.test.ts — TC-5.5: turn_open and message_not_found refusals plus deleted-target-reads-as-message_not_found; snapshot (messages + forms + queue) identical after each\\\",\\n      \\\"test/mutations.test.ts — background-mode edit-and-walk-away: cascade pokes ride the commit; rebuilds run with no further call (production path proof)\\\",\\n      \\\"test/cli-process-mutations.test.ts — TC-5.6: same edit via SDK and spawned CLI on twin fixtures: identical result JSON, identical queue rows (minus wall-clock queuedAt), identical message and form read-back\\\",\\n      \\\"test/cli-process-mutations.test.ts — TC-5.6 refusal parity: CLI exits 1 with the same stable message_not_found code; record unchanged\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"modified\\\": [],\\n    \\\"removed\\\": [],\\n    \\\"totalAfterStory\\\": 217,\\n    \\\"deltaFromPriorBaseline\\\": 9\\n  },\\n  \\\"gatesRun\\\": [\\n    { \\\"command\\\": \\\"pnpm run green-verify\\\", \\\"result\\\": \\\"pass\\\" },\\n    { \\\"command\\\": \\\"pnpm run verify-all\\\", \\\"result\\\": \\\"pass\\\" },\\n    { \\\"command\\\": \\\"pnpm vitest run test/mutations.test.ts\\\", \\\"result\\\": \\\"pass\\\" },\\n    { \\\"command\\\": \\\"LHC_PROCESS_SUITE=1 pnpm vitest run test/cli-process-mutations.test.ts\\\", \\\"result\\\": \\\"pass\\\" }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"selfReview\\\": {\\n    \\\"findingsFixed\\\": [\\n      \\\"Atomicity fault injection initially targeted a queued conflicting row, which the cascade's supersede step would have deleted before the enqueue; switched to a claimed row so the replacement insert genuinely collides mid-cascade and forces the rollback path\\\",\\n      \\\"Cascade source-version bump computed per rebuild group as max(existing versions) + 1 rather than per-form +1, so the two turn forms (one shared turn_derivation item) can never enqueue at diverged versions\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"findingsSurfaced\\\": [\\n      \\\"TC-5.4 timing: the edit must land inside the delayed double's 400ms provider window after the poll observes the handler start; edit completes in single-digit milliseconds so the margin is wide, but an extremely loaded CI machine could in principle let the v1 completion land first and fail the mid-flight coexistence assertion\\\",\\n      \\\"messages.listMessages and turns reads do not yet filter deleted_at (Story 6 scope); TC-5.5's deleted-target leg stamps deleted_at below the SDK and only asserts the edit refusal reads through the filtered validation view\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  \\\"openQuestions\\\": [],\\n  \\\"specDeviations\\\": [\\n    \\\"TC-5.6's CLI parity legs live in a new test/cli-process-mutations.test.ts instead of test/cli-process-work.test.ts named by the story's test mapping: that file is hash-locked by the Story 1 red manifest (green may add files, not edit) — same precedent Story 4 set with cli-process-report-repair.test.ts\\\",\\n    \\\"TC-5.4 holds the claimed pre-edit item via an unawaited manual-mode work.drain promise rather than background mode + drainSettled, so the drain report's stale_discarded disposition is directly assertable (the test plan's storage-contract note requires that assertion); the claimed-item-held-across-the-edit overlap is identical, and background mode is separately proven by the edit-and-walk-away test\\\",\\n    \\\"CLI accepts both --message (the story's and tech design's spelling) and --message-id (the house convention used by report/requeue); both parse into the same input\\\",\\n    \\\"For tool_call blocks the edit's content string lands verbatim as the new arguments value (estimate re-stamped from its serialization, mirroring projection); the design's edit signature is content: string for all kinds and only text-kind edits are TC-covered — noted for the Story 6/verifier record\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"recommendedNextStep\\\": \\\"Proceed to story verification; then Story 6 (delete) can reuse cascadeFromMessage's walk with the dropped-forms close path\\\"\\n}\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"11cfbce6-8c6f-47e4-8ae9-6d1d829e88b4\",\"total_cost_usd\":18.152113000000003,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":3883,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":315689,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":8460459,\"output_tokens\":66749,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":315689,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":2,\"output_tokens\":2944,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":224158,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":2297,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":2297},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":1524,\"outputTokens\":14,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.001594,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-fable-5\":{\"inputTokens\":3883,\"outputTokens\":66749,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":8460459,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":315689,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":18.150519000000006,\"contextWindow\":1000000,\"maxOutputTokens\":64000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"2f85d013-231e-4e14-8b3d-49347217203c\"}\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/04-inspection/artifacts/02-inspect-domain-overview-and-health